WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome
to the Huberman Lab podcast,

00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:04.920
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:05.970
for everyday life.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.100
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.900
I'm Andrew Huberman,
and I'm a professor

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.000
of neurobiology
and ophthalmology

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.830
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.380
Today we are
discussing happiness.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.690
We're going to discuss
the science of happiness,

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.930
because indeed, there are
excellent laboratories that

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.210
have worked for many decades
to try and understand

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.550
what is this thing
that we call happiness

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:31.700
and what brings us happiness
in the short and long term.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.700
In fact, we could probably
point to happiness

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.510
as one of the most sought-after
states or commodities,

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:38.360
or emotions.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.120
Whatever you want to
call it, happiness

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.290
is what many people are seeking
in work, in relationships,

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:45.770
and in general.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:48.460
And yet most of us can't
really define exactly what

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:51.490
happiness is or means for us.

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:53.350
We can point to
certain experiences,

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:56.390
we can try and describe our
states of mind and body,

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:59.770
but most people recognize
the feeling when we have it,

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.310
and we certainly recognize the
feeling of not being happy.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.770
Whether or not that means
simply not being happy

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:09.560
as the absence of happiness
or all-out depression.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.790
Now one of the key problems in
trying to understand happiness

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.610
and, indeed, the science
and psychology of happiness

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:19.660
is that it does indeed
involve other similar things.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:23.200
Things like joy, and
gratitude, and meaning.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.170
And indeed, many scientists
and psychologists

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.600
have argued for
many, many decades

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.410
about what happiness really is.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.490
Now we can come up with
so-called operational

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:34.660
definitions of happiness.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:38.500
Operational definitions are
basically agreed-upon terms

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.560
or agreed-upon
definitions and conditions

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.020
that will define something
such as happiness.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.360
Much in the same way
that we can all probably

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.580
come up with an operational
definition of milk.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.940
But of course, milk can be
cow's milk, it can be oat milk,

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.980
it can be soy milk,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.870
So too, something
like happiness can

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:59.530
be micro-divided and sliced
and diced into as many things

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:00.550
as we decide.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:03.710
Today we are really going to
focus on three main things.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:07.150
First, we are going to define
happiness as a brain state

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:09.018
and as a state of mind and body.

00:02:09.018 --> 00:02:11.560
We're going to take a look at
what the science says about all

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:12.060
of that.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.010
Second, we are going to talk
about tools and practices

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.460
for placing ourselves
into states of happiness.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:20.950
And while for most of
us, we think of happiness

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:24.040
as something that only arrives
through the acquisition

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:28.130
of some goal or some thing
external to us, and of course,

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:29.380
that is true.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:32.200
There is also something
called synthetic happiness

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.150
or synthesized happiness
which turns out

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:37.672
to be at least as powerful and
perhaps even more powerful.

00:02:37.672 --> 00:02:39.130
Now I'll just say
right off the bat

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.380
that I'm not going to tell
you that all you have to do

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:43.120
is sit in a chair and
imagine being happy

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.410
in order to feel happy.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:47.140
Synthesize happiness actually
involves some very concrete

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:50.720
steps that have been defined by
excellent labs in psychology,

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.800
so we're going to talk
about synthesize happiness

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.490
as it relates to what you can
do to obtain happy states more

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:59.420
readily or more frequently.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.390
And then, third, we're
going to talk about some

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:04.060
of the misconceptions or what
I would call the contradictions

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.620
of happiness research.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.050
And what I mean by
that is most of you

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:11.050
have probably heard about
the general conditions

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:12.220
for obtaining happiness.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:13.678
And they always
seem to circle back

00:03:13.678 --> 00:03:17.560
to some of the same basic
features of get great sleep,

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:22.120
have great social connection,
pursue meaning, don't focus

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.758
to any overextend on
things like pursuing money

00:03:25.758 --> 00:03:27.550
because there are indeed
these studies that

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:29.920
show that the amount of
money that people make

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.323
does not necessarily scale
directly with happiness.

00:03:33.323 --> 00:03:35.740
We'll talk about those studies
in some detail a little bit

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:36.460
later.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.080
And while all of
that literature is

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.410
very powerful and
informative, there

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:43.420
is what I see as
a contradiction.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:47.283
Which is for instance, that for
many of us, including myself,

00:03:47.283 --> 00:03:48.700
especially in the
years when I was

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.890
in graduate school
and a postdoc,

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:54.760
there were times in which
pursuing and being involved

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.160
in work and pursuing
degrees and finding

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.820
meaning in my vocation
actually separated me

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:02.290
from the opportunity
to have quite

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:05.140
as many social connections,
or quite as much sleep,

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:08.470
or quite as much exercise, or
even quite as much sunshine,

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:09.500
for that matter.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:12.010
So all of the things that we're
told that we need in order

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.390
to access happiness
on a regular basis

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:16.990
oftentimes contradict
with the pressures

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:19.060
and the requirements
of not just daily life

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.250
but in building a
life that allows

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.890
us to have the kind of
resources that we need in order

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:28.390
to have things like quality
social connection, and the time

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:31.360
and opportunity to get regular
exercise and great nutrition,

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:32.590
et cetera, et cetera.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.690
So, again, while this
isn't necessarily

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:36.850
a complaint with any
of the research out

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:39.910
of the fields of
psychology on happiness,

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:42.460
it is important
that we acknowledge

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:45.202
these contradictions that
exist in the discussion

00:04:45.202 --> 00:04:45.910
around happiness.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.040
In particular, the
popular discussions

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:49.790
around the science of happiness.

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:52.420
So today, what we are
going to arrive at,

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.040
what you will
finish this episode,

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:57.520
with is a set of tools and a
framework for understanding

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.070
the pursuit of happiness
in the short and long-term

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:02.350
as it relates to the
research from psychology

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.420
but also the neuroscience.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:07.420
And my goal today is really
to try and place that all

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.670
into a structured
framework so that you

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:13.780
can know where you are in
your journey or the landscape

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:16.240
around happiness in your
pursuit of happiness.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.550
And what I won't
tell you is that you

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.470
need to abandon
all goals in terms

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:23.890
of pursuing money,
career, et cetera,

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:25.540
and simply focus
on relationships.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:28.420
But we will talk
about what constitutes

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:31.780
an excellent social bond or
even in excellent conversation.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:34.780
There's excellent research
that points to the fact

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:37.960
that even rather shallow
connections, that

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:40.390
is, connections between people
that you happen to just see

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:42.760
in the hallway on
a regular basis,

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:46.300
not even requiring
close bonds of any kind,

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:49.180
can be built into close
bonds that can deliver

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:52.720
a tremendous amount of feeling
and genuine social connection

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:55.460
provided certain
conditions are met.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:58.660
So today, again, it's
really about understanding

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:01.330
the science of happiness,
understanding the mechanisms

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:02.890
underlying what
we call happiness.

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:05.440
And providing you a
framework by which

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:08.530
you can pursue and
achieve happiness not just

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:11.410
as a long-term goal and not
just as a day-to-day goal

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:14.290
of little micro exercises
of gratitude, et cetera,

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:16.930
but rather as a way to
think about happiness

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:19.930
as a state that you have
control over, at least

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:21.700
in terms of your
ability to access

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:23.680
what I would call
the algorithms that

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:27.040
enable us or open the
opportunity to experience

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:27.610
happiness.

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:29.650
Now before we begin
today's episode,

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:33.280
I'd like to talk about a very
specific tool that applies yes

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.320
to our pursuit of
happiness but actually

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.750
to our pursuit of everything,
including quality sleep

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:40.150
and ongoing
motivation, et cetera.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:42.550
I've talked many, many
times before on this podcast

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:44.830
and on other podcasts,
and on social media

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:47.800
about the critical
value of getting

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:51.130
regular bright light, ideally
sunlight, in your eyes

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:52.540
within the first hour of waking.

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:55.630
Or if the sun isn't out when
you wake up in the morning,

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:57.490
to turn on a lot of
bright artificial lights

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:58.907
and then get
sunlight in your eyes

00:06:58.907 --> 00:07:00.680
for anywhere from
5 to 20 minutes,

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.940
depending on how cloudy it is
in the early part of the day.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:06.640
Absolutely outsized
effects mood and focus

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:09.430
during the day and
quality of sleep at night.

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:12.070
Now there's another
sort of central tenet

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:14.682
of getting great sleep
and improving mood

00:07:14.682 --> 00:07:15.890
and focus throughout the day.

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:18.070
And that's to avoid
bright artificial light

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.750
exposure to your eyes between
the hours of about 10 PM

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:21.880
to 4:00 AM.

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:24.730
Now leaving shift workers aside,
and we have an entire episode

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:27.160
devoted to shift work, most
people are asleep at night

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:28.450
and awake during the day.

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:31.570
And you would be wise to
avoid exposure of your eyes

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:33.790
to bright artificial light
between the hours of 10:00

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:35.313
PM and 4:00 AM.

00:07:35.313 --> 00:07:37.480
If you're going to use
screens or artificial lights,

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:39.890
dim them down as far as you can.

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:42.190
Now there are several studies
that point to the fact

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:44.923
that one of the major
issues with getting

00:07:44.923 --> 00:07:47.090
bright light in your eyes
between the hours of 10:00

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:50.020
PM and 4:00 AM is that
it has a negative impact

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:52.900
on the so-called dopaminergic or
dopamine circuits of the brain

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:55.600
and body, which can
enhance depression.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:59.050
That is lead to ongoing
lower mood and affect.

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:00.550
So that's a reason
to dim the lights

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:03.070
or avoid bright lights
between 10:00 PM and 4:00 AM.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:06.670
However, I and many others
need to use artificial light

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:09.400
and screens sometimes, even
between the hours of 10:00

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.860
PM and midnight or even
midnight to 3:00 AM,

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:15.220
depending on what's going
on in my life or your life.

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:17.125
That may include you as well.

00:08:17.125 --> 00:08:19.000
Now it turns out that
there are powerful ways

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:23.020
to offset some, not all, but
some of the negative effects

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.460
of viewing artificial lights
between the hours of 10:00

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:26.920
PM and 4:00 AM.

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:29.320
And one of the most
powerful ways to do that

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:31.810
is to simply adjust
the overall brightness

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:34.000
of your artificial
lighting throughout the day

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.320
and in the evening.

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:38.320
So one of the issues nowadays
that we're really facing

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.990
is that people are simply not
getting enough bright light

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:43.355
in their eyes from sunlight
or from other sources

00:08:43.355 --> 00:08:44.980
during the daytime,
and they're getting

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:47.880
far too much bright
light in their eyes

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.820
largely from artificial sources,
of course, in the evening

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:51.540
and at night.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.520
Not just from 10 PM
to 4:00 AM, but also

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.620
in the evening hours from 6 to
10 PM and so on and so forth.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:59.130
So a very simple yet
powerful solution

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:02.340
that supported by peer-reviewed
research in humans

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:05.550
is to try and make your
indoor working and/or home

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:08.232
environment during the
day as bright as possible.

00:09:08.232 --> 00:09:10.440
Now, if you can achieve that
through direct sunlight,

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:10.990
terrific.

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:13.800
If you can get outside a lot
during the daytime, terrific.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:15.300
But many people simply cannot.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:18.580
But most people do have some
windows in their environment.

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:20.370
I realize some don't
but, most people do.

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:22.050
And as a consequence,
most people

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:27.600
are using rather dim artificial
lighting indoors during the day

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.700
and then very bright
artificial lighting

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:33.262
indoors in the evening, and
at night, that's a problem.

00:09:33.262 --> 00:09:34.970
And if you think about
it, logically, you

00:09:34.970 --> 00:09:36.920
want to do the exact reverse.

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:39.020
So it's been shown
that if you simply

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.270
increase the amount of
bright artificial light

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:43.940
that you were exposed to
during the day, and remember

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:47.390
this is not an excuse to not get
your morning sunlight viewing,

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:50.668
but in addition to that, to make
your indoor artificial lights

00:09:50.668 --> 00:09:52.460
very bright, bright,
bright, bright, bright

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:53.540
throughout the day.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:58.250
And then much dimmer from the
hours of 6:00 PM until bedtime.

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:00.170
Or if you can't do
that, then maybe as soon

00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:03.710
as you get home from about
8:00 PM until bedtime.

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:05.630
And then dim them way,
way down between 10:00

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:07.790
PM and 4:00 AM or off entirely.

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:10.130
That's going to be
a far better pattern

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:12.470
for your sleep-wake
cycles, focus, mood, et

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:14.730
cetera than what
most people do, which

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:16.730
is to have a few windows
in their indoor working

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:20.060
environment during the day and
keep the indoor lights rather

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:22.880
dim at a time when they
need more photons, more

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:23.480
light energy.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:24.770
And then in the evening,
when they get home

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:26.605
because it's dark
outside, they tend

00:10:26.605 --> 00:10:27.980
to turn the lights
much brighter.

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:29.660
You actually want
to do the reverse.

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:31.410
Now there's an even
simpler solution,

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:34.670
which is to get some bright
sunlight in your eyes

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:36.650
right around the time of sunset.

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:38.780
It doesn't have to
be exactly at sunset,

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:41.540
it could be in the late
afternoon and evening.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.730
But it's been shown now
in studies on humans,

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:45.355
and I'll provide a
link to at least one

00:10:45.355 --> 00:10:46.790
of those studies,
that by getting

00:10:46.790 --> 00:10:50.000
some bright light in your
eyes, ideally from sunlight,

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.647
in the late afternoon
and evening.

00:10:51.647 --> 00:10:54.230
And, of course, the timing will
vary depending on time of year

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:56.330
and where you are
located on the planet.

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:59.898
But facing the
sun around sunset,

00:10:59.898 --> 00:11:01.940
you don't actually have
to see the sun cross down

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:03.530
below the horizon,
but facing the sun

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:06.950
around that time for anywhere
from 5 to 10 minutes or even

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:09.740
less, even two to
five minutes, can

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:11.510
adjust the
sensitivity of neurons

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:14.780
in your retina that communicate
light information to the brain

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:17.150
and make it such that in
the evening when you use

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:20.060
artificial lights, they
aren't going to have as much

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:22.580
of a detrimental effect
on your dopamine system

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:25.370
and for impairing your sleep.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:27.882
So the idea is as much bright
light, ideally from sunlight

00:11:27.882 --> 00:11:29.840
but also from artificial
sources, from the time

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:32.360
you wake up in the
morning until the evening.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:33.712
Maybe around 6:00 or 7:00.

00:11:33.712 --> 00:11:35.670
Maybe in the summer
months, a little bit later.

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:38.660
And then really try and
get as little bright light

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:42.950
in your eyes as you can in the
evening and nighttime hours.

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:47.750
And ideally, you would also get
some sunlight exposure right

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:49.970
around the time of sunset
or in the late afternoon.

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:51.860
Go outside, take
your sunglasses off.

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:54.590
Don't try and do this through a
windshield or through a window.

00:11:54.590 --> 00:11:55.860
It will not work.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:57.188
You have to get outside.

00:11:57.188 --> 00:11:58.730
If you're under an
overhang, at least

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:01.350
try and get some direct sunlight
in your eyes at that time.

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:03.440
And that will adjust
the sensitivity

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:06.200
of your retina such that
bright artificial lights

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:08.240
or artificial lights
of any kind that you're

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:11.210
exposed to in the evening and
in the late hours of the night

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:13.130
won't have as much of
a detrimental effect.

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:14.630
That said, if you
go to the bathroom

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:17.053
in the middle of the night,
try and keep the lights dim.

00:12:17.053 --> 00:12:19.220
Many people have asked
whether or not, for instance,

00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:23.000
a nightlight or a flashlight
is going to have as much

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:23.930
of a negative effect.

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:25.140
This is very straightforward.

00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:27.710
If you think about it, if you
shine a light at something,

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:29.450
you can see into
your environment.

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:30.500
If you've ever been
camping or you've

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:32.810
walked with a flashlight,
you can see things around you

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:34.370
that you wouldn't
otherwise, of course.

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:36.150
But if you were to shine
that light in your eyes,

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:37.192
it would be far brighter.

00:12:37.192 --> 00:12:39.860
So yes, of course, if you get
up in the middle of the night

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:42.200
and you can use your phone
flashlight to illuminate

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:43.820
the environment that you're
in so that you can safely

00:12:43.820 --> 00:12:45.820
go to where you need to
go and then back to bed,

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:48.650
that's going to be far better
than turning on the lights

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:51.170
or, of course, shining
light in your eyes, right.

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:54.140
So the idea is bright,
bright, bright in the morning

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:55.200
and throughout the day.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:58.130
And as dim and dark
as possible at night.

00:12:58.130 --> 00:13:00.680
And that afternoon light
viewing provides, sort of,

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:03.560
what I call a Netflix
inoculation that

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:05.965
will allow you to adjust
your retinal sensitivity

00:13:05.965 --> 00:13:08.090
and give you a little bit
more flexibility in terms

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:10.370
of allowing some
nighttime light exposure

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:11.960
without the detrimental effects.

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:14.820
Now I realize today's
episode is about happiness,

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:17.450
it's not about
sunlight or dopamine.

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:19.940
And yet, as we'll
talk about more

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:23.280
in just a moment, if you're
not optimizing your sleep

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:27.410
and if you are using or
being exposed to light rather

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:30.260
at the wrong times of
the day/night cycle,

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:33.200
that is going to make it
very hard for the other sorts

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:35.270
of practices that
relate to happiness

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:36.690
to have their full impact.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:39.890
So the backdrop, where I would
say the kind of landscape

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:42.080
of your chemicals
and your hormones,

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:44.970
is powerfully controlled by not
just the brightness of light

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.690
but the timing of light
and your exposure to light.

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:50.390
In particular, your exposure
to light to your eyes

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:52.790
is something that you have
a lot of control over.

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:55.610
You don't have absolute control,
but you have a lot of control

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:56.280
over.

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:58.940
And it's been proven that
even these small steps, which

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:00.003
are completely cost-free.

00:14:00.003 --> 00:14:01.670
They require just a
few minutes of time,

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:04.520
but no purchase a
product or anything else

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:08.060
