00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:53.910
Let's talk about happiness.

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:57.390
This thing that everybody seems
to want, and yet not everybody

00:17:57.390 --> 00:18:02.530
can agree on what exactly
it is or how to get it.

00:18:02.530 --> 00:18:06.570
Now I want to start by quoting
a previous guest on the Huberman

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:07.710
Lab podcast.

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:10.500
And that is a colleague of mine
at Stanford School of Medicine,

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:15.720
Dr. Karl Deisseroth, who's both
a bioengineer and a clinician.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:19.110
That is, he's a psychiatrist
who spends a lot of his time

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:22.380
both running a laboratory
and seeing patients,

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:23.790
human patients, of course.

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:26.010
And I once was at
a meeting where

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:29.880
I heard Karl say something
to the extent of we

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:31.873
don't know what
other people feel.

00:18:31.873 --> 00:18:33.540
In fact, most of the
time, we don't even

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:35.220
really know how we feel.

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:37.320
And while that
statement was meant

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:39.900
to report several different
things about the way

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:41.400
that the brain
works and emotions,

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:44.412
et cetera, one of the things
that he was emphasizing,

00:18:44.412 --> 00:18:46.620
and I know he was emphasizing
it because he confirmed

00:18:46.620 --> 00:18:52.170
this for me, was the
fact that language.

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:55.950
Things like the word
happiness, or joy, or meaning,

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:59.460
or pleasure, or delight,
are actually not

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:02.610
very precise when it comes to
describing our brain and body

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:03.370
states.

00:19:03.370 --> 00:19:07.200
So, for instance, if I tell
you I'm feeling pretty happy.

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:11.400
I know what that means for
me, at least in this moment,

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:13.290
but you don't really
know whether or not

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:16.860
it means the same thing as what
pretty happy means for you.

00:19:16.860 --> 00:19:20.190
If I say I'm extremely
happy and I have a big grin.

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:23.100
I have a grin on my face that I
can't seem to wipe off my face.

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:26.340
Well, then, you might get a
sense of how much happier I

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:28.540
am than pretty happy.

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:32.160
But it's still hard to calibrate
my level of internal state

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:34.230
or happiness, and the
same is true for you

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:35.550
and for everybody else.

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:37.590
And it's important for
us to acknowledge this

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:41.850
because at this point
in human history, 2022,

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:44.670
we don't really
have a measurement

00:19:44.670 --> 00:19:47.640
like body temperature or
heart rate or heart rate

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:51.330
variability or even a way
to measure neurochemicals

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:54.060
in the brain and body
that give us anything

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:58.350
better than a crude correlate
or an estimate, at best,

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:00.150
of what happiness is.

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:02.660
So that's really important to
understand and to keep in mind

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:03.660
throughout this episode.

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:08.910
It doesn't mean that we cannot
have a strong data-driven

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:11.398
conversation about happiness
and what brings us to a state

00:20:11.398 --> 00:20:11.940
of happiness.

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:15.360
But it's very important to
understand that language is not

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:18.780
an ideal and maybe even
a deficient tool in terms

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:22.380
of describing our emotions and
our states of mind and body.

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:24.990
Now equally important
is to understand

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:27.930
that while we do have
neurotransmitters,

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:30.660
that is, the chemicals
that are released between

00:20:30.660 --> 00:20:32.730
neurons, nerve cells
that allow neurons

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:36.360
to communicate, things
like glutamate and gaba,

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:37.380
for instance.

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:39.780
And we have what are
called modulators.

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:41.400
These are chemicals
also released

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:46.020
by neurons that impact the
electrical firing and chemical

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:47.100
release of other neurons.

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:51.510
Things like serotonin, and
dopamine, and acetylcholine,

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:52.950
and epinephrine.

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:55.800
Neuromodulators and
neurotransmitters

00:20:55.800 --> 00:21:00.120
are always present in a
cocktail in our brain and body.

00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:02.430
That is, they are present
in different ratios

00:21:02.430 --> 00:21:03.670
and at different levels.

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:06.000
So we need to completely
discard with the idea

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:10.290
that any one neurotransmitter
or any one neuromodulator

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:13.920
is solely responsible
for a state of happiness

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:17.620
or for a lack of state of
happiness, for that matter.

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:21.340
That said, it is true
that for people that

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:24.640
tend to have lower baseline
levels of, for instance,

00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:27.880
dopamine, their
levels of happiness,

00:21:27.880 --> 00:21:29.740
or we should say their
self-reported levels

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:32.890
of happiness, tend to
be lower than for those

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:36.340
that have greatly elevated
baseline levels of dopamine.

00:21:36.340 --> 00:21:38.350
Now, this can be
best appreciated

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:41.440
at the extremes
where, for instance,

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:43.690
in conditions like
Parkinson's disease

00:21:43.690 --> 00:21:46.330
or other conditions where
people's levels of dopamine

00:21:46.330 --> 00:21:49.460
in their brain is
severely depleted.

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:52.760
Mind you, we also see this
in drug-addicted individuals

00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:55.310
that are in a withdrawal state
because they're trying to quit

00:21:55.310 --> 00:21:57.680
or they don't have access to the
drug that normally stimulates

00:21:57.680 --> 00:21:58.513
release of dopamine.

00:21:58.513 --> 00:22:01.280
Think the cocaine addict
who can't get cocaine.

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:02.720
Or the methamphetamine
addict that

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:06.670
can't or is trying to avoid
taking methamphetamine.

00:22:06.670 --> 00:22:09.640
Or the Parkinson's patient
who has fewer dopamine

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:11.620
neurons because
they degenerated.

00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:14.440
Those individuals do tend
to be more depressed.

00:22:14.440 --> 00:22:15.880
They tend to have lower affect.

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:17.500
They are less happy.

00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:20.860
At least, that's how they report
themselves to be emotionally.

00:22:20.860 --> 00:22:23.590
And that's what we observe when
we look at them behaviorally

00:22:23.590 --> 00:22:26.710
in terms of the amount of
smiling, the amount of energy

00:22:26.710 --> 00:22:28.240
they seem to have.

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:30.010
At the opposite extreme.

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:33.160
And while still focusing
on the kind of pathology

00:22:33.160 --> 00:22:35.530
of neurotransmitter and
neuromodulator systems,

00:22:35.530 --> 00:22:39.910
an individual who is in
a manic phase of bipolar

00:22:39.910 --> 00:22:43.300
will tend to have very
elevated levels of dopamine.

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:45.100
And those people will
talk a mile a minute,

00:22:45.100 --> 00:22:46.360
and they won't require sleep.

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:49.458
And at least to them, every
idea is an exciting idea and one

00:22:49.458 --> 00:22:50.500
that they want to pursue.

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:54.790
We did an entire episode about
bipolar depression, A.K.A.

00:22:54.790 --> 00:22:55.498
Bipolar disorder.

00:22:55.498 --> 00:22:57.540
So if you'd like to learn
more about that, please

00:22:57.540 --> 00:22:58.690
check out that episode.

00:22:58.690 --> 00:23:00.680
That and all other
episodes of the podcast,

00:23:00.680 --> 00:23:03.790
of course, you can find it
hubermanlab.com in all formats.

00:23:03.790 --> 00:23:06.520
But the point here is
that very low levels

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:09.640
are very high levels of
dopamine are correlated

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:13.720
with certain states of,
for instance, low happiness

00:23:13.720 --> 00:23:15.722
or the absence of happiness.

00:23:15.722 --> 00:23:17.680
We could even call it
depression in some cases.

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:20.500
Or extreme happiness
or even euphoria.

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:22.150
Sometimes even
inappropriate euphoria,

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:25.300
as is the case with
bipolar depression

00:23:25.300 --> 00:23:30.570
or sometimes called bipolar
mania or bipolar disorder.

00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:34.610
Now, of course, there's a range
in between depressed and manic.

00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:38.670
And most people, fortunately
reside somewhere in that range.

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:40.350
And it is indeed a continuum.

00:23:40.350 --> 00:23:43.070
And I think it's safe to
say that levels of dopamine

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:45.260
probably do correlate
with levels of happiness.

00:23:45.260 --> 00:23:48.320
But there is no
one single chemical

00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:54.920
nor chemical signature, that is,
no specific recipe of two parts

00:23:54.920 --> 00:23:57.290
dopamine, to one part
serotonin, to one part

00:23:57.290 --> 00:24:01.580
acetylcholine that we can
say equates to happiness.

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:03.410
Indeed there's now
tremendous controversy

00:24:03.410 --> 00:24:05.540
as to whether or not,
for instance, having

00:24:05.540 --> 00:24:08.060
lower levels of
serotonin is actually

00:24:08.060 --> 00:24:10.242
the cause of depression
or merely correlates

00:24:10.242 --> 00:24:12.200
with depression or maybe
doesn't even correlate

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:13.200
with depression, at all.

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:14.780
This became especially
controversial

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:17.450
because, in the last year,
the so-called serotonin

00:24:17.450 --> 00:24:20.510
hypothesis of depression has
been called into question.

00:24:20.510 --> 00:24:24.260
And indeed, it does seem to be
the case that for individuals

00:24:24.260 --> 00:24:26.810
that are depressed,
their levels of serotonin

00:24:26.810 --> 00:24:28.650
can sometimes be normal.

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:31.440
However, and this is
an important however,

00:24:31.440 --> 00:24:33.620
that does not mean that
administering drugs

00:24:33.620 --> 00:24:36.110
that increase levels of
serotonin in depressed people

00:24:36.110 --> 00:24:39.050
does not sometimes
and indeed often help

00:24:39.050 --> 00:24:40.515
ameliorate some
of their symptoms.

00:24:40.515 --> 00:24:42.890
And I should mention that many
of the selective serotonin

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:43.890
reuptake inhibitors.

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:46.940
So-called SSRIs such
as Prozac and Zoloft,

00:24:46.940 --> 00:24:49.910
et cetera, are still
considered excellent treatments

00:24:49.910 --> 00:24:52.950
for conditions like OCD
and so on and so forth.

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:55.410
But what I'm trying to do is
make two important points.

00:24:55.410 --> 00:25:00.650
First of all, that language
is not a great indicator

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:01.890
of internal state.

00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:04.465
Especially when trying to
understand other people's

00:25:04.465 --> 00:25:05.090
internal state.

00:25:05.090 --> 00:25:08.210
And that is especially true
for things like happiness.

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:10.670
And that there is
no one chemical

00:25:10.670 --> 00:25:13.610
signature of happiness.

00:25:13.610 --> 00:25:15.910
There's no one neural
modulator or combinations

00:25:15.910 --> 00:25:17.560
of neuromodulators
that we can say

00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:19.690
is the cocktail for happiness.

00:25:19.690 --> 00:25:24.140
But, and it's a very
important, but when

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:26.570
levels of dopamine
and serotonin tend

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:29.420
to be chronically
low for an individual

00:25:29.420 --> 00:25:31.790
below their typical
baseline, they

00:25:31.790 --> 00:25:34.520
will, yes, tend to
be lower in affect

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:37.800
and have lower mood and less
episodes of happiness per day,

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:40.380
per week, per month,
per year, et cetera.

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:43.470
Conversely, when an
individual has elevations

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:44.910
in dopamine and
serotonin levels,

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:47.550
in particular dopamine levels
and the other so-called

00:25:47.550 --> 00:25:50.770
catecholamines, which include
epinephrine and norepinephrine.

00:25:50.770 --> 00:25:53.940
So the catecholamines are
dopamine, epinephrine,

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:54.870
and norepinephrine.

00:25:54.870 --> 00:25:56.580
They're all very
similar biochemically.

00:25:56.580 --> 00:26:00.600
They all lead to states of
elevated motivation, energy,

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:01.920
and so on.

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:04.830
When those chemicals are
elevated above baseline,

00:26:04.830 --> 00:26:08.400
people do tend to have elevated
sense of mood and well-being,

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:10.860
and in particular
sense of possibility

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:12.780
about what they
can do in the world

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:14.950
and what the world
can offer them.

00:26:14.950 --> 00:26:17.790
So we need to acknowledge
those two features of language

00:26:17.790 --> 00:26:21.120
and neurochemistry as we
wade into the discussion

00:26:21.120 --> 00:26:23.580
about the psychology
of happiness.

00:26:23.580 --> 00:26:26.910
And, in particular, about
the controlled experiments

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:29.550
that have been done in
excellent laboratories focused

00:26:29.550 --> 00:26:32.460
on the psychology of happiness
and what brings happiness

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:33.510
and what does not.

00:26:33.510 --> 00:26:36.010
There have been some excellent
studies on happiness.

00:26:36.010 --> 00:26:38.850
And these come in
two forms generally.

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:42.150
One form of these
studies is individuals

00:26:42.150 --> 00:26:43.320
come into a laboratory.

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:45.630
They participate in an
experiment over the course

00:26:45.630 --> 00:26:47.130
of a day or months.

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:49.540
And then, data are
collected analyzed,

00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:53.430
and the papers are submitted,
and published, and discussed.

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:56.040
The other form is so-called
longitudinal study.

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:58.050
Where individuals come
into the laboratory,

00:26:58.050 --> 00:27:00.930
and they are studied over
a very long period of time.

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:06.980
Ranging from months to years
and sometimes even decades.

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:12.260
And then, the variables of
age, life circumstances,

00:27:12.260 --> 00:27:15.590
and other factors can be
incorporated into the data.

00:27:15.590 --> 00:27:19.070
And typically, there
are multiple papers,

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:22.340
there's data published
throughout the longitudinal

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:24.050
study, or sometimes
it's just one paper

00:27:24.050 --> 00:27:26.510
at the end of the
longitudinal study.

00:27:26.510 --> 00:27:29.330
Let's talk about one of
the more famous and perhaps

00:27:29.330 --> 00:27:33.200
the longest-running
longitudinal study on happiness.

00:27:33.200 --> 00:27:36.410
This is a study that was
initiated or conceived

00:27:36.410 --> 00:27:38.870
in 1938 at Harvard University.

00:27:38.870 --> 00:27:40.910
The so-called Harvard
Happiness Project.

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:42.660
Some of you probably
heard about this.

00:27:42.660 --> 00:27:45.860
It involved Harvard
College sophomores

00:27:45.860 --> 00:27:49.400
and other individuals who were
incorporated into this study

00:27:49.400 --> 00:27:50.720
as well.

00:27:50.720 --> 00:27:54.230
It's a study that initially
had more than a couple

00:27:54.230 --> 00:27:55.820
of hundred subjects.

00:27:55.820 --> 00:27:58.880
But because some have
either dropped out and not

00:27:58.880 --> 00:28:02.660
been able to be contacted and
monitored over time or died,

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:05.390
or for whatever reason,
are no longer participating

00:28:05.390 --> 00:28:08.220
in the study, they're very
few of these individuals left.

00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:10.557
And yet there's tremendous
power to a study like this.

00:28:10.557 --> 00:28:12.890
It's such an impressive study,
and we're all so grateful

00:28:12.890 --> 00:28:14.960
that laboratories
at Harvard decided

00:28:14.960 --> 00:28:18.560
to initiate and continue
this study because it

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:21.290
is one of the few studies,
perhaps the study that

00:28:21.290 --> 00:28:25.010
has allowed us to understand
happiness in our species

00:28:25.010 --> 00:28:27.410
over a very long period of time.

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:28.910
Like any study,
it's not perfect.

00:28:28.910 --> 00:28:32.600
It didn't include a
lot of matching by sex

00:28:32.600 --> 00:28:34.370
or matching by
vocation, or matching

00:28:34.370 --> 00:28:35.780
by income and background.

00:28:35.780 --> 00:28:38.420
And back then, there was
also a lot less discussion

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:40.560
about trauma and
histories around trauma,

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:42.890
as well as positive
episodes in people's lives.

00:28:42.890 --> 00:28:46.580
Nonetheless, there's a lot of
power in a study like this.

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:48.863
And there are some
very basic takeaways,

00:28:48.863 --> 00:28:51.530
some of which you may have heard
before but some of which may be

00:28:51.530 --> 00:28:54.780
surprising those
of you who haven't.

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:57.680
So one of the key
things about the study

00:28:57.680 --> 00:29:00.830
is people in the
study, at least those

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:03.950
who still have intact memory,
which many of them do,

00:29:03.950 --> 00:29:06.680
are able to think
back on not just

00:29:06.680 --> 00:29:10.370
their previous year or week but
10 years ago, 20 years ago, 50

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:13.700
years ago and compare
what makes them happy

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:15.860
at one age versus another age.

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:18.480
A number of things have
emerged from that conversation.

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:20.798
So I just want to discuss
some of the highlight points

00:29:20.798 --> 00:29:22.340
then we'll get into
a little bit more

00:29:22.340 --> 00:29:23.910
of the nitty-gritty of the data.

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:27.050
First of all, it's been
discussed many, many times

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:31.670
that the total amount of
income that an individual makes

00:29:31.670 --> 00:29:34.550
or has, and again, this
could be income from work,

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:37.280
or it could be money
that they inherited,

00:29:37.280 --> 00:29:42.330
does not seem to directly relate
to their level of happiness.

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:45.980
Now a lot of people take
that point and think,

00:29:45.980 --> 00:29:47.720
oh, money doesn't matter.

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:51.500
Other people hear that point
and think to themselves,

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:54.528
yeah, right, easy to say
if you have a lot of money.

00:29:54.528 --> 00:29:56.570
We'll talk about the
interpretation of those data

00:29:56.570 --> 00:29:58.080
in just a few minutes.

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:00.830
But I do want to
earmark that finding.

00:30:00.830 --> 00:30:07.760
Because I agree that while
money or total resources itself

00:30:07.760 --> 00:30:11.690
does not predict happiness
in any kind of direct way,

00:30:11.690 --> 00:30:13.400
that is not the
same thing as saying

00:30:13.400 --> 00:30:16.550
having very few resources will
make you happier, of course.

00:30:16.550 --> 00:30:18.530
I don't think anyone
would imagine that.

00:30:18.530 --> 00:30:22.340
But it also tends to overlook
an important point, which

00:30:22.340 --> 00:30:24.710
is something that I certainly
have learned to appreciate

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:27.650
in my life and something
that I especially appreciated

00:30:27.650 --> 00:30:30.482
when I was a student
and post-doc, which

00:30:30.482 --> 00:30:31.190
is the following.

00:30:31.190 --> 00:30:33.530
People will say money
can't buy happiness.

00:30:33.530 --> 00:30:36.870
And we'll talk about the buy
aspect of that in a moment.

00:30:36.870 --> 00:30:38.150
And indeed, that's true.

00:30:38.150 --> 00:30:40.682
If you look at this
longitudinal study or you

00:30:40.682 --> 00:30:42.140
look at other
studies that are done

00:30:42.140 --> 00:30:44.270
on a more short-term basis.

00:30:44.270 --> 00:30:46.820
Once people get past a
certain level of income

00:30:46.820 --> 00:30:51.000
relative to their cost of
living, the amount of happiness

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:52.620
does not scale with that income.

00:30:52.620 --> 00:30:55.800
That is, for every
additional $1,000 or $10,000

00:30:55.800 --> 00:30:57.330
that they earn,
they don't report

00:30:57.330 --> 00:31:00.270
being that much happier
on a daily basis.

00:31:00.270 --> 00:31:06.660
Now that said, I venture the
argument that while money truly

00:31:06.660 --> 00:31:10.890
cannot buy happiness, it
absolutely can buffer stress.

00:31:10.890 --> 00:31:12.750
And in particular,
it can buffer stress

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:16.110
in the form of the
ability to purchase or pay

00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:20.340
for goods and services,
and in particular services.

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:24.210
You're not going to tell
me that having children

00:31:24.210 --> 00:31:26.340
doesn't involve some
increase in the demands

00:31:26.340 --> 00:31:28.350
on your life, less
sleep, and more demands.

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:30.360
And it certainly is
the case that if you

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:32.740
can hire help to clean.

00:31:32.740 --> 00:31:35.550
You can hire nannies
if that's your thing.

00:31:35.550 --> 00:31:38.280
You can hire help to assist
with babysitting or even night

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:41.070
nurses if you're having trouble
sleeping that will literally

00:31:41.070 --> 00:31:43.350
allow you to sleep while
they take care of your child

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:44.770
in the middle of the night.

00:31:44.770 --> 00:31:46.290
Often give excellent care.

00:31:46.290 --> 00:31:47.550
One hopes excellent care.

00:31:47.550 --> 00:31:49.500
That that won't offset
some of the stress

00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:50.970
associated with lack of sleep.

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:53.290
So there are a million
different examples

00:31:53.290 --> 00:31:56.340
one could give of this, but
I certainly experienced this

00:31:56.340 --> 00:31:57.340
during graduate school.

00:31:57.340 --> 00:31:59.820
In fact, I experience both
sides of the equation here.

00:31:59.820 --> 00:32:02.470
I made very little money
as a graduate student.

00:32:02.470 --> 00:32:05.340
I had essentially no savings
when I started graduate school,

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:06.570
and I made very little money.

00:32:06.570 --> 00:32:08.237
The amount doesn't
matter at this point,

00:32:08.237 --> 00:32:10.950
but I could just barely
afford rent and my food.

00:32:10.950 --> 00:32:14.950
I actually opted to live in the
laboratory a lot of the time.

00:32:14.950 --> 00:32:17.255
And by doing that,
I had more money

00:32:17.255 --> 00:32:19.380
to spend on other things
that were important to me.

00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:21.640
Now I did not have a
family at the time,

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:23.220
and so I was able to do that.

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:24.750
Something that not
everyone can do.

00:32:24.750 --> 00:32:28.440
But I made very little
money, but at the same time,

00:32:28.440 --> 00:32:30.010
I was in laboratory
all the time,

00:32:30.010 --> 00:32:31.630
and that's where I wanted to be.

00:32:31.630 --> 00:32:33.450
And so my level of
stress was actually

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:35.010
pretty low because
I was investing

00:32:35.010 --> 00:32:37.110
all my time and energy
into the very thing

00:32:37.110 --> 00:32:41.420
that I knew would eventually
help bring me more resources.

00:32:41.420 --> 00:32:44.360
When I moved from being a
graduate student to a postdoc,

00:32:44.360 --> 00:32:45.350
for instance.

00:32:45.350 --> 00:32:48.720
A postdoc is generally a
three to five-year period.

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:50.570
It's sort of like
residency in medicine,

00:32:50.570 --> 00:32:51.890
where you're no
longer taking courses,

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:53.240
but you continue to do research.

00:32:53.240 --> 00:32:55.700
In fact, entire new
lines of research

00:32:55.700 --> 00:32:58.100
and prior to getting
a professorship.

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:03.770
My income went up slightly,
went up by about 30% to 40%.

00:33:03.770 --> 00:33:07.880
But because of where I moved
and because of the times,

00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:10.790
my cost of living went way,
way up and I was extremely

00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:11.790
stressed.

00:33:11.790 --> 00:33:14.870
So it wasn't my
absolute income, it

00:33:14.870 --> 00:33:18.290
was my absolute income
relative to my cost of living.

00:33:18.290 --> 00:33:20.330
The other thing that
one needs to consider

00:33:20.330 --> 00:33:24.410
when considering income versus
cost of living is there's

00:33:24.410 --> 00:33:25.702
also this notion of peer group.

00:33:25.702 --> 00:33:27.660
And we're going to talk
more about social bonds

00:33:27.660 --> 00:33:28.675
and connections later.

00:33:28.675 --> 00:33:30.050
But one thing that
I noticed when

00:33:30.050 --> 00:33:32.180
I moved from being a
graduate student to a postdoc

00:33:32.180 --> 00:33:36.950
was I was a graduate student in
a small town where I had access

00:33:36.950 --> 00:33:40.280
if I chose to participate
in most, if not all,

00:33:40.280 --> 00:33:43.280
of the social gatherings because
they were all very low cost.

00:33:43.280 --> 00:33:45.470
People tended to aggregate
at the farmer's market

00:33:45.470 --> 00:33:45.975
on Saturday.

00:33:45.975 --> 00:33:48.350
Most people wouldn't even
purchase anything, at least not

00:33:48.350 --> 00:33:50.060
the graduate students
wouldn't purchase anything.

00:33:50.060 --> 00:33:51.435
It was just a
place to aggregate.

00:33:51.435 --> 00:33:53.360
People sometimes play
pickup games of soccer

00:33:53.360 --> 00:33:55.580
or just hang out
have a cup of coffee.

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:57.560
There was a volleyball
game on Fridays.

00:33:57.560 --> 00:34:00.030
Sometimes people would go
out to eat that evening,

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:02.180
which of course, costs
money, et cetera,

00:34:02.180 --> 00:34:03.950
but it was relatively
low cost of living.

00:34:03.950 --> 00:34:08.120
And social connections and
peer group interactions

00:34:08.120 --> 00:34:11.270
were all generated around
the same fairly low-cost

00:34:11.270 --> 00:34:12.260
activities.

00:34:12.260 --> 00:34:16.550
When I transitioned to being
a postdoc, I made more money,

00:34:16.550 --> 00:34:18.710
but cost of living went up.

00:34:18.710 --> 00:34:21.380
But in addition to
that, my peer group

00:34:21.380 --> 00:34:24.980
tended to want to engage in
the same kinds of activities

00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:28.250
that people in that larger
city were engaged in.

00:34:28.250 --> 00:34:31.489
So peer group has a tremendously
powerful influence on

00:34:31.489 --> 00:34:34.100
whether or not we gauge
the amount of money

00:34:34.100 --> 00:34:37.637
that we have as bringing
us happiness or not.

00:34:37.637 --> 00:34:39.679
And that really speaks to
the critical importance

00:34:39.679 --> 00:34:42.139
of social interactions
and certain kinds

00:34:42.139 --> 00:34:44.188
of social interactions,
in particular.

00:34:44.188 --> 00:34:46.730
Now, if any of that was unclear,
what I'm basically saying is

00:34:46.730 --> 00:34:49.070
it's not just about being
able to pay your rent.

00:34:49.070 --> 00:34:51.440
It's also about
being able to access

00:34:51.440 --> 00:34:56.239
the kinds of social interactions
that you deem are quote-unquote

00:34:56.239 --> 00:34:58.760
correct for you at
that stage of life

00:34:58.760 --> 00:35:00.860
and in the place where
you happen to be living.

00:35:00.860 --> 00:35:04.880
Because if you can meet all
the demands of costs of rent

00:35:04.880 --> 00:35:07.610
and paying your power
bill and food, et cetera,

00:35:07.610 --> 00:35:10.580
but you are socially
isolated because your peer

00:35:10.580 --> 00:35:13.880
group, or those around you
that you want to engage with,

00:35:13.880 --> 00:35:17.600
are engaging in activities that
you either don't have time for,

00:35:17.600 --> 00:35:19.940
literally, because
you're doing other things

00:35:19.940 --> 00:35:23.150
or that you don't have the
financial resources for,

00:35:23.150 --> 00:35:27.530
then that can actually
severely impact this rating

00:35:27.530 --> 00:35:29.360
of what we call happiness.

00:35:29.360 --> 00:35:31.070
Why am I parsing
this so finally?

00:35:31.070 --> 00:35:33.740
Well, I'm parsing and finally
because I think that most of us

00:35:33.740 --> 00:35:36.950
have heard the outcome of
this study from Harvard

00:35:36.950 --> 00:35:41.072
or the more short-term
studies, also many of which

00:35:41.072 --> 00:35:41.780
are from Harvard.

00:35:41.780 --> 00:35:43.550
We'll talk about the
just phenomenal work

00:35:43.550 --> 00:35:46.092
from Dan Gilbert's laboratory
and other laboratories who have

00:35:46.092 --> 00:35:47.480
focused on issues like these.

00:35:47.480 --> 00:35:49.100
And I certainly don't
want to take anything away

00:35:49.100 --> 00:35:49.892
from those results.

00:35:49.892 --> 00:35:51.950
They're very powerful
and important results

00:35:51.950 --> 00:35:53.870
that really point over
and over to the fact

00:35:53.870 --> 00:35:57.990
that people's happiness does not
necessarily scale with income.

00:35:57.990 --> 00:36:01.610
In fact, it tends not
to past a certain level,

00:36:01.610 --> 00:36:04.173
and yet I think
we'd be remiss, I

00:36:04.173 --> 00:36:05.840
think actually it
would be inappropriate

00:36:05.840 --> 00:36:08.870
for me to say that the amount
of income that one makes

00:36:08.870 --> 00:36:09.890
is not important.

00:36:09.890 --> 00:36:13.430
Because if the amount of
money that you happen to have

00:36:13.430 --> 00:36:16.880
or are making does
not allow you to meet

00:36:16.880 --> 00:36:21.560
your basic needs of shelter,
health care, et cetera.

00:36:21.560 --> 00:36:23.810
And/or doesn't
allow you to access

00:36:23.810 --> 00:36:28.310
the kind of social interactions
that can renew and reset

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:30.620
or, I would say,
directly enhance

00:36:30.620 --> 00:36:33.020
the kind of neurotransmitter
systems and hormones

00:36:33.020 --> 00:36:35.780
that lead us to feel that
we are happy in our life

00:36:35.780 --> 00:36:38.870
and we're having quality
social connections, well then

00:36:38.870 --> 00:36:40.057
that's very stressful.

00:36:40.057 --> 00:36:42.390
And this brings me back to
the statement I made earlier,

00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:45.390
which is, indeed, money
cannot buy happiness,

00:36:45.390 --> 00:36:47.520
but it certainly
can buffer stress.

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:49.400
And one of the ways
that it buffers stress

00:36:49.400 --> 00:36:52.160
is by allowing options
of different kinds

00:36:52.160 --> 00:36:53.690
of social interactions.

00:36:53.690 --> 00:36:56.630
Options of different
types of recreation

00:36:56.630 --> 00:37:00.200
that one can engage in to access
new forms of social interaction

00:37:00.200 --> 00:37:01.590
and so on and so on.

00:37:01.590 --> 00:37:04.435
So we need to be a little bit
careful or at least nuanced

00:37:04.435 --> 00:37:06.560
about this statement that
money can't buy happiness

00:37:06.560 --> 00:37:08.477
and that the data support
the fact that wealth

00:37:08.477 --> 00:37:10.190
doesn't determine happiness.

00:37:10.190 --> 00:37:12.440
I think there is a truth
to that but there's

00:37:12.440 --> 00:37:16.130
another side to that, I think
is less often acknowledged

00:37:16.130 --> 00:37:17.833
and that certainly
I've experienced

00:37:17.833 --> 00:37:20.000
and that I think many of
you out there have probably

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:21.240
experienced as well.

00:37:21.240 --> 00:37:23.390
One other major finding of
the Harvard longitudinal

00:37:23.390 --> 00:37:26.210
study on happiness, as well
as shorter-term studies

00:37:26.210 --> 00:37:29.270
on happiness, is
that much as you've

00:37:29.270 --> 00:37:32.630
heard, perhaps that no
one on their deathbed

00:37:32.630 --> 00:37:36.050
says they wish they had
worked more, well indeed,

00:37:36.050 --> 00:37:38.510
the total amount of time
that one spends working

00:37:38.510 --> 00:37:42.360
does not seem to
determine one's happiness.

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:45.800
And yet I also want to earmark
that result as one that we need

00:37:45.800 --> 00:37:47.840
to parse a bit more carefully.

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:51.080
Because work, last
time I checked

00:37:51.080 --> 00:37:53.540
and certainly for me,
is the way typically

00:37:53.540 --> 00:37:55.020
that people earn an income.

00:37:55.020 --> 00:37:57.380
And as we just talked
about a moment ago,

00:37:57.380 --> 00:38:00.890
income is often a way
that people have access

00:38:00.890 --> 00:38:03.590
to or provide access
for their family

00:38:03.590 --> 00:38:06.920
to things like
recreation that opens up

00:38:06.920 --> 00:38:09.560
the opportunity for more
social connection, right.

00:38:09.560 --> 00:38:12.890
So we have to be careful with
how we interpret these blanket

00:38:12.890 --> 00:38:16.220
statements that have become
very popular that money doesn't

00:38:16.220 --> 00:38:19.190
determine happiness and that
the amount that you work

00:38:19.190 --> 00:38:21.350
isn't going to
determine happiness.

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:24.650
It certainly is the case
that if you earn more money

00:38:24.650 --> 00:38:28.370
from working more and
that money is devoted

00:38:28.370 --> 00:38:30.170
to things that bring
more opportunities

00:38:30.170 --> 00:38:32.630
for social connection
or for buffering stress

00:38:32.630 --> 00:38:35.890
in other areas of your
life, including health care,

00:38:35.890 --> 00:38:37.640
care for your children,
care for yourself,

00:38:37.640 --> 00:38:41.210
recreation, other things
that you enjoy, well then

00:38:41.210 --> 00:38:45.680
I think it's a little bit naive
to assume that work itself is

00:38:45.680 --> 00:38:48.210
somehow counter to happiness.

00:38:48.210 --> 00:38:49.910
Which, of course, it isn't.

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:54.710
And it especially isn't if we
combine that feature of work

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:57.500
with another important feature
of the human psyche, which

00:38:57.500 --> 00:38:59.130
is this notion of meaning.

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:00.600
Now in the
not-too-distant future,

00:39:00.600 --> 00:39:03.470
we will do an episode of
this podcast on meaning

00:39:03.470 --> 00:39:06.080
and what constitutes meaning
in a given endeavor, work

00:39:06.080 --> 00:39:07.220
or otherwise.

00:39:07.220 --> 00:39:11.780
But much of the psychology of
the last century, and still

00:39:11.780 --> 00:39:14.360
today, focuses on this
feature of meaning

00:39:14.360 --> 00:39:17.690
as a critical one in terms
of what makes us happy

00:39:17.690 --> 00:39:21.480
and what doesn't make us happy,
certainly in the long term.

00:39:21.480 --> 00:39:23.240
And I can certainly
say for myself

00:39:23.240 --> 00:39:26.300
that learning and teaching, and
doing research in my laboratory

00:39:26.300 --> 00:39:31.190
brings me tremendous feeling
of meaning and happiness.

00:39:31.190 --> 00:39:33.680
Some people consider
their work simply

00:39:33.680 --> 00:39:37.230
a way to gain a paycheck,
and other people

00:39:37.230 --> 00:39:39.890
find that they would do the
very work they do regardless of

00:39:39.890 --> 00:39:41.140
whether or not they were paid.

00:39:41.140 --> 00:39:43.680
In fact, many people will do
volunteer work and other forms

00:39:43.680 --> 00:39:45.670
of work for zero money.

00:39:45.670 --> 00:39:48.240
So this idea that
money isn't important

00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:52.020
or that work is not as
important as we deem it to be,

00:39:52.020 --> 00:39:54.360
that also needs to be
considered from a number

00:39:54.360 --> 00:39:55.600
of different perspectives.

00:39:55.600 --> 00:39:57.150
And again, by no
means am I trying

00:39:57.150 --> 00:39:59.670
to undermine the data of
these impressive studies, both

00:39:59.670 --> 00:40:01.410
the longitudinal and
short-term studies,

00:40:01.410 --> 00:40:04.770
but I think we do have to be
cautious in our discussion

00:40:04.770 --> 00:40:08.340
of results like these
because the internet is

00:40:08.340 --> 00:40:13.080
replete with conversations
about the big factors that

00:40:13.080 --> 00:40:13.980
determine happiness.

00:40:13.980 --> 00:40:16.350
It's going to be social
connection, not income.

00:40:16.350 --> 00:40:19.140
It's going to be
the amount of time

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:23.370
that you are able to have open
thinking and creativity, which

00:40:23.370 --> 00:40:25.770
I think is an essential feature
of happiness, by the way.

00:40:25.770 --> 00:40:27.180
Physical health.

00:40:27.180 --> 00:40:31.050
In particular, one's
ability to stay mobile

00:40:31.050 --> 00:40:33.870
and to be able to access
the kind of daily activities

00:40:33.870 --> 00:40:36.450
that one needs to
accomplish unassisted

00:40:36.450 --> 00:40:41.110
is a strong correlate of
happiness and so on and so on.

00:40:41.110 --> 00:40:44.130
And, of course, there are
the basic physiology factors.

00:40:44.130 --> 00:40:47.010
The things that feed back
onto our overall feelings

00:40:47.010 --> 00:40:47.700
of well-being.

00:40:47.700 --> 00:40:49.560
And I've talked
about these before,

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:51.780
and we'll just put these
quickly into a bin.

00:40:51.780 --> 00:40:53.970
You can think of this
as a toolkit of things

00:40:53.970 --> 00:40:56.820
that you and everyone really
should be constantly trying

00:40:56.820 --> 00:41:00.450
to access, if not optimize, on
a regular basis because they

00:41:00.450 --> 00:41:02.160
raise the tide or
what I would call

00:41:02.160 --> 00:41:04.623
the buoyancy of
your overall system,

00:41:04.623 --> 00:41:05.790
meaning your brain and body.

00:41:05.790 --> 00:41:09.480
And that would be getting
sufficient deep sleep at least

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:11.010
80% of the nights of your life.

00:41:11.010 --> 00:41:13.350
And ideally, the remaining
20% you're not getting deep

00:41:13.350 --> 00:41:17.340
sleep or as much of it
because of positive events.

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:19.170
Quality nutrition.

00:41:19.170 --> 00:41:20.760
Quality social interactions.

00:41:20.760 --> 00:41:22.602
And we will define that
a little bit better.

00:41:22.602 --> 00:41:24.810
In fact, we will define that
in a lot of detail later

00:41:24.810 --> 00:41:26.940
in this episode and
actually how to get better

00:41:26.940 --> 00:41:28.830
at creating quality
social interactions,

00:41:28.830 --> 00:41:31.840
even very brief
social interactions.

00:41:31.840 --> 00:41:35.190
So we have sleep,
we have nutrition,

00:41:35.190 --> 00:41:36.810
we have social interactions.

00:41:36.810 --> 00:41:38.730
We have purposeful
work, whether or not

00:41:38.730 --> 00:41:40.650
it's paid work or non-paid work.

00:41:40.650 --> 00:41:43.980
And, of course, there
are things like exercise

00:41:43.980 --> 00:41:47.070
and maybe relationships to
pets and things of that sort.

00:41:47.070 --> 00:41:49.060
And there are a
few others as well.

00:41:49.060 --> 00:41:51.660
All of those are known to
increase your overall state

00:41:51.660 --> 00:41:54.120
of well-being, that
puts you in a position

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:56.910
to access more meaning
and happiness, et cetera.

00:41:56.910 --> 00:41:58.980
But for most
people, I think it's

00:41:58.980 --> 00:42:01.200
fair to say that
earning a living.

00:42:01.200 --> 00:42:05.490
And earning a living by working
is the typical way in which

00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:07.120
we spend most of our time.

00:42:07.120 --> 00:42:09.480
So I think we need to
put a special bracket

00:42:09.480 --> 00:42:10.560
around those activities.

00:42:10.560 --> 00:42:12.602
And it's something we will
return to a little bit

00:42:12.602 --> 00:42:15.450
later in terms of trying to
understand how periods of life

00:42:15.450 --> 00:42:19.200
in which there are big
or extensive work demands

00:42:19.200 --> 00:42:21.450
or extensive family
demands on us

00:42:21.450 --> 00:42:24.030
are indeed compatible
with states of happiness

00:42:24.030 --> 00:42:27.090
or frequent states of happiness
and how better to access those.

00:42:27.090 --> 00:42:30.270
Rather than simply say
money isn't important

00:42:30.270 --> 00:42:32.952
or the amount of time at
work really isn't important.

00:42:32.952 --> 00:42:35.160
That's not what people are
going to pay attention to.

00:42:35.160 --> 00:42:37.770
In fact, I don't know how
I will feel on my deathbed.

00:42:37.770 --> 00:42:39.180
How could I?

00:42:39.180 --> 00:42:41.730
Human beings are pretty
good about understanding

00:42:41.730 --> 00:42:43.050
how they feel in the present.

00:42:43.050 --> 00:42:46.320
If not describing it, they
are pretty good at feeling it

00:42:46.320 --> 00:42:49.200
if they have any sense
of internal state.

00:42:49.200 --> 00:42:50.280
That is interception.

00:42:50.280 --> 00:42:53.430
And you could have some idea
of how you feel in a moment.

00:42:53.430 --> 00:42:57.180
We're pretty good about
describing our past feelings,

00:42:57.180 --> 00:43:00.270
at least in broad
contour, but we are not

00:43:00.270 --> 00:43:02.890
very good at projecting how
we will feel in the future.

00:43:02.890 --> 00:43:04.307
And in fact, that's
a theme that's

00:43:04.307 --> 00:43:06.540
going to come up
again and again today.

00:43:06.540 --> 00:43:11.580
Nonetheless, what we do know on
the basis of really solid data

00:43:11.580 --> 00:43:14.700
are that certain aspects
of our well-being

00:43:14.700 --> 00:43:17.040
tend to change
across our lifespan.

00:43:17.040 --> 00:43:20.130
Now lifespan is
something that we

00:43:20.130 --> 00:43:22.830
need to consider from
also a bit of nuance

00:43:22.830 --> 00:43:25.870
because humans are indeed
living longer and longer.

00:43:25.870 --> 00:43:27.840
And if we look at
the data on happiness

00:43:27.840 --> 00:43:32.430
across the lifespan dated maybe
30 or 40 years back or even

00:43:32.430 --> 00:43:35.790
20 years ago, it is
consistently described

00:43:35.790 --> 00:43:38.640
in that literature as a
so-called u-shaped function.

00:43:38.640 --> 00:43:45.090
Where people in their 20s
report being very, very happy,

00:43:45.090 --> 00:43:49.210
but as time goes on and they
acquire more responsibility.

00:43:49.210 --> 00:43:51.150
So typically, getting
married and having

00:43:51.150 --> 00:43:55.920
children in their mid to late
20s and 30s and into their 40s.

00:43:55.920 --> 00:43:58.020
Having more work
demands, et cetera,

00:43:58.020 --> 00:44:00.370
happiness tends to be
rated lower and lower,

00:44:00.370 --> 00:44:01.830
at least in those
previous studies.

00:44:01.830 --> 00:44:05.790
And then happiness tended
to increase as people

00:44:05.790 --> 00:44:07.500
approach their 50s and 60s.

00:44:07.500 --> 00:44:09.653
And they tended to retire,
and their work demands

00:44:09.653 --> 00:44:11.070
were shed from
them, and they were

00:44:11.070 --> 00:44:13.800
able to enjoy the small things
of life despite the fact

00:44:13.800 --> 00:44:17.280
that, in general, I
would say almost always

00:44:17.280 --> 00:44:20.160
people's health is not as
vigorous when they're 70

00:44:20.160 --> 00:44:21.750
as it is when they're 20.

00:44:21.750 --> 00:44:23.700
There are exceptions
to that, of course.

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:26.650
Of course, you can adjust the
rate of cognitive and physical

00:44:26.650 --> 00:44:27.150
decline.

00:44:27.150 --> 00:44:28.850
But in general,
people in their 20s

00:44:28.850 --> 00:44:30.600
feel more physically
and mentally vigorous

00:44:30.600 --> 00:44:33.390
than they do in their
60s and 70s, in general.

00:44:33.390 --> 00:44:36.780
That u-shaped function
that I just described still

00:44:36.780 --> 00:44:38.910
holds true today,
but of course, there

00:44:38.910 --> 00:44:43.740
have been some major shifts
to the general life stages

00:44:43.740 --> 00:44:45.985
and when people undergo
those life stages.

00:44:45.985 --> 00:44:48.360
For instance, many people are
getting married much later.

00:44:48.360 --> 00:44:50.620
Many people are opting
to not have children.

00:44:50.620 --> 00:44:54.120
In fact, if you look at the data
on whether or not people have

00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:57.115
children or not and how
that relates to happiness,

00:44:57.115 --> 00:44:59.490
everyone will tell you that
their kids are their greatest

00:44:59.490 --> 00:45:01.800
source of joy, at least most
people will tell you that

00:45:01.800 --> 00:45:05.040
and are a tremendous
source of happiness.

00:45:05.040 --> 00:45:05.640
It's obvious.

00:45:05.640 --> 00:45:08.700
Kids are delightful, and
raising kids while hard

00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:10.930
is a wonderful experience.

00:45:10.930 --> 00:45:13.620
If you look at the
ratings of happiness

00:45:13.620 --> 00:45:18.600
among people that elected to not
have children versus those that

00:45:18.600 --> 00:45:21.300
had, most people
who have children

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:23.250
report their overall
levels of happiness

00:45:23.250 --> 00:45:28.170
as lower than that of people
who opt not to have children.

00:45:28.170 --> 00:45:31.080
Now there are a lot of ways
to interpret those findings.

00:45:31.080 --> 00:45:34.800
And by no means am I encouraging
people to not have children.

00:45:34.800 --> 00:45:37.920
That's a issue that you have
to resolve for yourself,

00:45:37.920 --> 00:45:39.000
of course.

00:45:39.000 --> 00:45:41.710
But we could imagine,
for instance,

00:45:41.710 --> 00:45:44.160
that people who opt
not to have children

00:45:44.160 --> 00:45:46.890
have more income to devote
to things more focused

00:45:46.890 --> 00:45:50.500
on themselves or their partner
or other aspects of their life.

00:45:50.500 --> 00:45:52.650
We don't know if that's
the underlying reason.

00:45:52.650 --> 00:45:55.200
We could perhaps
conclude that people

00:45:55.200 --> 00:45:57.900
who opt not to have children
are getting more sleep

00:45:57.900 --> 00:46:01.260
on a regular basis or have
more time for exercise

00:46:01.260 --> 00:46:03.300
or the other sorts of
things that elevate

00:46:03.300 --> 00:46:05.250
states of mood and well-being.

00:46:05.250 --> 00:46:07.380
Again we do not know
what the underlying

00:46:07.380 --> 00:46:08.760
reasons are for this finding.

00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:12.540
But it does seem that
despite most every parent

00:46:12.540 --> 00:46:15.030
reporting that their kids are
their greatest source of joy

00:46:15.030 --> 00:46:16.920
and quote-unquote
happiness in life

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:20.430
that people who opt not to have
children are at least as happy

00:46:20.430 --> 00:46:24.000
or report being at least
as happy or even happier

00:46:24.000 --> 00:46:27.000
than those that opt
to have children.

00:46:27.000 --> 00:46:29.220
And, of course, I
want to be very clear

00:46:29.220 --> 00:46:32.087
that I'm not trying to
settle any arguments about

00:46:32.087 --> 00:46:34.170
whether or not people
should have children or not.

00:46:34.170 --> 00:46:37.170
I happen to find children
and animals delightful.

00:46:37.170 --> 00:46:40.830
And I'm always happy when
people opt to have children

00:46:40.830 --> 00:46:43.278
provided they are taking
good care of their children

00:46:43.278 --> 00:46:45.820
or doing their very best to take
good care of their children.

00:46:45.820 --> 00:46:47.340
So that's my stance.

00:46:47.340 --> 00:46:49.080
But of course,
you're all entitled

00:46:49.080 --> 00:46:50.680
to your own stance on this.

00:46:50.680 --> 00:46:52.275
There are also the
general arguments

00:46:52.275 --> 00:46:54.150
that people like to have
about whether or not

00:46:54.150 --> 00:46:57.073
the population of the Earth will
be sustained or not sustained

00:46:57.073 --> 00:46:58.740
based on current birth
rates, et cetera.

00:46:58.740 --> 00:47:02.040
Indeed many areas of the world,
birth rates are going down.

00:47:02.040 --> 00:47:04.260
It is actually
something that just

00:47:04.260 --> 00:47:05.820
as a perhaps point
of interest has

00:47:05.820 --> 00:47:11.280
been studied from the somewhat
unusual but logical perspective

00:47:11.280 --> 00:47:15.420
of whether or not child diapers
are selling at the same rate

00:47:15.420 --> 00:47:18.960
as they were some years ago and
whether or not adult diapers

00:47:18.960 --> 00:47:23.380
for the elderly are being sold
at the same rate or greater.

00:47:23.380 --> 00:47:26.820
If you think about it is one
indirect measure of whether

00:47:26.820 --> 00:47:29.840
or not people are living longer
and/or opting to have children.

00:47:29.840 --> 00:47:31.590
Definitely in discussion
for another time,

00:47:31.590 --> 00:47:33.870
probably for another
podcast entirely.

