00:48:48.200 --> 00:48:52.310
So this u-shaped function
of people being happier

00:48:52.310 --> 00:48:54.740
earlier in life
and then reporting

00:48:54.740 --> 00:48:56.630
feeling far less happy
and then happiness

00:48:56.630 --> 00:48:59.480
returning to them, that is,
the rising of the u again

00:48:59.480 --> 00:49:02.390
in their later years, is
something that I do believe

00:49:02.390 --> 00:49:05.180
should be repeated
in modern times.

00:49:05.180 --> 00:49:08.600
And repeated in a way that
takes into account that you

00:49:08.600 --> 00:49:10.820
might be shifted to the right.

00:49:10.820 --> 00:49:14.140
That is, I am certainly
aware that people are

00:49:14.140 --> 00:49:15.850
tending to get married later.

00:49:15.850 --> 00:49:17.570
Many are opting to
not have children.

00:49:17.570 --> 00:49:20.980
So, for instance, the
question arises whether or not

00:49:20.980 --> 00:49:23.170
that u-shaped curve
should have a bump down

00:49:23.170 --> 00:49:24.860
at the bottom of
the u among those

00:49:24.860 --> 00:49:26.110
that opt not to have children.

00:49:26.110 --> 00:49:29.770
Because the argument was made in
the discussion of those papers

00:49:29.770 --> 00:49:32.260
that the reason why
happiness is lower

00:49:32.260 --> 00:49:34.390
when people are in
their 30s, 40s, and 50s

00:49:34.390 --> 00:49:37.330
is because they're devoting more
time to raising their children

00:49:37.330 --> 00:49:40.008
and devoting more time to work.

00:49:40.008 --> 00:49:41.800
I would hope people
would enjoy their work,

00:49:41.800 --> 00:49:43.600
but not everybody really
enjoys their work.

00:49:43.600 --> 00:49:46.083
And many people, even if
they do enjoy their work

00:49:46.083 --> 00:49:47.500
and they find
meaning in it, still

00:49:47.500 --> 00:49:49.570
find it stressful,
which certainly

00:49:49.570 --> 00:49:52.990
can run counter to happiness.

00:49:52.990 --> 00:49:56.410
Nowadays, you could imagine
that because a number of people

00:49:56.410 --> 00:49:59.260
are opting perhaps to work
less or to not have children

00:49:59.260 --> 00:50:01.312
or both, where they
find tremendous meaning

00:50:01.312 --> 00:50:02.770
from their work
that there would be

00:50:02.770 --> 00:50:07.420
a bump at the bottom of that
u among those that decided to

00:50:07.420 --> 00:50:10.113
simply not take on these
additional responsibilities.

00:50:10.113 --> 00:50:11.530
That would be an
interesting test,

00:50:11.530 --> 00:50:16.030
I think, of whether or not the
total load of responsibility

00:50:16.030 --> 00:50:20.440
is really what's correlating
with reported happiness or not.

00:50:20.440 --> 00:50:23.380
Now one very consistent
finding that has absolutely

00:50:23.380 --> 00:50:25.880
stood the test of time, and
it's kind of an interesting one.

00:50:25.880 --> 00:50:27.490
It's a little bit of a
pop psychology finding,

00:50:27.490 --> 00:50:29.980
but I think it points to
something interesting that we

00:50:29.980 --> 00:50:31.360
will return to
again and again is

00:50:31.360 --> 00:50:34.660
that people tend
to report feeling

00:50:34.660 --> 00:50:37.130
lower levels of happiness,
believe it or not,

00:50:37.130 --> 00:50:38.710
on their birthday.

00:50:38.710 --> 00:50:43.330
And the argument for why
this is the following.

00:50:43.330 --> 00:50:46.870
That typically, we
go through our year

00:50:46.870 --> 00:50:50.500
not comparing ourselves to
our peers terribly much.

00:50:50.500 --> 00:50:52.257
We might do that
a little bit more

00:50:52.257 --> 00:50:54.590
when we're in elementary
school, high school, et cetera.

00:50:54.590 --> 00:50:56.950
We're sort of age match,
maybe even college as well.

00:50:56.950 --> 00:51:00.310
But an evaluation of ourselves
to our age-match peers

00:51:00.310 --> 00:51:04.030
is not typically something
that we do on a daily basis.

00:51:04.030 --> 00:51:06.910
Whereas on our birthday,
we get a snapshot

00:51:06.910 --> 00:51:10.510
of where we are in the arc of
time, or at least in our life.

00:51:10.510 --> 00:51:14.350
And many people report feeling
rather low on their birthday

00:51:14.350 --> 00:51:17.980
because they use that as
a benchmark or a window

00:51:17.980 --> 00:51:20.530
into the things that they
have not accomplished.

00:51:20.530 --> 00:51:23.710
The things that,
despite being age blank,

00:51:23.710 --> 00:51:25.480
they still haven't accomplished.

00:51:25.480 --> 00:51:27.610
And so that's interesting
because what it really

00:51:27.610 --> 00:51:28.910
points to is two things.

00:51:28.910 --> 00:51:32.170
One the extent to which much
of our feelings of happiness

00:51:32.170 --> 00:51:34.910
are relative, in particular,
relative to our peers.

00:51:34.910 --> 00:51:37.570
So there's that
social aspect again.

00:51:37.570 --> 00:51:40.660
And the fact that
most of the time, we

00:51:40.660 --> 00:51:43.150
are not very good at
orienting ourselves

00:51:43.150 --> 00:51:44.253
in the longer arc of time.

00:51:44.253 --> 00:51:45.670
We're pretty good
at knowing where

00:51:45.670 --> 00:51:47.712
we are in the arc of a
day, or the arc of a week,

00:51:47.712 --> 00:51:50.680
or the arc of a month, or even
a year, but that most of us

00:51:50.680 --> 00:51:52.510
are not very good at
reflecting on where

00:51:52.510 --> 00:51:53.968
we are in our life arc.

00:51:53.968 --> 00:51:55.510
And, of course, most
of us don't know

00:51:55.510 --> 00:51:57.550
how long we will
live anyway, but we

00:51:57.550 --> 00:51:58.960
do have some general sense.

00:51:58.960 --> 00:52:02.290
I mean, very few people
live past the age of 100.

00:52:02.290 --> 00:52:05.020
Many people live to be 70 or 80.

00:52:05.020 --> 00:52:08.950
And again, life span
is extending as far

00:52:08.950 --> 00:52:11.080
as we know from year to year.

00:52:11.080 --> 00:52:14.440
But, in general, people report
that on their birthdays.

00:52:14.440 --> 00:52:16.210
And I should say these
are for birthdays

00:52:16.210 --> 00:52:20.050
aged 25 or later, at
least in the studies

00:52:20.050 --> 00:52:21.798
I was able to access.

00:52:21.798 --> 00:52:23.590
I don't think that a
lot of three-year-olds

00:52:23.590 --> 00:52:25.923
sit around comparing themselves
to other three-year-olds

00:52:25.923 --> 00:52:27.820
and how well they're
doing or 12-year-olds.

00:52:27.820 --> 00:52:30.550
You can imagine some people
might do that at 18, et cetera.

00:52:30.550 --> 00:52:32.890
But it's really by the
mid-20s that people

00:52:32.890 --> 00:52:35.050
start evaluating
themselves to their peers

00:52:35.050 --> 00:52:38.050
in terms of life progression
and so-called milestones.

00:52:38.050 --> 00:52:40.660
It's been argued that that's
one of the reasons why people

00:52:40.660 --> 00:52:42.790
report lower affect,
lower levels of happiness

00:52:42.790 --> 00:52:43.540
on their birthday.

00:52:43.540 --> 00:52:45.498
Something that's a little
bit counterintuitive.

00:52:45.498 --> 00:52:47.207
And, of course, there
are things that are

00:52:47.207 --> 00:52:48.730
anti-correlated with happiness.

00:52:48.730 --> 00:52:51.040
I'd be remiss if I didn't
mention a few of these.

00:52:51.040 --> 00:52:54.850
That's longitudinal study,
the Harvard happiness project

00:52:54.850 --> 00:52:57.820
has reported, for
instance, that people that

00:52:57.820 --> 00:53:00.940
are chronic smokers of
nicotine and chronic

00:53:00.940 --> 00:53:03.850
consumers of alcohol, in
particular alcoholics.

00:53:03.850 --> 00:53:05.620
That is, people who
suffer from alcoholism

00:53:05.620 --> 00:53:08.950
or what sometimes is called
alcohol use disorder that

00:53:08.950 --> 00:53:11.260
is strongly anti-correlated
with happiness.

00:53:11.260 --> 00:53:13.390
And I should also mention
that the family members,

00:53:13.390 --> 00:53:15.130
and in particular,
the romantic partners

00:53:15.130 --> 00:53:19.720
of people who are chronic
smokers and the partners

00:53:19.720 --> 00:53:23.200
of people who are chronic
alcohol users, often

00:53:23.200 --> 00:53:24.790
will report lower
levels of happiness.

00:53:24.790 --> 00:53:26.830
Especially if they
themselves are not

00:53:26.830 --> 00:53:31.300
chronic smokers or regular
consumers of alcohol.

00:53:31.300 --> 00:53:35.020
So we've done episodes on
nicotine in particular,

00:53:35.020 --> 00:53:36.730
and that touched on
smoking, of course.

00:53:36.730 --> 00:53:39.370
And we've done an
episode on alcohol

00:53:39.370 --> 00:53:41.740
and the effects of
alcohol on health.

00:53:41.740 --> 00:53:43.810
Again, you can find
those at hubermanlab.com.

00:53:43.810 --> 00:53:46.990
This study from the
Harvard happiness project

00:53:46.990 --> 00:53:49.690
really has strong
data supporting

00:53:49.690 --> 00:53:52.810
the fact that avoiding
being a nicotine smoker.

00:53:52.810 --> 00:53:54.888
There are positive health
effects of nicotine

00:53:54.888 --> 00:53:56.680
that are discussed in
the episode nicotine.

00:53:56.680 --> 00:53:59.440
But being a-- but smoking
nicotine, in particular,

00:53:59.440 --> 00:54:03.460
is counterproductive for
people's at least self-reported

00:54:03.460 --> 00:54:05.530
happiness and certainly
overall health.

00:54:05.530 --> 00:54:08.140
I think there's zero question
that smoking increases

00:54:08.140 --> 00:54:09.730
cancers of different kinds.

00:54:09.730 --> 00:54:11.650
And that alcohol consumption.

00:54:11.650 --> 00:54:14.440
And in particular, alcohol
consumption beyond two

00:54:14.440 --> 00:54:16.390
drinks per week.

00:54:16.390 --> 00:54:18.850
Two drinks being the
typical volume of a beer,

00:54:18.850 --> 00:54:20.920
a glass of wine or
cocktail, et cetera,

00:54:20.920 --> 00:54:23.087
is detrimental for
various aspects of health.

00:54:23.087 --> 00:54:24.670
And, of course, there
are other things

00:54:24.670 --> 00:54:28.150
that you could imagine would
relate to a lack of happiness.

00:54:28.150 --> 00:54:30.230
For instance, a major trauma.

00:54:30.230 --> 00:54:32.500
Physical or emotional trauma.

00:54:32.500 --> 00:54:34.690
That could include the loss
of a major relationship,

00:54:34.690 --> 00:54:37.000
a death of a close one.

00:54:37.000 --> 00:54:40.210
Being the victim of a violent
crime and things of that sort.

00:54:40.210 --> 00:54:45.070
And yet it's been argued, in
fact, strongly argued that when

00:54:45.070 --> 00:54:49.420
you look at people's levels
of happiness after a trauma,

00:54:49.420 --> 00:54:54.070
that if you wait
about a year or so,

00:54:54.070 --> 00:54:56.230
sometimes is even as
short as three months

00:54:56.230 --> 00:54:59.380
after a trauma, that
people self-reported levels

00:54:59.380 --> 00:55:02.830
of happiness are not
significantly lower than they

00:55:02.830 --> 00:55:04.360
were prior to the trauma.

00:55:04.360 --> 00:55:08.830
Now I very much want to
highlight, underline, and bold

00:55:08.830 --> 00:55:11.322
and asterisks that
statement as one

00:55:11.322 --> 00:55:13.030
that we really need
to explore carefully.

00:55:13.030 --> 00:55:17.230
Because there are other data
that strongly point to the fact

00:55:17.230 --> 00:55:20.080
that major life
traumas can severely

00:55:20.080 --> 00:55:22.270
disrupt one sense of
happiness and well-being.

00:55:22.270 --> 00:55:24.728
And I think as long as we're
going to have this discussion,

00:55:24.728 --> 00:55:27.790
we should point to a useful
definition of trauma.

00:55:27.790 --> 00:55:29.830
And the definition
that I'll paraphrase

00:55:29.830 --> 00:55:33.940
is one that was supplied by a
former guest on the Huberman

00:55:33.940 --> 00:55:37.330
Lab podcast, Dr. Paul Conti,
who's a psychiatrist who's

00:55:37.330 --> 00:55:38.710
written a book called Trauma.

00:55:38.710 --> 00:55:42.492
I personally think it's the
best book on trauma and tools

00:55:42.492 --> 00:55:43.450
for alleviating trauma.

00:55:43.450 --> 00:55:47.350
It's incredibly thorough, easy
to read, and well-informed.

00:55:47.350 --> 00:55:49.897
And here again,
I'm paraphrasing,

00:55:49.897 --> 00:55:52.480
but Dr. Conti describes trauma
as something that fundamentally

00:55:52.480 --> 00:55:56.440
changes the way that our
brain and body function

00:55:56.440 --> 00:55:58.990
in a way that makes
other aspects of living

00:55:58.990 --> 00:56:00.580
more challenging.

00:56:00.580 --> 00:56:03.520
Again, an event, either
emotional or physical,

00:56:03.520 --> 00:56:05.560
or both, that fundamentally
changes the way

00:56:05.560 --> 00:56:10.000
that our brain and/or
body, our nervous system,

00:56:10.000 --> 00:56:13.420
and other organs function
in a way that prevents us

00:56:13.420 --> 00:56:15.910
from enjoying daily activities.

00:56:15.910 --> 00:56:17.830
And that could even be
ongoing distraction.

00:56:17.830 --> 00:56:20.110
Traumas can create
rumination, or they

00:56:20.110 --> 00:56:22.870
can create obsessive thought,
or they can create dissociation.

00:56:22.870 --> 00:56:24.200
Any number of different things.

00:56:24.200 --> 00:56:26.830
Again check out that
episode with Dr. Paul Conti

00:56:26.830 --> 00:56:28.570
if you'd like to learn
more about trauma

00:56:28.570 --> 00:56:29.470
and how it manifests.

00:56:29.470 --> 00:56:32.020
But the idea that's
been put forth

00:56:32.020 --> 00:56:34.750
by a number of researchers
in the field of happiness

00:56:34.750 --> 00:56:37.450
that three months after
a major trauma, people

00:56:37.450 --> 00:56:40.870
aren't reported that they
are feeling any less happy

00:56:40.870 --> 00:56:43.270
than before the trauma,
that was surprising to me.

00:56:43.270 --> 00:56:46.220
So I went into this
literature a bit more deeply.

00:56:46.220 --> 00:56:49.480
One of the basis of that
general line of thinking

00:56:49.480 --> 00:56:53.290
is a-- what I consider a now
classic and very important

00:56:53.290 --> 00:56:55.270
and frankly excellent
talk that was

00:56:55.270 --> 00:56:58.750
given by Professor Dan Gilbert
on the science of happiness.

00:56:58.750 --> 00:57:01.780
You can find this on YouTube.

00:57:01.780 --> 00:57:04.840
I say a classic one because
it was done some years ago.

00:57:04.840 --> 00:57:06.850
It's received millions of views.

00:57:06.850 --> 00:57:09.100
And one of the points that
he makes in that talk which

00:57:09.100 --> 00:57:11.140
is grounded in
research carried out

00:57:11.140 --> 00:57:14.530
by his laboratory and other
laboratories is that--

00:57:14.530 --> 00:57:16.250
he poses a question.

00:57:16.250 --> 00:57:18.790
He says you know,
let's do a quiz.

00:57:18.790 --> 00:57:21.260
Would you rather be someone
who wins the lottery

00:57:21.260 --> 00:57:23.260
and he shows a picture
of somebody who just won,

00:57:23.260 --> 00:57:25.510
I think it was several
hundreds of millions of dollars

00:57:25.510 --> 00:57:29.140
in the lottery, or was
recently made paraplegic lost

00:57:29.140 --> 00:57:30.950
use of their legs?

00:57:30.950 --> 00:57:34.280
And then goes on to
state that one year

00:57:34.280 --> 00:57:36.210
after people have
won the lottery,

00:57:36.210 --> 00:57:39.860
this major monetary windfall
versus have become paraplegic

00:57:39.860 --> 00:57:42.680
is that their self-reported
levels of happiness

00:57:42.680 --> 00:57:43.770
are the same.

00:57:43.770 --> 00:57:47.030
Which I think is
incredibly surprising.

00:57:47.030 --> 00:57:50.900
I heard this, and I immediately
thought of an experience

00:57:50.900 --> 00:57:54.560
that I've had where I
teach a course at Stanford

00:57:54.560 --> 00:57:57.020
School of Medicine on
neural regeneration.

00:57:57.020 --> 00:57:59.510
And it's actually a course
that I attended some years

00:57:59.510 --> 00:58:01.170
ago when I was a
post-doc at Stanford,

00:58:01.170 --> 00:58:03.000
so well over a decade ago.

00:58:03.000 --> 00:58:05.630
And we had-- excuse me.

00:58:05.630 --> 00:58:09.180
We had an individual
come into the course.

00:58:09.180 --> 00:58:11.030
This was an older gentleman.

00:58:11.030 --> 00:58:13.550
So older meaning he
was in his early 70s.

00:58:13.550 --> 00:58:16.370
And he had become paraplegic
fairly late in life

00:58:16.370 --> 00:58:18.020
from a cycling accident.

00:58:18.020 --> 00:58:23.610
And he was and is
an expert in what

00:58:23.610 --> 00:58:25.410
it is to become a
paraplegic, of course,

00:58:25.410 --> 00:58:27.827
because he had that experience,
but also because he spends

00:58:27.827 --> 00:58:30.120
a lot of his time doing
volunteer work with people who

00:58:30.120 --> 00:58:32.670
have become paraplegic
and have become paraplegic

00:58:32.670 --> 00:58:33.540
at different ages.

00:58:33.540 --> 00:58:37.470
And what he described to me
was that the overall outcomes

00:58:37.470 --> 00:58:39.990
for people that are
rendered paraplegic

00:58:39.990 --> 00:58:43.760
in terms of their mental health
and their physical well-being

00:58:43.760 --> 00:58:46.110
and their management
of general life skills

00:58:46.110 --> 00:58:50.400
scales with how early
they had that injury

00:58:50.400 --> 00:58:52.570
and how long they had
the use of their limbs.

00:58:52.570 --> 00:58:55.300
So it's not straightforward.

00:58:55.300 --> 00:59:00.190
When I heard this result
described by Dr. Dan Gilbert,

00:59:00.190 --> 00:59:02.440
that winning the lottery
and becoming paraplegic

00:59:02.440 --> 00:59:05.230
basically don't impact
your levels of happiness

00:59:05.230 --> 00:59:10.220
to any different degree when
people look back a year later,

00:59:10.220 --> 00:59:13.150
I was pretty surprised
given my experience

00:59:13.150 --> 00:59:15.170
of hearing this
lecture at Stanford.

00:59:15.170 --> 00:59:17.140
So I thought, wow,
from what I understand.

00:59:17.140 --> 00:59:19.360
Indeed, there are people
who are rendered paraplegic

00:59:19.360 --> 00:59:22.000
and manage that
transition very easily.

00:59:22.000 --> 00:59:24.592
It doesn't seem to disrupt
their feelings of well-being,

00:59:24.592 --> 00:59:26.050
et cetera, but for
other people, it

00:59:26.050 --> 00:59:30.610
can be severely disrupting
to their sense of well-being

00:59:30.610 --> 00:59:32.230
and so on and so forth.

00:59:32.230 --> 00:59:35.290
I went back and
examined these data.

00:59:35.290 --> 00:59:37.690
And in fact, a
subsequent talk, it's

00:59:37.690 --> 00:59:40.390
actually a podcast that
was given by Dr. Dan

00:59:40.390 --> 00:59:42.167
Gilbert some years later.

00:59:42.167 --> 00:59:43.750
So this would be
just a few years ago.

00:59:43.750 --> 00:59:46.180
I think in 2019-- there
is a specific date

00:59:46.180 --> 00:59:49.430
in which it was recorded
but just a few years ago.

00:59:49.430 --> 00:59:53.050
And indeed, he corrects
himself in that podcast.

00:59:53.050 --> 00:59:57.040
What he says is that he
misspoke in that earlier talk.

00:59:57.040 --> 01:00:00.550
That the difference in
self-reported levels

01:00:00.550 --> 01:00:03.760
of happiness for those that
have been rendered paraplegic

01:00:03.760 --> 01:00:08.860
for versus those who've won the
lottery is not as great as one

01:00:08.860 --> 01:00:09.445
would expect.

01:00:09.445 --> 01:00:12.070
I think most people would expect
that being rendered paraplegic

01:00:12.070 --> 01:00:14.200
would make people
far less happy.

01:00:14.200 --> 01:00:16.210
That's the expectation,
I think, anyway.

01:00:16.210 --> 01:00:17.890
And that people would
win the lottery,

01:00:17.890 --> 01:00:19.360
at least for some
period of time,

01:00:19.360 --> 01:00:21.190
would be far happier
than they were

01:00:21.190 --> 01:00:23.770
prior to winning the lottery.

01:00:23.770 --> 01:00:26.510
And especially given the
tremendous amount of money.

01:00:26.510 --> 01:00:29.600
And again, the fact that
money can't buy happiness,

01:00:29.600 --> 01:00:32.410
but that money does
indeed enable the ability

01:00:32.410 --> 01:00:34.990
to buffer stress
provided people were

01:00:34.990 --> 01:00:36.490
responsible with
that money and just

01:00:36.490 --> 01:00:38.320
didn't blow it or spend
it all right away.

01:00:38.320 --> 01:00:40.120
That they could start
to afford things

01:00:40.120 --> 01:00:42.730
that they couldn't afford, not
just in terms of luxury items

01:00:42.730 --> 01:00:44.700
but also the
ability to hire help

01:00:44.700 --> 01:00:47.200
that would free up time that
would allow them to do anything

01:00:47.200 --> 01:00:49.997
from travel that they couldn't
access before to meditate

01:00:49.997 --> 01:00:52.580
if that was something that they
didn't have time to do before.

01:00:52.580 --> 01:00:54.020
And so on and so forth.

01:00:54.020 --> 01:00:58.120
So the result quote
unquote that winners

01:00:58.120 --> 01:00:59.710
of the lottery and
recent paraplegics

01:00:59.710 --> 01:01:02.350
have the same levels of
happiness is actually not true.

01:01:02.350 --> 01:01:05.440
At least according to the
author of the original study.

01:01:05.440 --> 01:01:08.950
Now what he did not point to is
the degree to which that is not

01:01:08.950 --> 01:01:11.377
true, but he did point to
the direction of the result.

01:01:11.377 --> 01:01:13.960
And the fact that people who are
rendered paraplegic, in fact,

01:01:13.960 --> 01:01:17.850
are reporting
themselves as less happy

01:01:17.850 --> 01:01:19.560
than they were prior
to their injury.

01:01:19.560 --> 01:01:22.200
And certainly that their
levels of happiness

01:01:22.200 --> 01:01:24.753
are lower than those that
simply won the lottery

01:01:24.753 --> 01:01:26.670
hundreds of millions of
dollars, which I think

01:01:26.670 --> 01:01:28.960
is the more intuitive result.

01:01:28.960 --> 01:01:31.830
And so I think it's important
to be aware of that discrepancy

01:01:31.830 --> 01:01:33.330
because it's something
that was lost

01:01:33.330 --> 01:01:36.270
in the communication
around those results

01:01:36.270 --> 01:01:37.390
the first time around.

01:01:37.390 --> 01:01:40.530
And indeed, Dan Gilbert
is an excellent scientist

01:01:40.530 --> 01:01:44.280
and was quite good about trying
to correct the narrative.

01:01:44.280 --> 01:01:47.178
I myself, as a podcaster
who puts information

01:01:47.178 --> 01:01:49.470
on the internet, know that
the challenges of correcting

01:01:49.470 --> 01:01:52.945
narratives, especially of things
that came out some time ago,

01:01:52.945 --> 01:01:54.570
we always attempt to
do this as best we

01:01:54.570 --> 01:01:57.090
can, but not everyone that
saw that first video will

01:01:57.090 --> 01:02:00.240
necessarily hear the discussion
that has happened subsequently.

01:02:00.240 --> 01:02:05.280
So my hope is that Dr. Gilbert
will interpret me communicating

01:02:05.280 --> 01:02:07.260
this now not as an
attempt to criticize him

01:02:07.260 --> 01:02:09.510
but rather as an attempt
to praise his willingness

01:02:09.510 --> 01:02:12.100
to try and correct the
narrative to be more accurate.

01:02:12.100 --> 01:02:15.090
So to be very clear about what
this study did and didn't show.

01:02:15.090 --> 01:02:18.720
And here, I'm going to
combine these results

01:02:18.720 --> 01:02:21.030
with other studies
that I was able to find

01:02:21.030 --> 01:02:22.890
that explored
similar phenomenon.

01:02:22.890 --> 01:02:25.050
So major trauma, for instance.

01:02:25.050 --> 01:02:28.050
Not necessarily becoming
paraplegic but traumas

01:02:28.050 --> 01:02:30.630
of a different sort,
emotional traumas.

01:02:30.630 --> 01:02:33.060
When you look at the whole
of those data, at least,

01:02:33.060 --> 01:02:38.340
my read is that when people win
the lottery or acquire wealth

01:02:38.340 --> 01:02:41.220
through inheritance, some
form of wealth acquisition

01:02:41.220 --> 01:02:44.670
that is sudden and that wasn't
preceded by a specific effort

01:02:44.670 --> 01:02:45.900
to gain that wealth, right.

01:02:45.900 --> 01:02:48.330
Buying a lottery ticket
is a pretty quick thing.

01:02:48.330 --> 01:02:50.640
Inheritance is something
that you simply

01:02:50.640 --> 01:02:54.390
get by virtue of who you are,
not necessarily by effort.

01:02:54.390 --> 01:02:59.460
Well, that led to increases
in self-reported happiness

01:02:59.460 --> 01:03:02.430
compared to prior to the
inheritance of the lottery win,

01:03:02.430 --> 01:03:04.800
but it wasn't as
substantial as you

01:03:04.800 --> 01:03:07.440
might imagine if
you are approaching

01:03:07.440 --> 01:03:09.900
the notion of
happiness simply from,

01:03:09.900 --> 01:03:12.000
well, more money
equals more happiness.

01:03:12.000 --> 01:03:16.290
And while it is true that people
who are rendered paraplegic

01:03:16.290 --> 01:03:20.940
or who undergo psychological
traumas or physical traumas

01:03:20.940 --> 01:03:25.690
of any various kind
are, and frankly,

01:03:25.690 --> 01:03:28.200
are remarkably
resilient in many cases.

01:03:28.200 --> 01:03:31.380
They can still manage to
go about life and work

01:03:31.380 --> 01:03:33.870
and engage in
relationships, et cetera.

01:03:33.870 --> 01:03:38.100
There is a visible decrease
in overall levels of happiness

01:03:38.100 --> 01:03:40.470
and well-being,
in particular, if

01:03:40.470 --> 01:03:42.690
the psychological
and physical trauma

01:03:42.690 --> 01:03:47.580
renders their nervous system
different in a way that

01:03:47.580 --> 01:03:51.810
impacts other major areas of
life and enjoyment for them.

01:03:51.810 --> 01:03:54.850
And that's certainly true
one year out from the trauma.

01:03:54.850 --> 01:03:57.118
So the point is that
we do need to reframe

01:03:57.118 --> 01:03:59.160
this idea that whether or
not you win the lottery

01:03:59.160 --> 01:04:01.463
or become paraplegic or
suffer some major trauma,

01:04:01.463 --> 01:04:02.880
your levels of
happiness are going

01:04:02.880 --> 01:04:05.130
to be the same three
months or a year later.

01:04:05.130 --> 01:04:06.910
I don't think that's accurate.

01:04:06.910 --> 01:04:09.510
And in fact, Dr. Dan
Gilbert emphasized

01:04:09.510 --> 01:04:13.260
that that's not accurate
even in that initial study.

01:04:13.260 --> 01:04:15.060
And I think it's
an important thing

01:04:15.060 --> 01:04:17.730
to frame because that's
such a popular notion.

01:04:17.730 --> 01:04:19.980
Or that that idea
combined with the idea

01:04:19.980 --> 01:04:22.530
that increased earnings
don't make us happy

01:04:22.530 --> 01:04:26.910
combined with the idea that
we are happy early in life

01:04:26.910 --> 01:04:30.270
but then as more demands arise
in life, we become less happy,

01:04:30.270 --> 01:04:31.770
and then we become happy again.

01:04:31.770 --> 01:04:33.750
And that idea is
we already explored

01:04:33.750 --> 01:04:34.785
is not necessarily true.

01:04:34.785 --> 01:04:36.660
Frankly, I knew a lot
of teenagers and people

01:04:36.660 --> 01:04:39.235
in their early 20s that
are pretty unhappy who

01:04:39.235 --> 01:04:41.610
then become happier later as
they acquire more resources.

01:04:41.610 --> 01:04:43.740
Sometimes distance,
let's be honest,

01:04:43.740 --> 01:04:46.260
sometimes distance from
our family of origin

01:04:46.260 --> 01:04:48.510
makes us more happy,
sometimes less so.

01:04:48.510 --> 01:04:49.990
It's highly individual.

01:04:49.990 --> 01:04:53.400
So I think those general themes
that we've heard over and over,

01:04:53.400 --> 01:04:55.500
while they have merit and
they certainly stand up

01:04:55.500 --> 01:04:57.870
in some of the more
powerful longitudinal

01:04:57.870 --> 01:05:01.350
and short-term studies, there
is nuance, and in some cases,

01:05:01.350 --> 01:05:03.420
there are now additional
data that are causing us

01:05:03.420 --> 01:05:05.430
to revise those understandings.

01:05:05.430 --> 01:05:06.990
Now there is an
important point, or I

01:05:06.990 --> 01:05:10.740
should say the important
point, that we can really

01:05:10.740 --> 01:05:14.640
credit Dan Gilbert and others
in the field of psychology with

01:05:14.640 --> 01:05:16.950
and that we owe them a
great debt of gratitude

01:05:16.950 --> 01:05:21.390
for is that we do
have far more control

01:05:21.390 --> 01:05:24.180
over our levels of happiness
than we might think.

01:05:24.180 --> 01:05:27.420
And many of the
things that reside

01:05:27.420 --> 01:05:29.070
at that level of control.

01:05:29.070 --> 01:05:33.870
That is, the things that we can
do and think and say and access

01:05:33.870 --> 01:05:36.870
don't come from external things.

01:05:36.870 --> 01:05:40.380
They don't come necessarily
from the acquisition of material

01:05:40.380 --> 01:05:42.900
goods, but rather
there are things

01:05:42.900 --> 01:05:47.400
that we can do that can allow
us to so-called synthesize

01:05:47.400 --> 01:05:48.040
happiness.

01:05:48.040 --> 01:05:50.010
And I think this is
one of the great gifts

01:05:50.010 --> 01:05:52.950
of modern psychology is that--

01:05:52.950 --> 01:05:55.620
Dan Gilbert and others, the
Harvard happiness project,

01:05:55.620 --> 01:05:57.690
work at Yale and elsewhere.

01:05:57.690 --> 01:06:01.380
There are excellent labs working
on happiness all over the US

01:06:01.380 --> 01:06:03.580
and all over the world, frankly.

01:06:03.580 --> 01:06:05.550
One of the great gifts
that they've supplied us

01:06:05.550 --> 01:06:07.920
in the form of data
is that there really

01:06:07.920 --> 01:06:11.070
are things that we can all
do and think and access

01:06:11.070 --> 01:06:14.400
to allow ourselves to
so-called synthesize happiness.

01:06:14.400 --> 01:06:17.190
Now, this notion of
synthesizing happiness,

01:06:17.190 --> 01:06:20.070
or synthetic happiness
as it's sometimes called,

01:06:20.070 --> 01:06:22.200
can sometimes ruffle
people's feathers a bit.

01:06:22.200 --> 01:06:25.620
Because people immediately
flip to the idea

01:06:25.620 --> 01:06:27.270
that, oh, you're
just going to tell

01:06:27.270 --> 01:06:30.720
me to be grateful for what
I have or to just navel

01:06:30.720 --> 01:06:32.763
gaze or just to
imagine that I'm happy.

01:06:32.763 --> 01:06:34.680
But that's really not
what synthetic happiness

01:06:34.680 --> 01:06:35.587
is about at all.

01:06:35.587 --> 01:06:37.170
Synthetic happiness
actually has to do

01:06:37.170 --> 01:06:41.250
with some really important
larger principles about the way

01:06:41.250 --> 01:06:43.980
that our emotional
system and the way

01:06:43.980 --> 01:06:48.330
that the reward systems of
our brain really function.

01:06:48.330 --> 01:06:50.550
And they point to
important concepts

01:06:50.550 --> 01:06:52.260
that we're going to now discuss.

01:06:52.260 --> 01:06:55.270
Things like the hedonic
set point, for instance.

01:06:55.270 --> 01:06:57.960
Or the dopamine
system of anticipation

01:06:57.960 --> 01:07:01.350
of rewards versus receiving
words just as a brief insight

01:07:01.350 --> 01:07:02.550
into that.

01:07:02.550 --> 01:07:05.190
Our anticipation of
something positive

01:07:05.190 --> 01:07:08.100
oftentimes leads to
greater increases

01:07:08.100 --> 01:07:09.870
in the sorts of
neurochemicals that

01:07:09.870 --> 01:07:12.390
support a state of
happiness and well-being

01:07:12.390 --> 01:07:15.780
then the actual
acquisition of the thing

01:07:15.780 --> 01:07:17.040
that we're trying to obtain.

01:07:17.040 --> 01:07:18.457
And this goes back
to a theme I've

01:07:18.457 --> 01:07:21.040
discussed a few times
before in this podcast,

01:07:21.040 --> 01:07:23.820
in particular with my colleague
at Stanford School of Medicine,

01:07:23.820 --> 01:07:26.940
Dr. Anna Lembke, who wrote the
fabulous book Dopamine Nation.

01:07:26.940 --> 01:07:29.160
If you're interested in
dopamine and addiction,

01:07:29.160 --> 01:07:32.100
in particular, that's
a wonderful clear,

01:07:32.100 --> 01:07:34.830
and extremely informative read.

01:07:34.830 --> 01:07:36.750
And if you're interested
in dopamine more

01:07:36.750 --> 01:07:39.580
generally, not just in
the states of addiction

01:07:39.580 --> 01:07:42.390
but in everyday life and
in pursuit and motivation,

01:07:42.390 --> 01:07:45.432
The Molecule of More is an
excellent book related to that.

01:07:45.432 --> 01:07:46.890
And as I mentioned
earlier, we have

01:07:46.890 --> 01:07:49.680
this episode on dopamine
motivation and drive.

01:07:49.680 --> 01:07:53.700
The notion of
synthetic happiness

01:07:53.700 --> 01:07:56.760
is not simply about
imagining happiness,

01:07:56.760 --> 01:07:59.310
or thinking about happiness
or anticipating happiness.

01:07:59.310 --> 01:08:01.950
To some extent, it is,
but it relates to a number

01:08:01.950 --> 01:08:03.330
of other important themes.

01:08:03.330 --> 01:08:05.670
But it is grounded
very thoroughly

01:08:05.670 --> 01:08:09.030
in the neurobiology
of dopamine rewards.

01:08:09.030 --> 01:08:12.070
And I'll talk about some of that
neurobiology in a few moments.

01:08:12.070 --> 01:08:14.700
But I want to take
a couple of minutes

01:08:14.700 --> 01:08:16.920
and talk about what
synthetic happiness is

01:08:16.920 --> 01:08:18.660
and what some of
the conditions are

01:08:18.660 --> 01:08:20.729
for allowing us to
access the state

01:08:20.729 --> 01:08:22.479
of so-called
synthetic happiness.

01:08:22.479 --> 01:08:24.600
And I want to point
out at the outset

01:08:24.600 --> 01:08:28.979
that synthetic happiness, while
it might sound synthetic A.K.A.

01:08:28.979 --> 01:08:31.080
false, it's anything but.

01:08:31.080 --> 01:08:33.990
It actually turns out to be
among the more and perhaps

01:08:33.990 --> 01:08:35.850
the more potent
form of happiness

01:08:35.850 --> 01:08:37.109
that we can all access.

01:08:37.109 --> 01:08:41.069
And this is where themes
related to our control

01:08:41.069 --> 01:08:43.170
over our own
internal state really

01:08:43.170 --> 01:08:46.010
become not only valid
but very powerful.

01:08:46.010 --> 01:08:50.380
So, for instance, Dr.
Dan Gilbert and others

01:08:50.380 --> 01:08:55.420
have explored how opportunity
and choice, that is, freedom

01:08:55.420 --> 01:08:57.729
can and can't lead to
states of happiness.

01:08:57.729 --> 01:09:01.640
And the results of those studies
are very solid and, frankly,

01:09:01.640 --> 01:09:02.950
very surprising.

01:09:02.950 --> 01:09:05.830
Until you understand the
results, and once you do,

01:09:05.830 --> 01:09:08.800
I think you will immediately
see areas of your own life

01:09:08.800 --> 01:09:11.800
that you can start to
access more happiness, again

01:09:11.800 --> 01:09:14.620
genuine happiness,
simply by framing

01:09:14.620 --> 01:09:16.359
certain choices in
a particular way

01:09:16.359 --> 01:09:18.729
and maybe even by
eliminating choices.

01:09:18.729 --> 01:09:20.649
Now I'd like to focus
on the research aimed

01:09:20.649 --> 01:09:24.250
at understanding what increases
our levels of happiness.

01:09:24.250 --> 01:09:27.010
And I'd like to frame this
under the umbrella of two

01:09:27.010 --> 01:09:27.830
major themes.

01:09:27.830 --> 01:09:30.729
The first theme is
so-called natural happiness.

01:09:30.729 --> 01:09:33.430
Natural happiness is the sort
of happiness that most of us

01:09:33.430 --> 01:09:34.760
are familiar with.

01:09:34.760 --> 01:09:36.700
So the kind of
happiness that we expect

01:09:36.700 --> 01:09:40.479
to have if we, for
instance, complete a degree.

01:09:40.479 --> 01:09:43.149
Hopefully, a degree in a topic
meaningful and interesting

01:09:43.149 --> 01:09:45.430
to us but a degree nonetheless.

01:09:45.430 --> 01:09:47.590
Or we find a mate.

01:09:47.590 --> 01:09:49.990
Hopefully, a mate that we
enjoy spending time with.

01:09:49.990 --> 01:09:53.020
Or, for instance, making a
certain income or finding

01:09:53.020 --> 01:09:55.450
work that we enjoy
on a regular basis.

01:09:55.450 --> 01:09:56.980
All of those are
forms of happiness

01:09:56.980 --> 01:09:59.440
that, from a very early
time in development,

01:09:59.440 --> 01:10:01.830
we are taught exist.

01:10:01.830 --> 01:10:03.580
For instance, even
when we are very young,

01:10:03.580 --> 01:10:05.230
we are told that our
birthday is coming

01:10:05.230 --> 01:10:06.550
and that we are going
to get presents,

01:10:06.550 --> 01:10:07.967
and those presents
are going to be

01:10:07.967 --> 01:10:11.012
focused on knowledge of
things that we already enjoy.

01:10:11.012 --> 01:10:12.970
So if you're a little
kid, and you like trucks,

01:10:12.970 --> 01:10:15.100
or you're a little kid
and you like dolls,

01:10:15.100 --> 01:10:17.920
you can sort of expect that
those gifts will bring you

01:10:17.920 --> 01:10:19.690
some level of joy or happiness.

01:10:19.690 --> 01:10:22.540
And while that's a
small child example,

01:10:22.540 --> 01:10:24.850
that general notion
of natural happiness

01:10:24.850 --> 01:10:28.960
is, of course, one that
persists into adolescence,

01:10:28.960 --> 01:10:31.580
into young adulthood,
and into adulthood.

01:10:31.580 --> 01:10:35.020
And we quite understandably
come to associate

01:10:35.020 --> 01:10:39.280
this feeling of joy or happiness
with the receiving of things

01:10:39.280 --> 01:10:41.830
or the acquisition of
things, whether by effort,

01:10:41.830 --> 01:10:44.390
by gift, by inheritance,
or some other form.

01:10:44.390 --> 01:10:45.680
So that's natural happiness.

01:10:45.680 --> 01:10:48.520
And yet, as I mentioned
a little bit earlier,

01:10:48.520 --> 01:10:51.250
there's also this notion
of synthetic happiness.

01:10:51.250 --> 01:10:53.800
And some of the more interesting
and exciting research

01:10:53.800 --> 01:10:56.470
in the fields of psychology
and, in fact, neuroscience

01:10:56.470 --> 01:10:59.800
point to this idea of
synthetic happiness

01:10:59.800 --> 01:11:03.700
as at least as powerful
a source of happiness

01:11:03.700 --> 01:11:05.380
as natural happiness.

01:11:05.380 --> 01:11:09.250
Again, at least as powerful
and perhaps even more powerful.

01:11:09.250 --> 01:11:12.340
And of course, one has to take a
slightly different view of what

01:11:12.340 --> 01:11:15.430
happiness is in order to accept
this idea that we can create

01:11:15.430 --> 01:11:19.090
happiness for ourselves,
but that doesn't mean

01:11:19.090 --> 01:11:21.370
that the whole notion
of synthetic happiness

01:11:21.370 --> 01:11:23.230
is merely a passive
one where all we

01:11:23.230 --> 01:11:26.710
do is sit back and imagine being
happy and then we are happy.

01:11:26.710 --> 01:11:28.930
For better or for worse,
our nervous systems

01:11:28.930 --> 01:11:32.210
and our neurochemistry
simply don't work that way.

01:11:32.210 --> 01:11:34.600
In fact, synthetic
happiness has almost always

01:11:34.600 --> 01:11:36.430
been understood as
something that we

01:11:36.430 --> 01:11:38.980
have to put some effort
toward achieving.

01:11:38.980 --> 01:11:42.280
But, and this is an
important thing to point out,

01:11:42.280 --> 01:11:44.710
synthetic happiness
also requires

01:11:44.710 --> 01:11:48.520
that certain situational
or environmental conditions

01:11:48.520 --> 01:11:49.990
be met.

01:11:49.990 --> 01:11:53.590
A good example of this is some
of the work by Gillian Mandich,

01:11:53.590 --> 01:11:55.930
or I should say Dr.
Gillian Mandich, who's

01:11:55.930 --> 01:11:59.230
done some interesting work on
the conditions for creating

01:11:59.230 --> 01:12:03.370
happiness within our mind and
in our overall state of being.

01:12:03.370 --> 01:12:06.250
And she's been involved in a
number of different studies.

01:12:06.250 --> 01:12:08.890
But one of the ones that I
found particularly interesting

01:12:08.890 --> 01:12:13.030
is one in which they explored
different types of music

01:12:13.030 --> 01:12:15.430
and other aspects of
environmental settings.

01:12:15.430 --> 01:12:17.260
So you bring subjects
to the laboratory

01:12:17.260 --> 01:12:18.850
play them different
types of music.

01:12:18.850 --> 01:12:22.120
There are, in fact,
certain aspects of music

01:12:22.120 --> 01:12:24.950
that can create different
states of mind sadness,

01:12:24.950 --> 01:12:26.830
happiness, anticipation.

01:12:26.830 --> 01:12:28.930
In fact, there are
certain patterns of music

01:12:28.930 --> 01:12:32.770
that can reliably induce
anticipation of the fear

01:12:32.770 --> 01:12:34.360
and anxiety base type.

01:12:34.360 --> 01:12:37.120
So, for instance,
think the movie Jaws.

01:12:37.120 --> 01:12:39.460
If you recall, for those
of you who have seen Jaws,

01:12:39.460 --> 01:12:42.790
there's this ongoing
theme music any time

01:12:42.790 --> 01:12:46.690
the shark might be present in
the water or in a given scene.

01:12:46.690 --> 01:12:49.300
That essentially goes [HUMMING]

01:12:49.300 --> 01:12:51.100
Now for the musicians
out there, this

01:12:51.100 --> 01:12:53.350
has basis in things
like tritones and things

01:12:53.350 --> 01:12:55.420
that are understood
from the mathematics

01:12:55.420 --> 01:12:57.100
and the musical side.

01:12:57.100 --> 01:12:58.810
And from the
neuroscience side, are

01:12:58.810 --> 01:13:02.860
known to create a neural
state of anticipation.

01:13:02.860 --> 01:13:04.840
Yeah, a neural state
of anticipation

01:13:04.840 --> 01:13:06.880
and not necessarily
a positive one.

01:13:06.880 --> 01:13:10.120
And indeed, there are
other patterns of music

01:13:10.120 --> 01:13:12.670
that involve up tones.

01:13:12.670 --> 01:13:16.750
Think some of the music
that's typically been used

01:13:16.750 --> 01:13:19.210
in cartoons of various sorts.

01:13:19.210 --> 01:13:20.590
There's a long history of this.

01:13:20.590 --> 01:13:22.180
Indeed there's a
whole literature

01:13:22.180 --> 01:13:26.410
of psychological and now even
a smaller but still interesting

01:13:26.410 --> 01:13:28.570
literature on the
neuroscience of how

01:13:28.570 --> 01:13:30.820
certain patterns
of music can induce

01:13:30.820 --> 01:13:34.210
a state of joy and joyful
anticipation, in particular.

01:13:34.210 --> 01:13:36.760
A lot of those patterns
of music are incorporated

01:13:36.760 --> 01:13:39.760
into so-called happy
cartoons and Disney movies

01:13:39.760 --> 01:13:41.230
and things of that sort.

01:13:41.230 --> 01:13:46.290
In any case, Dr. Mandich and
others have explored how music,

01:13:46.290 --> 01:13:49.590
in particular, but other
features of the environment can

01:13:49.590 --> 01:13:51.210
or cannot induce
states of happiness.

01:13:51.210 --> 01:13:53.160
And the basic takeaway
from those studies

01:13:53.160 --> 01:13:58.890
is that while having a
certain environmental sound,

01:13:58.890 --> 01:14:05.130
musical tone, or visual feature
to a given space, a room,

01:14:05.130 --> 01:14:08.080
is necessary for a
state of happiness,

01:14:08.080 --> 01:14:09.750
it is not alone sufficient.

01:14:09.750 --> 01:14:13.500
What is required is that
individuals not only

01:14:13.500 --> 01:14:15.300
be placed into an
environment that

01:14:15.300 --> 01:14:20.220
contains music or visual items
or a combination of music

01:14:20.220 --> 01:14:22.620
and visual items that
can induce states

01:14:22.620 --> 01:14:25.560
of joy or happiness or
positive anticipation,

01:14:25.560 --> 01:14:29.550
but that they also are given
some sort of instruction

01:14:29.550 --> 01:14:33.180
or instruction manual as
how to synthesize happiness

01:14:33.180 --> 01:14:35.650
inside of that environment.

01:14:35.650 --> 01:14:37.530
This is important
because what this

01:14:37.530 --> 01:14:40.950
says is that our ability to
create states of happiness

01:14:40.950 --> 01:14:44.610
is dependent on our environment
but also requires effort

01:14:44.610 --> 01:14:46.080
from us.

01:14:46.080 --> 01:14:49.320
That also makes sense as to why
when we are under conditions

01:14:49.320 --> 01:14:50.315
of deprivation.

01:14:50.315 --> 01:14:51.690
So it could be
social deprivation

01:14:51.690 --> 01:14:53.280
or financial deprivation.

01:14:53.280 --> 01:14:56.310
Or even for people that are
very sensitive to whether.

01:14:56.310 --> 01:14:59.550
There are a certain number
of individuals, about 30%

01:14:59.550 --> 01:15:03.300
of people, who report feeling
very, very low under conditions

01:15:03.300 --> 01:15:05.550
where the sky is overcast.

01:15:05.550 --> 01:15:07.890
Especially if it's been
overcast for a number of days.

01:15:07.890 --> 01:15:10.770
The so-called seasonal
affective depression.

01:15:10.770 --> 01:15:12.960
Those individuals,
by the way, can often

01:15:12.960 --> 01:15:14.820
receive tremendous
benefits in terms

01:15:14.820 --> 01:15:17.470
of elevating their mood if they
make an effort to get sunlight.

01:15:17.470 --> 01:15:19.970
And if they can't get sunlight,
artificial light of the sort

01:15:19.970 --> 01:15:21.490
that we talked about earlier.

01:15:21.490 --> 01:15:24.360
But in any case,
there are a number

01:15:24.360 --> 01:15:28.380
of people that are profoundly
negatively influenced

01:15:28.380 --> 01:15:33.060
by the lack of positive
visual and auditory cues

01:15:33.060 --> 01:15:34.050
in their environment.

01:15:34.050 --> 01:15:37.380
But for most people,
we are in what

01:15:37.380 --> 01:15:39.270
I would call a
dynamic relationship

01:15:39.270 --> 01:15:40.400
with our environment.

01:15:40.400 --> 01:15:42.150
Our environment has
an effect on our mood.

01:15:42.150 --> 01:15:44.130
But the research
indicates that we also

01:15:44.130 --> 01:15:47.460
need to make some sort of
effort toward being happy.

01:15:47.460 --> 01:15:50.890
Now effort toward being
happy is a very vague term.

01:15:50.890 --> 01:15:53.010
So let's better
define what that is.

01:15:53.010 --> 01:15:55.530
In the case of Dr.
Mandich's work,

01:15:55.530 --> 01:15:58.290
this took the form of
doing so-called happiness

01:15:58.290 --> 01:16:00.000
inventories, right.

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:03.270
That can be focusing on things
that one is grateful for,

01:16:03.270 --> 01:16:05.850
things that they
particularly enjoy.

01:16:05.850 --> 01:16:09.390
This is somewhat of a
gratitude-type practice

01:16:09.390 --> 01:16:11.220
but includes some
other features as well

01:16:11.220 --> 01:16:12.930
that are more focused
on the things that

01:16:12.930 --> 01:16:15.372
bring you meaning and actually
engaging in the things

01:16:15.372 --> 01:16:16.330
that bring you meaning.

01:16:16.330 --> 01:16:17.913
So if you're trying
to think about how

01:16:17.913 --> 01:16:20.370
to improve your levels
of happiness, what

01:16:20.370 --> 01:16:23.340
this research essentially
says is that you would

01:16:23.340 --> 01:16:26.640
be smart to try and adjust
your home environment,

01:16:26.640 --> 01:16:29.288
adjust your work environment
so that it is cheerful to you.

01:16:29.288 --> 01:16:30.330
Maybe that means a plant.

01:16:30.330 --> 01:16:33.180
For me, in my laboratory,
one of the things

01:16:33.180 --> 01:16:35.400
that was really critical
that I had as a postdoc

01:16:35.400 --> 01:16:40.110
and in my own laboratory when
I first started my lab was I

01:16:40.110 --> 01:16:40.950
love aquaria.

01:16:40.950 --> 01:16:42.360
So I had multiple fish tanks.

01:16:42.360 --> 01:16:44.370
In fact, people in my laboratory
were always rolling their eyes.

01:16:44.370 --> 01:16:46.870
Why do we have to have all these
fish tanks with all these--

01:16:46.870 --> 01:16:50.022
I like freshwater tanks,
not saltwater tanks,

01:16:50.022 --> 01:16:51.480
for reasons that
aren't interesting

01:16:51.480 --> 01:16:52.313
for this discussion.

01:16:52.313 --> 01:16:56.268
But freshwater tanks with discus
fish, for instance, to me,

01:16:56.268 --> 01:16:57.060
are just beautiful.

01:16:57.060 --> 01:16:57.910
They make me happy.

01:16:57.910 --> 01:16:59.370
I just enjoy them.

01:16:59.370 --> 01:17:01.260
Music is a complicated
thing in laboratories

01:17:01.260 --> 01:17:02.560
because it's a shared space.

01:17:02.560 --> 01:17:05.040
So headphones are the
general requirement.

01:17:05.040 --> 01:17:07.945
But having either silence
if you love silence.

01:17:07.945 --> 01:17:09.570
And I happen to like
working in silence

01:17:09.570 --> 01:17:11.195
or listening to
certain forms of music.

01:17:11.195 --> 01:17:14.580
I do also use the 40
Hertz binaural beats.

01:17:14.580 --> 01:17:19.320
Or I particularly like listening
to Glenn Gould while I work

01:17:19.320 --> 01:17:21.340
or listening to whale
song, believe it or not,

01:17:21.340 --> 01:17:23.040
while I work because it doesn't
have any structure that I

01:17:23.040 --> 01:17:23.740
can follow.

01:17:23.740 --> 01:17:25.920
I don't speak whale,
and so I can't follow.

01:17:25.920 --> 01:17:28.530
But it sort of fills the space
in a way that I find pleasant.

01:17:28.530 --> 01:17:30.780
And I've put substantial
amounts of effort

01:17:30.780 --> 01:17:33.690
into making my laboratory
spaces and my office

01:17:33.690 --> 01:17:37.200
spaces, my workspaces,
nice places to be.

01:17:37.200 --> 01:17:39.180
Now I had no
knowledge of this work

01:17:39.180 --> 01:17:42.092
from Dr. Mandich and others
at the time when I did that.

01:17:42.092 --> 01:17:43.800
But what I found was
that over the years,

01:17:43.800 --> 01:17:48.117
I was challenged in maintaining
a kind of elevated mood

01:17:48.117 --> 01:17:50.700
while working in a laboratory,
not because I didn't thoroughly

01:17:50.700 --> 01:17:52.992
enjoy the work, I love doing
experiments with my hands,

01:17:52.992 --> 01:17:55.988
and I loved being in lab, but
at least the labs that I was

01:17:55.988 --> 01:17:57.780
in as a graduate student
and post-doc there

01:17:57.780 --> 01:18:00.390
were no windows so I wasn't
getting adequate sunshine.

01:18:00.390 --> 01:18:02.910
The windows that we
didn't open, so I

01:18:02.910 --> 01:18:05.590
wasn't getting a lot of fresh
air, and so on and so forth.

01:18:05.590 --> 01:18:07.680
So I've personally
found it very valuable

01:18:07.680 --> 01:18:10.770
to create an environment
both at work and at home

01:18:10.770 --> 01:18:14.660
that I find aesthetically
pleasant, at least in some way

01:18:14.660 --> 01:18:15.160
or another.

01:18:15.160 --> 01:18:16.827
And I realize people
have varying levels

01:18:16.827 --> 01:18:19.740
of control over their
aesthetic environment.

01:18:19.740 --> 01:18:21.457
Certainly, the
auditory environment

01:18:21.457 --> 01:18:23.790
can be controlled nowadays
through the use of headphones

01:18:23.790 --> 01:18:25.082
if you're allowed to use those.

01:18:25.082 --> 01:18:28.380
So, for instance, using music
or using background sound

01:18:28.380 --> 01:18:30.930
that you find very
pleasant combined

01:18:30.930 --> 01:18:33.450
with a concerted
effort on your part

01:18:33.450 --> 01:18:38.940
to create states of happiness
by hopefully doing work that's

01:18:38.940 --> 01:18:42.340
meaningful to you or at least is
leading to meaningful outcomes.

01:18:42.340 --> 01:18:44.380
We'll talk a little
bit more about that.

01:18:44.380 --> 01:18:46.530
But these happiness
inventories also

01:18:46.530 --> 01:18:49.740
turn out to be interesting and
important sources of creating

01:18:49.740 --> 01:18:51.300
so-called synthetic happiness.

01:18:51.300 --> 01:18:52.800
And we'll also talk
about other ways

01:18:52.800 --> 01:18:55.830
that one can create elevated
levels of synthetic happiness.

01:18:55.830 --> 01:18:57.600
And I realize the word
synthetic probably

01:18:57.600 --> 01:19:00.420
draws up connotations
of false happiness

01:19:00.420 --> 01:19:01.770
or contrived happiness.

01:19:01.770 --> 01:19:03.900
I wish instead of calling
it synthetic happiness.

01:19:03.900 --> 01:19:06.665
They had called it
self-created or self-directed

01:19:06.665 --> 01:19:08.790
happiness or something of
that sort because then it

01:19:08.790 --> 01:19:10.560
wouldn't sound as false.

01:19:10.560 --> 01:19:13.050
Because it's simply not false.

01:19:13.050 --> 01:19:15.480
It leads to the
same, as far as we

01:19:15.480 --> 01:19:17.730
know, identical neurochemical
and psychological states

01:19:17.730 --> 01:19:19.147
of happiness as
natural happiness.

01:19:19.147 --> 01:19:21.090
And might even be
more persistent

01:19:21.090 --> 01:19:22.440
than natural happiness.

01:19:22.440 --> 01:19:24.630
It certainly, is more
under our control.

01:19:24.630 --> 01:19:26.430
But the key point
is that environment

01:19:26.430 --> 01:19:31.560
and self-directed work at
being happy are both important,

01:19:31.560 --> 01:19:33.400
and they interact
with one another.

01:19:33.400 --> 01:19:36.000
So if you're somebody who
has a hard time synthesizing

01:19:36.000 --> 01:19:38.160
happiness through
any of the methods

01:19:38.160 --> 01:19:42.353
that we talk about today, don't
consider yourself deficient.

01:19:42.353 --> 01:19:44.770
It could very well be that the
environment that you're in,

01:19:44.770 --> 01:19:46.562
social environment or
physical environment,

01:19:46.562 --> 01:19:48.250
or auditory environment,
is simply not

01:19:48.250 --> 01:19:51.118
conducive to
synthesizing happiness.

01:19:51.118 --> 01:19:52.660
And for that reason,
I think the work

01:19:52.660 --> 01:19:55.120
of Gillian Mandich and
colleagues and others

01:19:55.120 --> 01:19:57.910
in the field is tremendously
important because it removes us

01:19:57.910 --> 01:20:01.120
from this pressure to
just synthesize happiness

01:20:01.120 --> 01:20:03.340
from within despite
our circumstances.

01:20:03.340 --> 01:20:06.820
I think many of us have heard
of the incredible stories

01:20:06.820 --> 01:20:10.120
of people like Viktor
Frankl or Nelson Mandela who

01:20:10.120 --> 01:20:12.280
were stripped of
their freedom and yet

01:20:12.280 --> 01:20:15.160
managed to maintain some
sense of positive anticipation

01:20:15.160 --> 01:20:18.460
or at least some
sense of identity

01:20:18.460 --> 01:20:21.490
that allowed them to still
access forms of happiness.

01:20:21.490 --> 01:20:23.620
Those are highly unique
situations, of course.

01:20:23.620 --> 01:20:26.920
And they speak to the
power of the human psyche

01:20:26.920 --> 01:20:29.860
for synthesizing happiness
and certainly for synthesizing

01:20:29.860 --> 01:20:32.620
a sense that there might
be a future and to live

01:20:32.620 --> 01:20:33.670
into that future.

01:20:33.670 --> 01:20:36.228
In their cases, incredibly
impressive ways.

01:20:36.228 --> 01:20:38.770
But I think for most everybody,
the environment that we're in

01:20:38.770 --> 01:20:40.930
has a powerful
impact on our mood.

01:20:40.930 --> 01:20:43.337
And some people
more than others.

01:20:43.337 --> 01:20:45.670
I know people that are perfectly
happy with blank walls.

01:20:45.670 --> 01:20:46.712
No pictures on the walls.

01:20:46.712 --> 01:20:48.460
Other people
benefit tremendously

01:20:48.460 --> 01:20:51.800
from having photos or plants
in their environment and so on.

01:20:51.800 --> 01:20:54.160
You really have to determine
what's needed for you

01:20:54.160 --> 01:20:55.552
and do your best
to try and place

01:20:55.552 --> 01:20:57.010
those things into
your environment,

01:20:57.010 --> 01:20:59.260
or rather, place yourself
into an environment that

01:20:59.260 --> 01:21:02.420
is conducive to you
synthesizing your happiness.

01:21:02.420 --> 01:21:04.180
In fact, the
powerful interaction

01:21:04.180 --> 01:21:06.820
between our environment
and our own ability

01:21:06.820 --> 01:21:09.850
to generate certain
kinds of emotions

01:21:09.850 --> 01:21:11.810
is well established
not just for happiness

01:21:11.810 --> 01:21:13.060
but for things like gratitude.

01:21:13.060 --> 01:21:16.450
So, for instance, there's a
classic study from AMES A-M-E-S

01:21:16.450 --> 01:21:20.590
in 2004 that was
focused on gratitude.

01:21:20.590 --> 01:21:23.230
And we've had an episode
on gratitude before.

01:21:23.230 --> 01:21:24.820
The basic takeaway
of that episode

01:21:24.820 --> 01:21:28.270
is that it turns out
receiving gratitude

01:21:28.270 --> 01:21:33.010
is a more powerful
stimulus for the release

01:21:33.010 --> 01:21:35.680
of neurochemicals and activation
of brain areas associated

01:21:35.680 --> 01:21:37.240
with so-called
prosocial behaviors

01:21:37.240 --> 01:21:40.300
and feelings of well-being,
including happiness.

01:21:40.300 --> 01:21:44.860
But also observing stories in
the form of movies or books

01:21:44.860 --> 01:21:47.140
or other narratives of
other people receiving help

01:21:47.140 --> 01:21:49.570
is also a very powerful
stimulus for gratitude.

01:21:49.570 --> 01:21:52.930
Also, giving gratitude
is very powerful but not

01:21:52.930 --> 01:21:55.060
as powerful as receiving
gratitude, at least

01:21:55.060 --> 01:21:57.010
that's what the research
says, or observing

01:21:57.010 --> 01:22:00.370
powerful exchanges of gratitude
between other individuals.

01:22:00.370 --> 01:22:02.260
What the study
from AMES showed is

01:22:02.260 --> 01:22:06.550
that gratitude as a state
of mind and as an emotion

01:22:06.550 --> 01:22:08.390
does not exist in a vacuum.

01:22:08.390 --> 01:22:11.602
It's not independent
of our surroundings.

01:22:11.602 --> 01:22:13.060
So, for instance,
just writing down

01:22:13.060 --> 01:22:14.852
all the things you're
grateful for while it

01:22:14.852 --> 01:22:17.440
has some positive impact,
the impact of that

01:22:17.440 --> 01:22:19.960
or receiving gratitude
or observing gratitude

01:22:19.960 --> 01:22:22.420
is far more potent, right.

01:22:22.420 --> 01:22:24.940
Bigger increases in
happiness and feelings

01:22:24.940 --> 01:22:27.190
of well-being and indeed
neurochemicals and activation

01:22:27.190 --> 01:22:30.160
of brain areas associated
with happiness and well-being

01:22:30.160 --> 01:22:32.020
when there's a reciprocity.

01:22:32.020 --> 01:22:35.147
When the person receiving
understands something

01:22:35.147 --> 01:22:36.730
about the person
that's giving to them

01:22:36.730 --> 01:22:38.897
and understands that the
person is giving genuinely,

01:22:38.897 --> 01:22:39.439
for instance.

01:22:39.439 --> 01:22:41.105
So there's an
environmental interaction.

01:22:41.105 --> 01:22:42.700
It's not just about
receiving, it's

01:22:42.700 --> 01:22:46.210
receiving from somebody that you
know genuinely wants to give.

01:22:46.210 --> 01:22:49.450
And likewise, for the
giver in that equation,

01:22:49.450 --> 01:22:51.970
the feelings of
well-being are far greater

01:22:51.970 --> 01:22:54.550
when the person
receiving whatever

01:22:54.550 --> 01:22:58.390
it is money, food, assistance
in some form or another.

01:22:58.390 --> 01:23:00.970
Could be physical
assistance, et cetera.

01:23:00.970 --> 01:23:04.030
When the giver has knowledge
that the person receiving it

01:23:04.030 --> 01:23:06.187
genuinely needed the thing
that they are receiving.

01:23:06.187 --> 01:23:08.770
So the important finding within
the research, again and again,

01:23:08.770 --> 01:23:12.020
is that happiness doesn't
exist in a vacuum.

01:23:12.020 --> 01:23:13.990
It's partially our
own responsibility

01:23:13.990 --> 01:23:14.990
to synthesize happiness.

01:23:14.990 --> 01:23:17.407
You know, I was told that many
times you're like happiness

01:23:17.407 --> 01:23:18.100
is in your head.

01:23:18.100 --> 01:23:20.080
Well, yes, indeed,
it's in your head,

01:23:20.080 --> 01:23:23.500
but it's also dependent
on interactions

01:23:23.500 --> 01:23:24.440
with your environment.

01:23:24.440 --> 01:23:27.140
Physical environment and
social environment, and so on.

01:23:27.140 --> 01:23:31.210
Likewise, gratitude is something
that we can create inside of us

01:23:31.210 --> 01:23:33.280
right through gratitude
lists and appreciation,

01:23:33.280 --> 01:23:36.190
or we can give both
powerful sources

01:23:36.190 --> 01:23:40.360
of evoking neurochemical changes
associated with gratitude

01:23:40.360 --> 01:23:42.340
and happiness and well-being.

01:23:42.340 --> 01:23:45.230
But it, too, doesn't
exist in a vacuum.

01:23:45.230 --> 01:23:47.560
There's a much greater
positive effect

01:23:47.560 --> 01:23:51.557
when we have knowledge about
why the giver is giving us

01:23:51.557 --> 01:23:53.140
something or that
the person receiving

01:23:53.140 --> 01:23:56.210
something is going to benefit
tremendously from receiving it.

01:23:56.210 --> 01:23:58.660
So I'm highlighting
this because I

01:23:58.660 --> 01:24:01.570
think that when we hear
about synthetic happiness,

01:24:01.570 --> 01:24:05.650
there's a kind of automatic
erasing of context

01:24:05.650 --> 01:24:06.850
that tends to occur.

01:24:06.850 --> 01:24:10.600
And in fact, if you were to
peruse the various videos

01:24:10.600 --> 01:24:14.680
online or papers that exist
on PubMed around happiness

01:24:14.680 --> 01:24:16.450
and synthetic happiness,
in particular,

01:24:16.450 --> 01:24:18.330
you would come away
with the impression

01:24:18.330 --> 01:24:20.080
that synthetic happiness
is just something

01:24:20.080 --> 01:24:22.210
that we're supposed to
snap our fingers in access

01:24:22.210 --> 01:24:24.880
or perhaps do very
specific things in access.

01:24:24.880 --> 01:24:28.760
But while that is true,
context really matters.

01:24:28.760 --> 01:24:30.370
And I think that's
an important point.

01:24:30.370 --> 01:24:33.160
Much in the same way
that the point needs

01:24:33.160 --> 01:24:36.280
to be made that while money
doesn't buy happiness,

01:24:36.280 --> 01:24:38.350
money can buffer stress
and certainly offer

01:24:38.350 --> 01:24:41.090
opportunities that can
provide opportunities

01:24:41.090 --> 01:24:42.180
for more happiness.

01:24:42.180 --> 01:24:45.150
So I think we are starting to
arrive at a general theme here,

01:24:45.150 --> 01:24:49.130
which is that nothing related
to our mood exists in isolation.

01:24:49.130 --> 01:24:51.350
And in fact, that leads
me to a discussion

01:24:51.350 --> 01:24:54.770
of one of the major
scientific findings

01:24:54.770 --> 01:24:58.310
in the realm of what sorts
of mindsets and behaviors

01:24:58.310 --> 01:25:00.110
can, in fact, lead to happiness.

01:25:00.110 --> 01:25:02.705
And this is a paper that
was published in 2008.

01:25:02.705 --> 01:25:04.580
Even though that might
seem like a while ago,

01:25:04.580 --> 01:25:07.100
it forms the basis for a
large amount of literature

01:25:07.100 --> 01:25:07.892
that followed.

01:25:07.892 --> 01:25:09.350
It's a very
interesting literature.

01:25:09.350 --> 01:25:13.040
This is work from Elizabeth
Dunn and colleagues

01:25:13.040 --> 01:25:14.970
and was published in
the Journal Science.

01:25:14.970 --> 01:25:18.110
Which, again, is one of the sort
of three apex journals, Nature,

01:25:18.110 --> 01:25:21.735
Science, Cell, I always say
is sort of the Super Bowl, NBA

01:25:21.735 --> 01:25:24.110
championships, and Stanley
Cup of scientific publishing--

01:25:24.110 --> 01:25:27.320
very, very stringent in
terms of the number of papers

01:25:27.320 --> 01:25:28.010
they let in.

01:25:28.010 --> 01:25:29.000
Very few, that is.

01:25:29.000 --> 01:25:32.000
And the title of this
paper makes fairly obvious

01:25:32.000 --> 01:25:33.210
what the paper is about.

01:25:33.210 --> 01:25:35.540
The title of the paper
is "spending money

01:25:35.540 --> 01:25:37.490
on others promotes happiness".

01:25:37.490 --> 01:25:40.010
And I know a number of you
probably hear that title

01:25:40.010 --> 01:25:41.390
and think, oh boy, here we go.

01:25:41.390 --> 01:25:44.000
He's going to tell us that
giving away all our money

01:25:44.000 --> 01:25:46.443
is going to make us happier
than receiving money.

01:25:46.443 --> 01:25:48.860
And I promise you, that is not
what I'm going to tell you.

01:25:48.860 --> 01:25:51.080
But nonetheless, this is
a very interesting study,

01:25:51.080 --> 01:25:52.747
and it's one that I
think that we really

01:25:52.747 --> 01:25:54.410
ought to pay attention to.

01:25:54.410 --> 01:25:56.780
Because what the
study is based on

01:25:56.780 --> 01:26:01.040
is the fact that income,
provided one's income

01:26:01.040 --> 01:26:03.470
meets a certain
level of basic needs,

01:26:03.470 --> 01:26:07.760
indeed has been shown to
have only a weak effect

01:26:07.760 --> 01:26:10.110
on overall happiness.

01:26:10.110 --> 01:26:10.610
OK?

01:26:10.610 --> 01:26:13.040
So quoting from the paper
in the first paragraph,

01:26:13.040 --> 01:26:15.890
quote "income has a reliable
but surprisingly weak

01:26:15.890 --> 01:26:18.140
effect on happiness
within nations".

01:26:18.140 --> 01:26:19.640
Within nations just
mean they looked

01:26:19.640 --> 01:26:21.440
at this in not just
the United States,

01:26:21.440 --> 01:26:24.090
but a number of other
places, as well.

01:26:24.090 --> 01:26:26.300
"Particularly once
basic needs are met."

01:26:26.300 --> 01:26:29.630
OK, so if that's the
case, then what aspects

01:26:29.630 --> 01:26:32.048
of money and having money
are related to happiness?

01:26:32.048 --> 01:26:34.340
Certainly, there are people
who have a lot of money who

01:26:34.340 --> 01:26:35.210
are very happy.

01:26:35.210 --> 01:26:36.890
Certainly, there are people
who have very little money

01:26:36.890 --> 01:26:37.760
who are very happy.

01:26:37.760 --> 01:26:39.645
And of course, the
reverse is also true.

01:26:39.645 --> 01:26:42.020
There are plenty of people
who don't have very much money

01:26:42.020 --> 01:26:44.060
who are unhappy,
and in fact, there

01:26:44.060 --> 01:26:47.360
are people who have a lot of
money who are very unhappy.

01:26:47.360 --> 01:26:50.330
A point that whenever
it's made often

01:26:50.330 --> 01:26:53.720
leads those with less money
to kind of roll their eyes,

01:26:53.720 --> 01:26:57.160
because the assumption is more
money does increase happiness.

01:26:57.160 --> 01:26:58.160
And in fact, it doesn't.

01:26:58.160 --> 01:27:03.980
And later, we'll get back to
this idea of whether or how one

01:27:03.980 --> 01:27:08.600
acquired their money has
any impact on whether

01:27:08.600 --> 01:27:12.170
or not that money increases
their happiness or not.

01:27:12.170 --> 01:27:13.745
Let's kind of earmark
that for later.

01:27:13.745 --> 01:27:15.620
In the meantime, let's
talk a little bit more

01:27:15.620 --> 01:27:17.300
about the findings
in this paper.

01:27:17.300 --> 01:27:19.940
This paper is
interesting, because what

01:27:19.940 --> 01:27:23.570
it did is it explored something
called prosocial spending.

01:27:23.570 --> 01:27:26.390
Prosocial spending
is a phenomenon

01:27:26.390 --> 01:27:28.670
where people are taking
a certain portion

01:27:28.670 --> 01:27:32.150
of their income, and they
are giving it to others.

01:27:32.150 --> 01:27:35.870
Often for causes or for
things that they think

01:27:35.870 --> 01:27:37.762
are important to see
happen in the world

01:27:37.762 --> 01:27:38.720
or change in the world.

01:27:38.720 --> 01:27:41.510
That could be a hungry
individual having access

01:27:41.510 --> 01:27:43.580
to food or medical care.

01:27:43.580 --> 01:27:45.350
It could be for
environmental causes.

01:27:45.350 --> 01:27:46.942
It could be for animal wellness.

01:27:46.942 --> 01:27:48.900
It could be for any number
of different things.

01:27:48.900 --> 01:27:50.840
It could even be
giving somebody money

01:27:50.840 --> 01:27:53.780
so that they can buy themselves
a gift or giving somebody money

01:27:53.780 --> 01:27:57.620
and not having any understanding
or expectation of what they're

01:27:57.620 --> 01:27:59.270
going to do with the money.

01:27:59.270 --> 01:28:02.240
Again, one of the central
themes around gratitude

01:28:02.240 --> 01:28:06.680
is that while
receiving is great,

01:28:06.680 --> 01:28:10.700
giving is also great in terms of
increasing sense of well-being.

01:28:10.700 --> 01:28:13.790
And one of the more
important features to that

01:28:13.790 --> 01:28:17.720
is when we give, either
in the form of words

01:28:17.720 --> 01:28:20.890
or in the form of
resources, knowledge

01:28:20.890 --> 01:28:24.320
that the person
receiving benefits

01:28:24.320 --> 01:28:26.450
from that in some
real way, greatly

01:28:26.450 --> 01:28:29.030
increases the
chance that there's

01:28:29.030 --> 01:28:33.098
an increase in happiness for the
giver, as well as the receiver.

01:28:33.098 --> 01:28:34.640
Again, that's a note
about gratitude,

01:28:34.640 --> 01:28:37.530
but not an insignificant one
as it relates to the study.

01:28:37.530 --> 01:28:40.850
So what the study found was
that higher prosocial spending

01:28:40.850 --> 01:28:42.500
was associated
with significantly

01:28:42.500 --> 01:28:43.340
greater happiness.

01:28:43.340 --> 01:28:46.160
This was a very statistically
significant effect.

01:28:46.160 --> 01:28:49.430
And they found that the
effects of income and prosocial

01:28:49.430 --> 01:28:54.398
spending were independent
and similar in magnitude.

01:28:54.398 --> 01:28:55.940
Independent and
similar in magnitude.

01:28:55.940 --> 01:28:57.523
I'll explain what
that means for those

01:28:57.523 --> 01:29:00.020
of you that might be confused
by that statement in just

01:29:00.020 --> 01:29:00.710
a moment.

01:29:00.710 --> 01:29:03.560
Whereas, quote, "personal
spending remained

01:29:03.560 --> 01:29:05.220
unrelated to happiness".

01:29:05.220 --> 01:29:06.770
So what this study
basically found

01:29:06.770 --> 01:29:11.240
was if people are allotted
a certain amount of money

01:29:11.240 --> 01:29:16.483
to give away, and one
adjusts for overall income,

01:29:16.483 --> 01:29:17.900
and this is
important, because you

01:29:17.900 --> 01:29:22.430
could imagine that for some
individual giving away $2,000

01:29:22.430 --> 01:29:24.620
might represent a
significant portion

01:29:24.620 --> 01:29:26.630
of their yearly
or monthly income.

01:29:26.630 --> 01:29:30.110
And for another individual it
might represent a tiny fraction

01:29:30.110 --> 01:29:31.190
of their income.

01:29:31.190 --> 01:29:35.540
But when you adjust for
income level, what you find

01:29:35.540 --> 01:29:39.460
is that people who
gave away money

01:29:39.460 --> 01:29:42.970
benefited tremendously in
terms of their own increase

01:29:42.970 --> 01:29:44.480
in happiness.

01:29:44.480 --> 01:29:47.890
In fact, quote, "employees who
devoted more of their bonuses

01:29:47.890 --> 01:29:49.810
to prosocial spending,
that is giving way

01:29:49.810 --> 01:29:52.540
more money, experience greater
happiness after receiving

01:29:52.540 --> 01:29:55.750
the bonus, and the manner in
which they spent that bonus was

01:29:55.750 --> 01:29:57.640
a more important predictor
of their happiness

01:29:57.640 --> 01:29:59.770
than the size of
the bonus itself".

01:29:59.770 --> 01:30:01.360
This was an actual
experiment they ran

01:30:01.360 --> 01:30:04.065
with real income, real money.

01:30:04.065 --> 01:30:05.440
I'm going to read
that again just

01:30:05.440 --> 01:30:07.190
to make sure it hits
home, because I found

01:30:07.190 --> 01:30:10.130
this to be really impactful.

01:30:10.130 --> 01:30:14.000
"Employees who devoted a
greater fraction of their bonus

01:30:14.000 --> 01:30:18.560
to prosocial spending, that is
giving away money to others,

01:30:18.560 --> 01:30:21.800
experienced greater happiness
after receiving the bonus.

01:30:21.800 --> 01:30:23.390
And the manner in
which they spent

01:30:23.390 --> 01:30:26.150
that bonus was a more important
predictor of their happiness

01:30:26.150 --> 01:30:27.870
than the size of
the bonus itself."

01:30:27.870 --> 01:30:30.740
So the actual bonus, the
receiving of the money,

01:30:30.740 --> 01:30:35.630
led to greater increases in
happiness if they gave it away.

01:30:35.630 --> 01:30:39.080
And the act of giving
it away itself,

01:30:39.080 --> 01:30:41.360
led to greater
increases in happiness

01:30:41.360 --> 01:30:43.440
than receiving the bonus.

01:30:43.440 --> 01:30:45.620
So it's a twofer,
as you might say.

01:30:45.620 --> 01:30:48.650
So the takeaway from this
study and studies like it,

01:30:48.650 --> 01:30:51.780
I think is pretty obvious that
to the extent that we can.

01:30:51.780 --> 01:30:53.990
And again, when I say to
the extent that we can,

01:30:53.990 --> 01:30:57.590
this means whatever
percentage of our own income

01:30:57.590 --> 01:31:00.960
that we can afford to give away,
or if we don't have income,

01:31:00.960 --> 01:31:02.990
the percentage of our effort.

01:31:02.990 --> 01:31:05.390
I mean this is about money,
but it's also about effort.

01:31:05.390 --> 01:31:06.740
We can help others, right?

01:31:06.740 --> 01:31:11.990
You can serve in food kitchens,
you can do community gardening,

01:31:11.990 --> 01:31:15.200
you can pick up trash, you
can do any number of things.

01:31:15.200 --> 01:31:17.540
You can assist a
neighbor with child care

01:31:17.540 --> 01:31:20.360
or you can assist a neighbor
who is physically less

01:31:20.360 --> 01:31:22.910
able to retrieve their
paper, et cetera, et cetera.

01:31:22.910 --> 01:31:26.660
The point is that
giving resources,

01:31:26.660 --> 01:31:30.680
certainly in the form of money,
but also in the form of effort

01:31:30.680 --> 01:31:34.910
and time, is immensely
beneficial for synthesizing

01:31:34.910 --> 01:31:36.230
our own happiness.

01:31:36.230 --> 01:31:38.990
That is for the
giver, us, to increase

01:31:38.990 --> 01:31:40.250
our levels of happiness.

01:31:40.250 --> 01:31:43.670
But the degree of increase
in our own happiness

01:31:43.670 --> 01:31:47.360
is proportional in
some way to the extent

01:31:47.360 --> 01:31:51.080
to which the person receiving
actually needed that help

01:31:51.080 --> 01:31:52.670
and registers that help.

01:31:52.670 --> 01:31:55.220
Excellent research
also points to the fact

01:31:55.220 --> 01:31:58.130
that another potent
way to synthesize

01:31:58.130 --> 01:32:01.250
happiness, that is to create
genuine states of happiness

01:32:01.250 --> 01:32:05.660
in ourselves, is to leverage
the so-called focus system.

01:32:05.660 --> 01:32:09.320
Or rather, I should say to
de-emphasize the tendency

01:32:09.320 --> 01:32:11.107
of our minds to wander.

01:32:11.107 --> 01:32:13.190
There's an excellent paper
on this, also published

01:32:13.190 --> 01:32:14.148
in the Journal Science.

01:32:14.148 --> 01:32:15.380
This is now a classic paper.

01:32:15.380 --> 01:32:17.880
I talked a little bit about it
in the episode on meditation,

01:32:17.880 --> 01:32:19.910
but for those of you
that did or perhaps

01:32:19.910 --> 01:32:22.820
didn't hear that episode, I
just want to briefly touch

01:32:22.820 --> 01:32:25.310
on a few aspects of the paper.

01:32:25.310 --> 01:32:29.102
And in particular, a few aspects
of the paper that I didn't talk

01:32:29.102 --> 01:32:29.810
about previously.

01:32:29.810 --> 01:32:31.640
And the title of
this paper, again,

01:32:31.640 --> 01:32:33.140
is a very
straightforward in terms

01:32:33.140 --> 01:32:34.550
of telling you what it's about.

01:32:34.550 --> 01:32:38.570
And that is, "A wandering
mind is an unhappy mind"

01:32:38.570 --> 01:32:40.640
by Killingsworth and Gilbert.

01:32:40.640 --> 01:32:42.663
This paper was published
in Science in 2010,

01:32:42.663 --> 01:32:44.330
and we will provide
a link to the paper.

01:32:44.330 --> 01:32:46.550
This is frankly, a
very interesting paper.

01:32:46.550 --> 01:32:48.920
This paper involved
several subjects,

01:32:48.920 --> 01:32:52.340
or I should say,
2,250 adult subjects.

01:32:52.340 --> 01:32:54.230
And what they were
able to do was

01:32:54.230 --> 01:32:55.730
to contact these
subjects while they

01:32:55.730 --> 01:32:58.070
were going about living
their daily lives

01:32:58.070 --> 01:33:00.170
and ask them both
what they were doing

01:33:00.170 --> 01:33:01.602
and what they were feeling.

01:33:01.602 --> 01:33:03.560
Some additional questions
that they asked them,

01:33:03.560 --> 01:33:06.500
but they were able to
establish whether or not

01:33:06.500 --> 01:33:10.550
people were watching television
or doing housework or working

01:33:10.550 --> 01:33:14.600
on a home computer or resting or
listening to music, et cetera,

01:33:14.600 --> 01:33:15.930
in their natural environment.

01:33:15.930 --> 01:33:17.510
So this is outside
the laboratory.

01:33:17.510 --> 01:33:21.080
And they were able to
assess to what extent

01:33:21.080 --> 01:33:25.490
those people were happy
or unhappy or neutral,

01:33:25.490 --> 01:33:27.977
or had some other
emotional state at the time

01:33:27.977 --> 01:33:30.560
when they were engaging in any
number of different activities.

01:33:30.560 --> 01:33:34.880
And they assessed whether or
not those individuals were also

01:33:34.880 --> 01:33:38.000
focused on or focused away
from whatever activity they

01:33:38.000 --> 01:33:38.960
were engaging in.

01:33:38.960 --> 01:33:41.030
And the takeaways from
this study are many,

01:33:41.030 --> 01:33:44.180
but for sake of today's
discussion, what

01:33:44.180 --> 01:33:46.340
I think is especially
interesting

01:33:46.340 --> 01:33:49.280
is that regardless
of whether or not

01:33:49.280 --> 01:33:52.460
people were engaging in
activities that they enjoyed

01:33:52.460 --> 01:33:57.770
or not, the tendency for their
mind to wander from an activity

01:33:57.770 --> 01:33:59.990
predicted lower
levels of happiness

01:33:59.990 --> 01:34:02.180
than if they tended to be
focused on the activity

01:34:02.180 --> 01:34:03.710
they were engaged in.

01:34:03.710 --> 01:34:06.480
Now that itself
should be surprising.

01:34:06.480 --> 01:34:09.200
I mean, what that says
is that even if somebody

01:34:09.200 --> 01:34:11.600
was engaged in activity
like cleaning their house

01:34:11.600 --> 01:34:14.600
or doing homework or reading
something that they weren't

01:34:14.600 --> 01:34:18.090
enjoying, if they were focused
on what they were doing,

01:34:18.090 --> 01:34:22.730
they tended to report as happier
than if their mind was drifting

01:34:22.730 --> 01:34:23.610
elsewhere.

01:34:23.610 --> 01:34:26.570
Now this also points to the
idea that perhaps our minds

01:34:26.570 --> 01:34:29.090
drift to unpleasant thoughts
more than pleasant thoughts.

01:34:29.090 --> 01:34:31.430
But they also addressed
that in the study.

01:34:31.430 --> 01:34:32.930
Point I'd like to
make here is quote

01:34:32.930 --> 01:34:34.430
"although people's
minds were more

01:34:34.430 --> 01:34:37.430
likely to wander
to pleasant topics

01:34:37.430 --> 01:34:39.753
than to unpleasant topics"
and there the difference

01:34:39.753 --> 01:34:40.670
is pretty significant.

01:34:40.670 --> 01:34:44.180
People's minds tended to
wander to pleasant topics

01:34:44.180 --> 01:34:48.080
about 43% of the time as
opposed to unpleasant topics

01:34:48.080 --> 01:34:49.910
about 27% of the time.

01:34:49.910 --> 01:34:54.020
Or to neutral topics and the
remaining 31% of samples.

01:34:54.020 --> 01:34:56.300
"People were no
happier when thinking

01:34:56.300 --> 01:35:00.600
about pleasant topics than
about their current activity".

01:35:00.600 --> 01:35:01.620
Think about that.

01:35:01.620 --> 01:35:03.180
"People were no
happier than when

01:35:03.180 --> 01:35:06.250
thinking about pleasant things
then their current activity".

01:35:06.250 --> 01:35:09.600
In fact, the mere focus
on what they were doing

01:35:09.600 --> 01:35:12.970
was more powerful
than anything else,

01:35:12.970 --> 01:35:15.310
even if they didn't enjoy
what they were doing.

01:35:15.310 --> 01:35:17.970
So they go on here to say, quote
"although negative moods are

01:35:17.970 --> 01:35:20.400
known to cause mind
wandering, I now

01:35:20.400 --> 01:35:23.280
see strongly suggested that
mind wandering was generally

01:35:23.280 --> 01:35:25.920
the cause, the
cause, and not merely

01:35:25.920 --> 01:35:28.090
the consequence of unhappiness."

01:35:28.090 --> 01:35:30.090
So there are a lot of
aspects of this study that

01:35:30.090 --> 01:35:31.320
are worth going into.

01:35:31.320 --> 01:35:35.100
But the major takeaway, or the
one that perhaps we should all

01:35:35.100 --> 01:35:37.980
be most concerned with,
is that when we are not

01:35:37.980 --> 01:35:39.660
focused on what we
are doing, we tend

01:35:39.660 --> 01:35:41.880
to be far less happy
than when we are

01:35:41.880 --> 01:35:43.237
focused on what we are doing.

01:35:43.237 --> 01:35:44.820
Even if what we are
doing is something

01:35:44.820 --> 01:35:46.395
that we don't deem
very pleasant.

01:35:46.395 --> 01:35:48.270
And certainly, if we
are engaged in something

01:35:48.270 --> 01:35:50.837
that we consider very pleasant
and we are very focused on,

01:35:50.837 --> 01:35:52.920
well then our levels of
happiness are the highest.

01:35:52.920 --> 01:35:54.120
That's sort of obvious.

01:35:54.120 --> 01:35:57.630
But what this really speaks
to is the tremendous power

01:35:57.630 --> 01:36:01.620
of building our ability to
focus on what we're doing.

01:36:01.620 --> 01:36:03.493
And to stay present
to what we are doing.

01:36:03.493 --> 01:36:05.160
Now this whole notion
of staying present

01:36:05.160 --> 01:36:07.390
is one that itself is a
little bit complicated.

01:36:07.390 --> 01:36:09.930
And in the episode on meditation
I talked a little bit about

01:36:09.930 --> 01:36:11.850
whether or not it's
beneficial to be

01:36:11.850 --> 01:36:15.180
present to our
internal state, or that

01:36:15.180 --> 01:36:17.340
is our inter receptive state.

01:36:17.340 --> 01:36:21.600
Our feelings of our heart
rate and how full or empty

01:36:21.600 --> 01:36:25.350
our gut feels or our state of
being from our skin inward,

01:36:25.350 --> 01:36:26.965
or whether or not
we should focus

01:36:26.965 --> 01:36:29.340
on being present to things in
our immediate surroundings.

01:36:29.340 --> 01:36:30.750
Both our versions
of being quote,

01:36:30.750 --> 01:36:33.100
unquote "present"
as you can imagine.

01:36:33.100 --> 01:36:35.940
But in the one case,
we're focused internally,

01:36:35.940 --> 01:36:37.710
in the other we're
focused externally,

01:36:37.710 --> 01:36:39.210
and of course, most
of the time it's

01:36:39.210 --> 01:36:40.493
some combination of the two.

01:36:40.493 --> 01:36:41.910
But what this study
really says is

01:36:41.910 --> 01:36:45.630
that any practice that can
powerfully impact our ability

01:36:45.630 --> 01:36:49.955
to remain present in the
activity we are engaged in,

01:36:49.955 --> 01:36:51.330
could even be a
phone call, could

01:36:51.330 --> 01:36:54.032
be texting for that matter,
could be social media

01:36:54.032 --> 01:36:54.990
for that matter, right?

01:36:54.990 --> 01:36:57.100
We're not placing judgment
on the activity here.

01:36:57.100 --> 01:36:58.767
In fact, what we're
really talking about

01:36:58.767 --> 01:37:02.040
is the enormous happiness
increasing value

01:37:02.040 --> 01:37:04.680
of being present to what
we're doing regardless

01:37:04.680 --> 01:37:05.880
of what we are doing.

01:37:05.880 --> 01:37:08.940
And a practice that's known to
be beneficial for increasing

01:37:08.940 --> 01:37:12.760
our ability to focus
is, among other things,

01:37:12.760 --> 01:37:15.310
a short meditation practice.

01:37:15.310 --> 01:37:18.120
In fact, work from Wendy
Suzuki's lab at NYU,

01:37:18.120 --> 01:37:20.130
again, Wendy's been a
guest on this podcast.

01:37:20.130 --> 01:37:22.020
Her laboratory has
shown that even

01:37:22.020 --> 01:37:25.110
a very brief meditation
of about 13 minutes,

01:37:25.110 --> 01:37:26.970
and this would be the
sort of quote unquote

01:37:26.970 --> 01:37:29.070
"classic" type of
meditation of eyes

01:37:29.070 --> 01:37:31.020
closed, focusing
on one's breathing.

01:37:31.020 --> 01:37:33.750
Even a very brief meditation
of just 13 minutes

01:37:33.750 --> 01:37:38.310
or so done consistently,
so ideally, every day.

01:37:38.310 --> 01:37:40.900
But I have to imagine that
even if you skip a day,

01:37:40.900 --> 01:37:42.120
there are still benefits.

01:37:42.120 --> 01:37:44.370
That sort of
meditation can greatly

01:37:44.370 --> 01:37:45.990
enhance one's ability to focus.

01:37:45.990 --> 01:37:48.060
In her studies,
that was also shown

01:37:48.060 --> 01:37:52.020
that sort of brief meditation
could also greatly enhance

01:37:52.020 --> 01:37:54.698
mood and sleep and
various aspects

01:37:54.698 --> 01:37:55.740
of cognitive performance.

01:37:55.740 --> 01:37:58.500
And work from my laboratory,
in collaboration with Dr. David

01:37:58.500 --> 01:38:00.990
Spiegel at Stanford,
department of psychiatry

01:38:00.990 --> 01:38:04.210
has shown that even briefer
meditations of even just five

01:38:04.210 --> 01:38:07.620
minutes per day can have fairly
outsized positive effects

01:38:07.620 --> 01:38:09.660
on a number of different
parameters, as well.

01:38:09.660 --> 01:38:12.360
Those very brief
types of meditations,

01:38:12.360 --> 01:38:15.960
because they really are
focusing and more accurately,

01:38:15.960 --> 01:38:18.240
I should say,
refocusing exercises.

01:38:18.240 --> 01:38:21.090
When you do that sort of
activity of closing your eyes

01:38:21.090 --> 01:38:23.610
and forcing yourself
to focus and refocus

01:38:23.610 --> 01:38:25.560
on your breath and
internal state,

01:38:25.560 --> 01:38:27.360
that is directing your
perception inward.

01:38:27.360 --> 01:38:30.390
Or if you choose, you
could deliberately

01:38:30.390 --> 01:38:32.790
focus your perception
on some external object

01:38:32.790 --> 01:38:36.060
or sound, for that matter.

01:38:36.060 --> 01:38:39.720
When one does that, the circuits
of the brain involved in focus

01:38:39.720 --> 01:38:41.490
dramatically improve.

01:38:41.490 --> 01:38:45.300
That is they rewire and
increase their ability for you

01:38:45.300 --> 01:38:47.370
to achieve focus.

01:38:47.370 --> 01:38:49.770
Many of us have heard about
meditation, many of us

01:38:49.770 --> 01:38:53.130
think about meditation as
a "mindfulness" exercise.

01:38:53.130 --> 01:38:55.710
Mindfulness in quotes, because
that itself needs definition.

01:38:55.710 --> 01:38:59.550
But I prefer to view
meditations of the sort

01:38:59.550 --> 01:39:02.700
that I just described
as perceptual or focus

01:39:02.700 --> 01:39:06.480
based training, which is
really what the data point to.

01:39:06.480 --> 01:39:08.460
Notions around consciousness
and states of mind

01:39:08.460 --> 01:39:12.030
are very hard to define, but
it's very clear that even

01:39:12.030 --> 01:39:15.300
a 5 minute a day or ideally,
an up to a 13 minute a day

01:39:15.300 --> 01:39:18.600
meditation can greatly
increase our ability to focus.

01:39:18.600 --> 01:39:21.090
And based on the
findings in this paper,

01:39:21.090 --> 01:39:24.720
"A wandering mind is an unhappy
mind" also make it very clear

01:39:24.720 --> 01:39:28.260
that the ability to
refocus again and again

01:39:28.260 --> 01:39:31.260
and again on what we're
doing throughout our day,

01:39:31.260 --> 01:39:33.240
regardless of what
we're doing, can

01:39:33.240 --> 01:39:37.530
have a very dramatic, in fact,
a statistically significant

01:39:37.530 --> 01:39:40.057
increase on our levels
of overall happiness.

01:39:40.057 --> 01:39:41.640
So what else does
the research tell us

01:39:41.640 --> 01:39:45.360
we can do to increase our
levels of genuine happiness?

01:39:45.360 --> 01:39:48.660
Well, it's very clear based
on the longitudinal study

01:39:48.660 --> 01:39:51.780
from Harvard, as well
as the Yale happiness

01:39:51.780 --> 01:39:55.230
project and the work of
numerous laboratories in the US

01:39:55.230 --> 01:39:59.410
and elsewhere, that
quality social connection

01:39:59.410 --> 01:40:02.860
is extremely powerful
in terms of its ability

01:40:02.860 --> 01:40:05.560
to increase our
levels of happiness.

01:40:05.560 --> 01:40:07.780
What is quality
social connection?

01:40:07.780 --> 01:40:10.750
Quality social connection
falls into a number

01:40:10.750 --> 01:40:11.740
of different bins.

01:40:11.740 --> 01:40:14.770
This can be romantic connection,
this can be friendship,

01:40:14.770 --> 01:40:16.990
this can even be
coworker or just

01:40:16.990 --> 01:40:20.470
daily superficial
interaction type connections.

01:40:20.470 --> 01:40:22.447
That's surprising
to a lot of people,

01:40:22.447 --> 01:40:25.030
because I think a lot of people
hear quality social connection

01:40:25.030 --> 01:40:27.100
and they think
deep conversation.

01:40:27.100 --> 01:40:29.500
But it's very clear
from the research

01:40:29.500 --> 01:40:33.040
that oftentimes our
conversations with people

01:40:33.040 --> 01:40:36.163
that we are closest to are
actually quite shallow.

01:40:36.163 --> 01:40:38.830
If you think about it, if you've
been in a romantic relationship

01:40:38.830 --> 01:40:40.670
or a friendship for a
long period of time,

01:40:40.670 --> 01:40:42.880
or maybe even a sibling
relationship or other family

01:40:42.880 --> 01:40:45.790
relationship, much of
what you talk about

01:40:45.790 --> 01:40:49.730
is fairly superficial
or fairly trivial.

01:40:49.730 --> 01:40:52.330
In fact, it's often a
sharing of the trivial day

01:40:52.330 --> 01:40:55.780
to day things between two
people or through groups

01:40:55.780 --> 01:40:58.300
of people that
leads to the feeling

01:40:58.300 --> 01:41:00.460
that people are really
connected to one another.

01:41:00.460 --> 01:41:03.820
In particular, if it's involving
shared experiences of any kind,

01:41:03.820 --> 01:41:05.480
good or bad.

01:41:05.480 --> 01:41:08.320
So there's an extensive
literature on social connection

01:41:08.320 --> 01:41:09.910
and how to build
social connection.

01:41:09.910 --> 01:41:11.530
This certainly
should be the topic

01:41:11.530 --> 01:41:13.520
of a full episode
of this podcast

01:41:13.520 --> 01:41:15.050
in the not too distant future.

01:41:15.050 --> 01:41:18.340
But because social connection
can have such a powerful impact

01:41:18.340 --> 01:41:21.790
on our states of happiness
and overall well-being,

01:41:21.790 --> 01:41:25.150
I want to emphasize a few
features of social connection

01:41:25.150 --> 01:41:27.400
that I think most people
might not appreciate.

01:41:27.400 --> 01:41:30.140
Once again, when we hear
quality social connection,

01:41:30.140 --> 01:41:32.860
I think most of us tend to
think about deep, meaningful

01:41:32.860 --> 01:41:36.160
conversation or long walks
on the beach or camping trips

01:41:36.160 --> 01:41:37.520
together or travel together.

01:41:37.520 --> 01:41:39.460
And while all of those
certainly qualify

01:41:39.460 --> 01:41:43.240
as wonderful opportunities
for social connection,

01:41:43.240 --> 01:41:45.490
opportunities for
quality social connection

01:41:45.490 --> 01:41:48.530
are certainly not limited to
those kinds of interactions.

01:41:48.530 --> 01:41:52.250
In fact, I can recall times
in my graduate career,

01:41:52.250 --> 01:41:54.880
so this would be times when I
was living in the laboratory.

01:41:54.880 --> 01:41:57.790
Because that was a significant
portion of my graduate years.

01:41:57.790 --> 01:42:01.030
And one of the more important
social connections for me

01:42:01.030 --> 01:42:04.960
was the staff that worked there
in the wee hours of the night

01:42:04.960 --> 01:42:06.890
and that came in very
early in the morning.

01:42:06.890 --> 01:42:09.940
So one of the more regular
social connections I had is I

01:42:09.940 --> 01:42:13.180
would brush my teeth in
the hallway bathroom.

01:42:13.180 --> 01:42:16.040
And there was no one else
really around at that time,

01:42:16.040 --> 01:42:17.830
except for the
janitors that tended

01:42:17.830 --> 01:42:19.480
to leave early in the morning.

01:42:19.480 --> 01:42:21.370
They had worked a good
portion of the night.

01:42:21.370 --> 01:42:23.662
Or that were arriving
very early in the day.

01:42:23.662 --> 01:42:25.870
And the fact that I would
see them on a regular basis

01:42:25.870 --> 01:42:27.670
and maybe exchange a few
words about their work

01:42:27.670 --> 01:42:29.920
or their families or the
holidays, actually for me,

01:42:29.920 --> 01:42:31.420
became very meaningful.

01:42:31.420 --> 01:42:33.820
In part, because my social
connections at the time

01:42:33.820 --> 01:42:36.160
were really limited to
only social connections

01:42:36.160 --> 01:42:38.380
that I had in the
context of work.

01:42:38.380 --> 01:42:41.290
Now some people might look
at my schedule at that time

01:42:41.290 --> 01:42:43.310
and look at my life at
that time and say, well,

01:42:43.310 --> 01:42:44.680
that was very unhealthy.

01:42:44.680 --> 01:42:47.350
You were lacking in
certain number of ways.

01:42:47.350 --> 01:42:49.870
But frankly, looking
back, and at the time,

01:42:49.870 --> 01:42:52.030
and I know this because
I journaled at the time,

01:42:52.030 --> 01:42:53.860
I was exceedingly
happy, at least

01:42:53.860 --> 01:42:55.270
for that stage of my life.

01:42:55.270 --> 01:42:56.860
At that stage of
my life, I wanted

01:42:56.860 --> 01:43:01.600
to be focused primarily on
doing experiments and immersing

01:43:01.600 --> 01:43:03.940
myself in my
scientific training.

01:43:03.940 --> 01:43:07.930
And for me, the even seemingly
insignificant interactions

01:43:07.930 --> 01:43:11.030
of talking to the
janitor in the morning

01:43:11.030 --> 01:43:13.850
or some of the other regular
staff was not insignificant.

01:43:13.850 --> 01:43:15.680
In fact, for me it
was very significant.

01:43:15.680 --> 01:43:17.990
And over the holidays when
their hours were reduced,

01:43:17.990 --> 01:43:19.580
I actually missed
them quite a lot.

01:43:19.580 --> 01:43:21.080
And even as I talk
about this, I can

01:43:21.080 --> 01:43:22.700
recall the feelings
of well-being

01:43:22.700 --> 01:43:24.377
of just seeing familiar faces.

01:43:24.377 --> 01:43:26.210
And that brings up an
important point, which

01:43:26.210 --> 01:43:30.200
is there's quite extensive
literature pointing to the fact

01:43:30.200 --> 01:43:34.370
that when we see faces,
especially faces in the morning

01:43:34.370 --> 01:43:38.750
and in the late afternoon,
there is a positive impact

01:43:38.750 --> 01:43:40.290
on the emotional circuitry.

01:43:40.290 --> 01:43:42.290
Or I should say, the
circuitry of the brain that

01:43:42.290 --> 01:43:44.390
underlies emotional well-being.

01:43:44.390 --> 01:43:46.430
And that shouldn't
come as surprising.

01:43:46.430 --> 01:43:50.150
We as old world primates,
much like other primates,

01:43:50.150 --> 01:43:53.810
are very dependent on faces
and facial expressions

01:43:53.810 --> 01:43:57.330
in terms of registering
our own place in life

01:43:57.330 --> 01:43:59.210
and our emotional state.

01:43:59.210 --> 01:44:00.950
Now the origins
of this are many.

01:44:00.950 --> 01:44:03.440
In particular, we
have a brain area,

01:44:03.440 --> 01:44:05.425
it's actually called
the fusiform face gyrus.

01:44:05.425 --> 01:44:06.800
This is an area
of the brain that

01:44:06.800 --> 01:44:09.380
was largely
discovered by a woman

01:44:09.380 --> 01:44:11.510
by Nancy Kanwisher at MIT.

01:44:11.510 --> 01:44:14.360
And the Kanwisher lab
has done extensive work

01:44:14.360 --> 01:44:16.760
showing that this
brain area that's

01:44:16.760 --> 01:44:21.410
dedicated to the processing
of faces, and not just faces

01:44:21.410 --> 01:44:24.470
in real life, but faces
on computer screens

01:44:24.470 --> 01:44:27.200
and elsewhere, are
intimately tied

01:44:27.200 --> 01:44:29.780
to areas of the brain that are
associated with emotionality.

01:44:29.780 --> 01:44:31.738
That's actually a work
from another laboratory,

01:44:31.738 --> 01:44:34.190
Dorsal's laboratory at
Caltech, now at Uc Berkeley,

01:44:34.190 --> 01:44:36.380
has shown that this
face processing

01:44:36.380 --> 01:44:41.240
area in the brain in both
non-human primates and primates

01:44:41.240 --> 01:44:44.660
is directly linked to
the areas of our brain

01:44:44.660 --> 01:44:47.180
that associate with
anxiety and fear.

01:44:47.180 --> 01:44:48.590
But also, areas
of the brain that

01:44:48.590 --> 01:44:50.030
are associated with well-being.

01:44:50.030 --> 01:44:51.770
So it comes as no
surprise that when

01:44:51.770 --> 01:44:53.990
we see faces, in
particular, friendly faces,

01:44:53.990 --> 01:44:56.780
even if we have just brief
interactions with those faces,

01:44:56.780 --> 01:44:58.970
and even if no
words are exchanged,

01:44:58.970 --> 01:45:01.430
that creates the
sense of social bond.

01:45:01.430 --> 01:45:03.650
And it creates a sense
of predictability.

01:45:03.650 --> 01:45:05.900
And I raise this again because
I think a lot of people

01:45:05.900 --> 01:45:07.610
think that social
connection always

01:45:07.610 --> 01:45:10.630
has to come in the form of
close friendships, which

01:45:10.630 --> 01:45:11.630
of course are wonderful.

01:45:11.630 --> 01:45:14.030
Or close romantic relationships,
which of course, are wonderful.

01:45:14.030 --> 01:45:16.770
Or close family relationships,
which of course, are wonderful.

01:45:16.770 --> 01:45:20.480
But as we'll soon discuss
in our model of happiness,

01:45:20.480 --> 01:45:22.070
or how to achieve
happiness based

01:45:22.070 --> 01:45:25.790
on the scientific
literature in a few minutes,

01:45:25.790 --> 01:45:29.720
social connection can and
should come in various forms.

01:45:29.720 --> 01:45:31.640
And when I say
various forms, I mean

01:45:31.640 --> 01:45:35.480
forms of brief interaction,
more superficial interaction,

01:45:35.480 --> 01:45:38.060
and forms of deeper interaction.

01:45:38.060 --> 01:45:40.520
All of those are relevant
to our states of happiness.

01:45:40.520 --> 01:45:43.850
And there's research to
support that daily interactions

01:45:43.850 --> 01:45:47.900
with somebody at a cafe or
just a brief hello or a smile.

01:45:47.900 --> 01:45:50.060
Provided that we
are both present,

01:45:50.060 --> 01:45:53.070
or we make the effort to be
present to those interactions,

01:45:53.070 --> 01:45:55.940
however brief they are,
can have a positive effect

01:45:55.940 --> 01:45:57.260
on people's overall well-being.

01:45:57.260 --> 01:46:00.470
And not just in that
moment, but consistently.

01:46:00.470 --> 01:46:02.210
Evidenced by the
fact, I think, that

01:46:02.210 --> 01:46:04.340
when I look back on
those years of working

01:46:04.340 --> 01:46:08.270
long hours in the laboratory and
essentially restricting myself

01:46:08.270 --> 01:46:12.680
either to exercising,
sleeping, eating or working.

01:46:12.680 --> 01:46:15.170
Again, that's what I wanted
at that stage of my life.

01:46:15.170 --> 01:46:16.920
Certainly, not the way
I live my life now.

01:46:16.920 --> 01:46:19.080
But that's what I wanted
that stage of my life.

01:46:19.080 --> 01:46:21.410
That even those
seemingly insignificant

01:46:21.410 --> 01:46:24.230
social interactions
were important to me

01:46:24.230 --> 01:46:26.690
and had a potent
impact on increasing

01:46:26.690 --> 01:46:27.620
my level of happiness.

01:46:27.620 --> 01:46:28.770
And frankly, still do.

01:46:28.770 --> 01:46:30.380
And I feel that right now.

01:46:30.380 --> 01:46:33.980
That said, I think all of us
can appreciate the immense value

01:46:33.980 --> 01:46:38.090
of social connection that is of
the more long lasting, and for

01:46:38.090 --> 01:46:39.930
lack of a better
word, richer type,

01:46:39.930 --> 01:46:43.250
whether or not that is with
siblings or with parents

01:46:43.250 --> 01:46:45.920
or with friends or
with romantic partners.

01:46:45.920 --> 01:46:48.320
For that reason, I want
to emphasize a little bit

01:46:48.320 --> 01:46:50.780
about what
constitutes connection

01:46:50.780 --> 01:46:52.430
and what constitutes
social connection.

01:46:52.430 --> 01:46:54.560
There are basically two
forms of social connection

01:46:54.560 --> 01:46:57.110
that have been studied
and I'll review both,

01:46:57.110 --> 01:46:59.600
as it relates to increasing
our levels of happiness.

01:46:59.600 --> 01:47:02.480
And the first one is
presence and eye contact,

01:47:02.480 --> 01:47:04.680
and the second is
physical contact.

01:47:04.680 --> 01:47:06.710
So in terms of presence
and eye contact

01:47:06.710 --> 01:47:08.840
there's been a lot
of studies about

01:47:08.840 --> 01:47:11.900
whether or not people
exchange direct eye contact

01:47:11.900 --> 01:47:15.230
during conversation or not,
dictating whether or not

01:47:15.230 --> 01:47:18.380
each individual in
that interaction

01:47:18.380 --> 01:47:21.050
feels as if they
had a connection.

01:47:21.050 --> 01:47:24.230
Now again, keep in mind that
while we think of connection

01:47:24.230 --> 01:47:26.870
as relating to some deep
or meaningful conversation,

01:47:26.870 --> 01:47:28.370
and oftentimes that
can be the case.

01:47:28.370 --> 01:47:32.990
Think for instance, an excellent
therapist patient relationship.

01:47:32.990 --> 01:47:35.180
Or an excellent
romantic relationship.

01:47:35.180 --> 01:47:38.300
Or an excellent friendship
where you really feel

01:47:38.300 --> 01:47:40.460
heard and understood, or
at least to the extent

01:47:40.460 --> 01:47:42.190
that people are
willing to explore

01:47:42.190 --> 01:47:43.190
certain topics with you.

01:47:43.190 --> 01:47:45.065
You're willing to hear
them and listen really

01:47:45.065 --> 01:47:46.482
carefully for what
they're saying,

01:47:46.482 --> 01:47:49.040
and they're willing to hear and
listen to what you're saying

01:47:49.040 --> 01:47:50.570
in an attempt to understand.

01:47:50.570 --> 01:47:53.060
That certainly can enhance
the sense of social connection

01:47:53.060 --> 01:47:55.940
leading to what people would
call social bonds leading

01:47:55.940 --> 01:47:57.410
to increased happiness.

01:47:57.410 --> 01:48:02.030
But eye contact is also known
to be an important feature.

01:48:02.030 --> 01:48:04.130
The thing about eye
contact is that most people

01:48:04.130 --> 01:48:06.440
assume that a lot
of eye contact,

01:48:06.440 --> 01:48:08.420
and in fact,
ongoing eye contact,

01:48:08.420 --> 01:48:11.570
is critical to a
sense of connection.

01:48:11.570 --> 01:48:13.250
And in fact, that's
not the case.

01:48:13.250 --> 01:48:16.577
There's a recent paper that I
find really interesting that

01:48:16.577 --> 01:48:18.410
was published in
Proceedings of the National

01:48:18.410 --> 01:48:21.140
Academy of Sciences in 2021.

01:48:21.140 --> 01:48:23.330
The title of this
paper is "Eye contact

01:48:23.330 --> 01:48:25.640
marks the rise and fall
of shared attention

01:48:25.640 --> 01:48:26.670
in conversation".

01:48:26.670 --> 01:48:28.920
I find this paper interesting
for a number of reasons.

01:48:28.920 --> 01:48:31.790
First of all, my laboratory
works on internal states

01:48:31.790 --> 01:48:33.380
and vision, so it
relates directly

01:48:33.380 --> 01:48:35.210
to the work that
my laboratory does.

01:48:35.210 --> 01:48:37.970
But also, that it
violates what I thought

01:48:37.970 --> 01:48:40.490
was a general rule of
social connection, which

01:48:40.490 --> 01:48:42.980
is this idea that two people
needed to be focused on one

01:48:42.980 --> 01:48:45.230
another, that is
looking at one another

01:48:45.230 --> 01:48:48.740
directly and fairly consistently
throughout a conversation

01:48:48.740 --> 01:48:51.170
in order for the feeling
of connection to emerge.

01:48:51.170 --> 01:48:53.335
But it turns out
that's not the case.

01:48:53.335 --> 01:48:55.460
And in fact, just to give
you the takeaway and then

01:48:55.460 --> 01:49:00.290
I'll flesh it out a little bit
with some data, eye contact,

01:49:00.290 --> 01:49:03.448
or I should say mutual
eye contact, so two people

01:49:03.448 --> 01:49:05.990
registering the presence of the
other person looking at them.

01:49:05.990 --> 01:49:07.640
You're looking at me
and I'm looking at you.

01:49:07.640 --> 01:49:09.098
If you're watching
this on YouTube,

01:49:09.098 --> 01:49:12.223
then perhaps we are actually
doing this at that moment.

01:49:12.223 --> 01:49:13.640
And if you're
listening, just know

01:49:13.640 --> 01:49:15.050
that I'm looking
directly into the camera

01:49:15.050 --> 01:49:16.467
as I'm saying this
at this moment.

01:49:16.467 --> 01:49:19.820
If we were to be looking
directly at one another,

01:49:19.820 --> 01:49:23.640
that, it turns out,
signals the next step,

01:49:23.640 --> 01:49:27.290
which is that it's very likely
that we will each both look

01:49:27.290 --> 01:49:28.350
away.

01:49:28.350 --> 01:49:31.490
And that turns out to
be a way in which we set

01:49:31.490 --> 01:49:35.083
and reset attention continually
during conversation.

01:49:35.083 --> 01:49:36.500
So again, I really
like this study

01:49:36.500 --> 01:49:38.730
because of the high fidelity.

01:49:38.730 --> 01:49:40.520
The high temporal precision.

01:49:40.520 --> 01:49:42.770
That is, the precision
over time at which they

01:49:42.770 --> 01:49:48.020
looked at eye contact and
engagement of attention

01:49:48.020 --> 01:49:49.340
between individuals.

01:49:49.340 --> 01:49:51.955
And they did this by looking
at things like pupil size

01:49:51.955 --> 01:49:53.330
and of course
where the eyes were

01:49:53.330 --> 01:49:54.665
looking and so on and so forth.

01:49:54.665 --> 01:49:56.790
The basic takeaway of this
study was the following.

01:49:56.790 --> 01:49:58.290
And here I'm quoting
from the study.

01:49:58.290 --> 01:50:01.490
Quote "Rather than
maximizing shared attention,

01:50:01.490 --> 01:50:03.770
good conversation
may require shifts

01:50:03.770 --> 01:50:07.520
in and out of shared states
accompanied by eye contact".

01:50:07.520 --> 01:50:10.610
So what this basically
says is that when

01:50:10.610 --> 01:50:13.125
two people are
involved in a very,

01:50:13.125 --> 01:50:14.750
let's call it an
intimate conversation,

01:50:14.750 --> 01:50:17.690
but the word intimate
should not be misconstrued

01:50:17.690 --> 01:50:20.330
to mean something about
intimacy or sexual intimacy

01:50:20.330 --> 01:50:21.710
or physical intimacy.

01:50:21.710 --> 01:50:23.990
Just a conversation
in which both people

01:50:23.990 --> 01:50:27.320
feel present to the
conversation and focused

01:50:27.320 --> 01:50:30.020
on that conversation and that
conversation and its contents

01:50:30.020 --> 01:50:30.950
only.

01:50:30.950 --> 01:50:33.840
The tendency is for people
to take turns talking,

01:50:33.840 --> 01:50:35.840
although sometimes,
depending on the individuals

01:50:35.840 --> 01:50:37.580
they might interrupt
more or less.

01:50:37.580 --> 01:50:40.370
Again, interrupting can
be a sign of interest,

01:50:40.370 --> 01:50:42.630
it doesn't always have
to be rude, by the way.

01:50:42.630 --> 01:50:45.290
But they're sharing information.

01:50:45.290 --> 01:50:48.500
Hopefully, about a common
topic or set of topics.

01:50:48.500 --> 01:50:51.503
They will at some moment
look at one another.

01:50:51.503 --> 01:50:52.670
That's what the study shows.

01:50:52.670 --> 01:50:55.700
And that after briefly
gazing directly at one

01:50:55.700 --> 01:50:59.810
another, attention peaks,
and then they will look away,

01:50:59.810 --> 01:51:01.670
and attention will get reduced.

01:51:01.670 --> 01:51:03.740
And then the
conversation consists

01:51:03.740 --> 01:51:08.510
of a series of focusing back
on one another with their eyes,

01:51:08.510 --> 01:51:10.940
and then focusing off,
focusing on and focusing off.

01:51:10.940 --> 01:51:13.940
And those mutual
eye contact moments

01:51:13.940 --> 01:51:16.730
actually predict the
breaking of attention.

01:51:16.730 --> 01:51:18.858
So it's this ramping up
of attention and braking

01:51:18.858 --> 01:51:19.400
of attention.

01:51:19.400 --> 01:51:21.560
Ramping of attention and
braking of attention.

01:51:21.560 --> 01:51:23.960
I think these are important
results because they violate

01:51:23.960 --> 01:51:27.680
this stereotype or assumption
that deep social connection

01:51:27.680 --> 01:51:31.040
of the sort leading
to happiness always

01:51:31.040 --> 01:51:35.180
involves ongoing eye
contact or ongoing focus.

01:51:35.180 --> 01:51:39.650
Just as with meditation, just
as with any activity, frankly,

01:51:39.650 --> 01:51:43.580
we undergo shifts in
attention and focus.

01:51:43.580 --> 01:51:46.550
That is focus ramps up and
then it breaks, and then

01:51:46.550 --> 01:51:47.240
it re-engages.

01:51:47.240 --> 01:51:49.880
It ramps up it breaks,
and then it re-engages.

01:51:49.880 --> 01:51:52.280
And that, it turns
out, is the basis

01:51:52.280 --> 01:51:55.170
of in-depth connected
conversation.

01:51:55.170 --> 01:51:57.650
So for those of you that
are interested in creating

01:51:57.650 --> 01:52:01.110
social connection
in any context,

01:52:01.110 --> 01:52:04.020
and in particular for sake
of increasing happiness,

01:52:04.020 --> 01:52:06.750
because it's very clear
that social connections,

01:52:06.750 --> 01:52:09.390
even if they are fairly
superficial social connections,

01:52:09.390 --> 01:52:11.340
can increase our
sense of happiness.

01:52:11.340 --> 01:52:13.290
Seeing faces is important.

01:52:13.290 --> 01:52:16.080
Ideally, faces in person,
although I suppose these days,

01:52:16.080 --> 01:52:19.530
over Zoom or over other
screen type medium

01:52:19.530 --> 01:52:21.610
would be a close second.

01:52:21.610 --> 01:52:25.470
But the point is that if you
want to increase happiness

01:52:25.470 --> 01:52:27.303
you need to have quality
social connections.

01:52:27.303 --> 01:52:29.387
And if you want to have
quality social connections

01:52:29.387 --> 01:52:31.053
you need to be
present and engage

01:52:31.053 --> 01:52:32.220
in those social connections.

01:52:32.220 --> 01:52:36.330
And that requires a viewing
of each other's faces,

01:52:36.330 --> 01:52:39.420
ideally, which is not to say
that a phone call or text

01:52:39.420 --> 01:52:41.140
exchange can't be meaningful.

01:52:41.140 --> 01:52:44.070
But that faces are really
the most powerful way

01:52:44.070 --> 01:52:45.990
to engage in social contact.

01:52:45.990 --> 01:52:49.560
And that eye contact, not
consistent eye contact,

01:52:49.560 --> 01:52:52.080
but eye contact of the sort
that builds up and then breaks

01:52:52.080 --> 01:52:54.090
and builds up and breaks
across the interaction

01:52:54.090 --> 01:52:59.460
is going to be the best way
that we are aware of to feel

01:52:59.460 --> 01:53:01.410
that one had a real connection.

01:53:01.410 --> 01:53:02.910
This should also
remove any pressure

01:53:02.910 --> 01:53:05.580
that you might feel to
constantly look at somebody

01:53:05.580 --> 01:53:08.095
or to be completely eyes
open staring at them

01:53:08.095 --> 01:53:10.470
without blinking or diverting
your attention at any point

01:53:10.470 --> 01:53:11.850
during a conversation.

01:53:11.850 --> 01:53:14.070
This also frankly,
is an opportunity

01:53:14.070 --> 01:53:17.610
where if somebody says, hey,
you're not paying attention

01:53:17.610 --> 01:53:20.670
because you look away,
that you may actually

01:53:20.670 --> 01:53:22.860
be engaging in what is
the more typical form

01:53:22.860 --> 01:53:23.910
of healthy connection.

01:53:23.910 --> 01:53:26.693
I talked about this long ago
on an episode about focus.

01:53:26.693 --> 01:53:28.110
It turns out when
we are listening

01:53:28.110 --> 01:53:29.872
very intently to
somebody and trying

01:53:29.872 --> 01:53:31.830
to remember the information
they're telling us,

01:53:31.830 --> 01:53:33.332
we will often close our eyes.

01:53:33.332 --> 01:53:35.790
And that's not a form of lack
of attention, that's actually

01:53:35.790 --> 01:53:37.050
a form of attending in.

01:53:37.050 --> 01:53:40.410
Because we have so much of
our brain devoted to vision.

01:53:40.410 --> 01:53:42.870
When 40% of our brain
is devoted to vision

01:53:42.870 --> 01:53:44.890
in some way or another,
when we close our eyes,

01:53:44.890 --> 01:53:47.280
we can actually devote
more attentional resources

01:53:47.280 --> 01:53:50.250
to remembering the specifics
of what people are telling us.

01:53:50.250 --> 01:53:52.620
But again, please don't
go through conversations

01:53:52.620 --> 01:53:54.310
with your eyes closed
the entire time.

01:53:54.310 --> 01:53:57.390
I think that would certainly
not be conducive to building

01:53:57.390 --> 01:53:58.330
social connection.

01:53:58.330 --> 01:54:01.710
So we know that faces are
important for social connection

01:54:01.710 --> 01:54:04.290
as it relates to
synthetic happiness.

01:54:04.290 --> 01:54:07.320
And we know that eye contact is
really important for building

01:54:07.320 --> 01:54:09.670
social connection.

01:54:09.670 --> 01:54:12.520
Physical contact
is also important

01:54:12.520 --> 01:54:13.540
for social connection.

01:54:13.540 --> 01:54:17.000
And not just romantic or
sexual type connection.

01:54:17.000 --> 01:54:19.480
In fact, there is a form
of physical connection

01:54:19.480 --> 01:54:22.840
that is present
in other primates.

01:54:22.840 --> 01:54:25.960
In fact, it's present as far
as we know, in all mammals.

01:54:25.960 --> 01:54:31.060
And is also very much a feature
of the human nervous system.

01:54:31.060 --> 01:54:32.868
And that's something
called allogrooming.

01:54:32.868 --> 01:54:34.660
I have to imagine that
most people probably

01:54:34.660 --> 01:54:36.077
haven't heard of allogrooming.

01:54:36.077 --> 01:54:37.660
The reason I'm
bringing up alogrooming

01:54:37.660 --> 01:54:41.080
is that it stems from a
fairly extensive literature

01:54:41.080 --> 01:54:45.310
about the prosocial,
pro happiness

01:54:45.310 --> 01:54:47.783
effects of pets on humans.

01:54:47.783 --> 01:54:49.450
In fact, if you want
to read up on this,

01:54:49.450 --> 01:54:52.810
there is a paper out of Yale
University on this topic

01:54:52.810 --> 01:54:53.980
that was published in 2018.

01:54:53.980 --> 01:54:56.313
The title of the paper is
"The influence of interactions

01:54:56.313 --> 01:55:00.368
with dogs on affect", OK
emotion, "anxety and arousal

01:55:00.368 --> 01:55:00.910
in children".

01:55:00.910 --> 01:55:02.570
And it references
some other studies

01:55:02.570 --> 01:55:03.820
that were performed on humans.

01:55:03.820 --> 01:55:07.270
And the basic takeaway is
that these so-called AAAs,

01:55:07.270 --> 01:55:12.010
animal assisted activities,
represent a really potent way

01:55:12.010 --> 01:55:15.430
to increase people,
including children's feelings

01:55:15.430 --> 01:55:16.210
of well-being.

01:55:16.210 --> 01:55:18.580
Now what's interesting
about this to me

01:55:18.580 --> 01:55:22.000
is that dogs
themselves don't really

01:55:22.000 --> 01:55:25.990
have to do much except be
present in the room in order

01:55:25.990 --> 01:55:29.860
for these positive effects, that
is, the reductions in anxiety,

01:55:29.860 --> 01:55:32.200
increases in happiness,
et cetera, to occur.

01:55:32.200 --> 01:55:34.317
And in fact, they can
be very, very brief.

01:55:34.317 --> 01:55:36.400
As they describe in the
paper, "brief unstructured

01:55:36.400 --> 01:55:38.140
interactions with
an unfamiliar dog",

01:55:38.140 --> 01:55:40.030
so you don't even
need to know this dog,

01:55:40.030 --> 01:55:43.300
"after exposure to a moderate
stressor showed higher positive

01:55:43.300 --> 01:55:46.690
affect relevant to participants
who received a soothing object

01:55:46.690 --> 01:55:48.530
or waited for the
same amount of time."

01:55:48.530 --> 01:55:52.240
So just even seeing a dog
for a brief amount of time

01:55:52.240 --> 01:55:56.260
has been shown to reduce
stress and improve happiness,

01:55:56.260 --> 01:55:57.910
or I should say,
increase feelings

01:55:57.910 --> 01:56:01.660
of happiness
overall then a child

01:56:01.660 --> 01:56:03.293
receiving a soothing object.

01:56:03.293 --> 01:56:05.710
Which was, at least for me, a
little bit counterintuitive.

01:56:05.710 --> 01:56:07.030
I would have thought
that children receiving

01:56:07.030 --> 01:56:08.405
a soothing object
would have been

01:56:08.405 --> 01:56:10.760
the more powerful stimulus.

01:56:10.760 --> 01:56:13.198
But in fact, it wasn't, at
least not in this study.

01:56:13.198 --> 01:56:15.490
The real question I think we
should be asking ourselves

01:56:15.490 --> 01:56:18.520
is, what is it about
interactions with others

01:56:18.520 --> 01:56:21.220
and with other animals
that could potentially

01:56:21.220 --> 01:56:24.950
have this prosocial
happiness enhancing effect?

01:56:24.950 --> 01:56:27.100
And the reason I
raise this is also

01:56:27.100 --> 01:56:31.660
because I think many people
are interested in either owning

01:56:31.660 --> 01:56:33.992
or having interactions with
pets as a way to improve

01:56:33.992 --> 01:56:35.200
their feelings of well-being.

01:56:35.200 --> 01:56:38.350
And I say having
interactions with because I

01:56:38.350 --> 01:56:40.840
myself am a good example
of somebody who wasn't

01:56:40.840 --> 01:56:43.183
always able to have pets.

01:56:43.183 --> 01:56:45.100
So when I was a graduate
student in a postdoc,

01:56:45.100 --> 01:56:46.672
I very much wanted a dog.

01:56:46.672 --> 01:56:48.130
Very, very, very
much wanted a dog.

01:56:48.130 --> 01:56:50.297
In fact, there was a rule
in my family at some point

01:56:50.297 --> 01:56:53.172
that I wasn't allowed to talk
about dogs anymore because I

01:56:53.172 --> 01:56:54.880
was talking about all
the breeds of dogs.

01:56:54.880 --> 01:56:58.270
Going to dog breeders,
examining different breeds,

01:56:58.270 --> 01:57:00.310
going to the pound, et cetera.

01:57:00.310 --> 01:57:03.430
The point was that I was
obsessed with getting a dog,

01:57:03.430 --> 01:57:05.800
but I knew I wasn't in a good
position to own a dog yet.

01:57:05.800 --> 01:57:08.300
I didn't have the finances, I
didn't have the correct living

01:57:08.300 --> 01:57:10.270
situation and so on.

01:57:10.270 --> 01:57:12.335
Eventually, I did
own a dog, of course.

01:57:12.335 --> 01:57:13.460
But at the time I couldn't.

01:57:13.460 --> 01:57:15.952
So what I would do is every
Sunday I would go to a place

01:57:15.952 --> 01:57:18.160
where they fostered dogs
and they needed dog walkers.

01:57:18.160 --> 01:57:19.285
And I would walk their dog.

01:57:19.285 --> 01:57:22.330
I would also walk
my neighbor's dogs.

01:57:22.330 --> 01:57:23.540
I didn't charge them for it.

01:57:23.540 --> 01:57:26.140
In fact, I felt like I was
being paid by getting time

01:57:26.140 --> 01:57:27.040
with those dogs.

01:57:27.040 --> 01:57:29.500
And in fact, I put an ad
at that time on Craigslist

01:57:29.500 --> 01:57:31.570
that I would walk
people's dogs for free,

01:57:31.570 --> 01:57:33.490
and only a few people
took that seriously.

01:57:33.490 --> 01:57:36.400
But of the ones that did,
I had a great little cadre

01:57:36.400 --> 01:57:38.920
of dog owners that would allow
me to take their dogs out

01:57:38.920 --> 01:57:40.030
and I was super happy.

01:57:40.030 --> 01:57:41.930
It just made me
very, very happy.

01:57:41.930 --> 01:57:44.170
And I really enjoyed it.

01:57:44.170 --> 01:57:47.230
And frankly, it was a
great opportunity for me

01:57:47.230 --> 01:57:50.080
to also get to the various dog
breeds and the different dog

01:57:50.080 --> 01:57:52.990
temperaments and to learn a bit
about my ability to interact

01:57:52.990 --> 01:57:54.613
with dogs in a certain way.

01:57:54.613 --> 01:57:56.530
I actually got to be a
pretty good dog walker.

01:57:56.530 --> 01:57:57.910
Unfortunately, later
I got a bulldog.

01:57:57.910 --> 01:57:58.960
And it turns out
no matter how good

01:57:58.960 --> 01:58:01.940
a dog walker you are, Bulldogs
just simply don't like to walk.

01:58:01.940 --> 01:58:04.000
In fact, if you've ever
walked up to a bulldog

01:58:04.000 --> 01:58:06.550
and you've offered to
scratch or pet that dog,

01:58:06.550 --> 01:58:08.600
you'll notice that
Bulldogs love that.

01:58:08.600 --> 01:58:11.800
And I would argue, having
been a bulldog owner,

01:58:11.800 --> 01:58:14.410
that they like it because it's
an opportunity for them to stop

01:58:14.410 --> 01:58:14.988
moving.

01:58:14.988 --> 01:58:16.780
But that's more about
the bulldog than what

01:58:16.780 --> 01:58:18.155
I'm about to tell
you next, which

01:58:18.155 --> 01:58:21.190
is this principle of
so-called allogrooming.

01:58:21.190 --> 01:58:23.140
Allogrooming is a
pattern of behavior

01:58:23.140 --> 01:58:26.980
that's observed in essentially
all mammals but very strongly

01:58:26.980 --> 01:58:29.980
in nonhuman primates
and primates,

01:58:29.980 --> 01:58:36.260
where individuals within a
species touch one another.

01:58:36.260 --> 01:58:37.930
And this is non-sexual touch.

01:58:37.930 --> 01:58:42.250
So this would be someone
brushing somebody else's hair

01:58:42.250 --> 01:58:45.680
or combing their hair or even
using a lint roller on them,

01:58:45.680 --> 01:58:46.580
for instance.

01:58:46.580 --> 01:58:49.660
Or someone grooming
somebody else.

01:58:49.660 --> 01:58:52.330
Now typically, one needs
to have an established

01:58:52.330 --> 01:58:53.660
relationship with this person.

01:58:53.660 --> 01:58:55.420
So it could be a professional
type relationship

01:58:55.420 --> 01:58:57.128
where this is a barber
cutting somebody's

01:58:57.128 --> 01:59:01.612
hair or a hairdresser cutting
or styling somebody's hair.

01:59:01.612 --> 01:59:03.070
It could be somebody
giving someone

01:59:03.070 --> 01:59:04.510
a manicure or a pedicure.

01:59:04.510 --> 01:59:06.940
Could be somebody doing skin
care or massage for somebody

01:59:06.940 --> 01:59:08.440
in a professional context.

01:59:08.440 --> 01:59:10.810
Or it could be two
people who have agreed

01:59:10.810 --> 01:59:13.205
that it is appropriate
for the context

01:59:13.205 --> 01:59:14.830
and for the relationship
for one person

01:59:14.830 --> 01:59:17.110
to be grooming somebody else.

01:59:17.110 --> 01:59:19.600
Can even, believe it or not
there's literature on this,

01:59:19.600 --> 01:59:22.930
can even extend into
the realm of people

01:59:22.930 --> 01:59:25.210
sort of cleaning and
picking off other people.

01:59:25.210 --> 01:59:27.580
Now when we see
this in primates,

01:59:27.580 --> 01:59:31.820
it seems like a very cute and
sort of almost understandable

01:59:31.820 --> 01:59:32.320
behavior.

01:59:32.320 --> 01:59:33.850
We can see these
pictures online.

01:59:33.850 --> 01:59:36.100
If you look them up, you can
just look up allogrooming

01:59:36.100 --> 01:59:38.380
and you'll see vast
number of pictures

01:59:38.380 --> 01:59:40.660
of for instance, baboons
picking little things out

01:59:40.660 --> 01:59:42.520
of each other's hair.

01:59:42.520 --> 01:59:46.610
Or grooming and kind of
perusing one another,

01:59:46.610 --> 01:59:49.530
to find things,
presumably parasites

01:59:49.530 --> 01:59:51.280
or like little bits
of plants or something

01:59:51.280 --> 01:59:52.947
like that they want
to remove from them.

01:59:52.947 --> 01:59:55.870
Allogrooming is
known to stimulate

01:59:55.870 --> 01:59:59.510
a certain category of neurons
called the C tactile fibers.

01:59:59.510 --> 02:00:02.620
These are a particular category
of so-called sensory neurons

02:00:02.620 --> 02:00:03.890
that innervate our skin.

02:00:03.890 --> 02:00:06.880
So these are literally like
little endings of neurons,

02:00:06.880 --> 02:00:09.010
little wires that
end up in the skin,

02:00:09.010 --> 02:00:11.470
that when they are
touched lightly

02:00:11.470 --> 02:00:14.302
tend to create a
feeling of well-being

02:00:14.302 --> 02:00:15.760
in the person that's
being touched.

02:00:15.760 --> 02:00:17.710
Again, this is
consensual touch that's

02:00:17.710 --> 02:00:19.300
very context appropriate.

02:00:19.300 --> 02:00:22.360
But it's known to
increase levels

02:00:22.360 --> 02:00:26.210
of oxytocin, a kind of hormone
slash neurotransmitter.

02:00:26.210 --> 02:00:27.460
It's both, really.

02:00:27.460 --> 02:00:31.060
That is known to evoke
feelings of bond.

02:00:31.060 --> 02:00:34.240
Or of feeling bonded to
somebody or something.

02:00:34.240 --> 02:00:36.610
And for many people
we hear about oxytocin

02:00:36.610 --> 02:00:40.050
and we think about the bond
between parent and child,

02:00:40.050 --> 02:00:41.800
in particular mother
and infant where it's

02:00:41.800 --> 02:00:43.060
been most extensively studied.

02:00:43.060 --> 02:00:45.580
Or between two members
of a romantic couple.

02:00:45.580 --> 02:00:47.840
But if you look at the
literature on allogrooming,

02:00:47.840 --> 02:00:50.950
what you find is that when
humans groom one another,

02:00:50.950 --> 02:00:54.430
the increases in oxytocin
that are experienced

02:00:54.430 --> 02:01:00.430
are at least on par with and in
fact, more often, more dramatic

02:01:00.430 --> 02:01:02.920
in response to allogrooming
than in response

02:01:02.920 --> 02:01:04.700
to other forms of touch.

02:01:04.700 --> 02:01:07.690
So the point here
is that allogrooming

02:01:07.690 --> 02:01:10.240
is a prosocial
behavior that tends

02:01:10.240 --> 02:01:13.300
to associate with
and promote feelings

02:01:13.300 --> 02:01:15.280
of well-being and happiness.

02:01:15.280 --> 02:01:17.150
And this is not
a trivial effect.

02:01:17.150 --> 02:01:20.170
If you look at the brain imaging
data or other forms of data

02:01:20.170 --> 02:01:23.170
on this, allogrooming
is a very powerful form

02:01:23.170 --> 02:01:26.590
of bonding between individuals
that's completely nonverbal.

02:01:26.590 --> 02:01:28.833
In fact, most often it
doesn't involve eye contact.

02:01:28.833 --> 02:01:31.000
I suppose two people could
be looking at one another

02:01:31.000 --> 02:01:32.800
grooming one another,
but typically, this

02:01:32.800 --> 02:01:36.010
is done from the
side or from behind.

02:01:36.010 --> 02:01:38.830
Why did I bring up
the paper on pets?

02:01:38.830 --> 02:01:43.150
Well it turns out that
when humans stroke dogs,

02:01:43.150 --> 02:01:45.610
or brush their
dogs or stroke cats

02:01:45.610 --> 02:01:48.490
or brush their cats,
et cetera, that

02:01:48.490 --> 02:01:50.800
is a form of human to
animal allogrooming.

02:01:50.800 --> 02:01:55.670
And it's one in which
both the pet and the human

02:01:55.670 --> 02:01:58.790
receive huge
increases in oxytocin

02:01:58.790 --> 02:02:01.910
and other related neurochemicals
that make us feel bonded.

02:02:01.910 --> 02:02:04.490
I bring this up because
the Harvard longitudinal

02:02:04.490 --> 02:02:06.920
study on happiness,
and many, many others,

02:02:06.920 --> 02:02:09.110
if not hundreds of other
studies on happiness,

02:02:09.110 --> 02:02:12.163
point to the importance of
quality social connection.

02:02:12.163 --> 02:02:13.580
You hear this over
and over again.

02:02:13.580 --> 02:02:15.110
People on their
deathbeds don't say

02:02:15.110 --> 02:02:16.490
they wish they had worked more.

02:02:16.490 --> 02:02:19.070
People on their deathbeds
talk about the richness

02:02:19.070 --> 02:02:20.750
of social connections
or the wish

02:02:20.750 --> 02:02:23.270
that they had invested
more in social connections.

02:02:23.270 --> 02:02:25.610
I think a lot of people
think of social connections

02:02:25.610 --> 02:02:30.680
only in terms of travel with
or conversation with others.

02:02:30.680 --> 02:02:35.090
But much of what we perceive
as deep social connections

02:02:35.090 --> 02:02:38.060
also involves physical contact.

02:02:38.060 --> 02:02:40.520
And that's something
that's deeply rooted

02:02:40.520 --> 02:02:41.990
in our evolutionary biology.

02:02:41.990 --> 02:02:44.690
And it's present both in us
and in non-human primates.

02:02:44.690 --> 02:02:48.200
And it's clear
that we can engage

02:02:48.200 --> 02:02:52.490
in these kinds of pro-social,
non verbal, non eye contact

02:02:52.490 --> 02:02:56.150
type behaviors through things
like non-sexual tactile

02:02:56.150 --> 02:02:57.500
touch, a.k.a.

02:02:57.500 --> 02:02:58.220
allogrooming.

02:02:58.220 --> 02:03:00.720
So we've been talking about a
number of the different things

02:03:00.720 --> 02:03:03.530
that one can do in order to
increase levels of happiness.

02:03:03.530 --> 02:03:05.480
And certainly,
before we conclude

02:03:05.480 --> 02:03:08.270
today, I'm going to
touch back into not just

02:03:08.270 --> 02:03:10.740
synthetic happiness and the
various things we can do,

02:03:10.740 --> 02:03:13.640
such as prosocial
spending, allogrooming,

02:03:13.640 --> 02:03:16.250
social connection, et cetera.

02:03:16.250 --> 02:03:19.040
But also things
related to happiness

02:03:19.040 --> 02:03:24.650
that involve focus on vocation
and work and pursuit of goals.

02:03:24.650 --> 02:03:26.450
Because as I mentioned
at the beginning,

02:03:26.450 --> 02:03:29.348
those are also
critical to increasing

02:03:29.348 --> 02:03:30.890
our state of happiness,
and certainly

02:03:30.890 --> 02:03:32.690
our state of security
and the feeling

02:03:32.690 --> 02:03:34.790
that we can provide for
ourselves and perhaps

02:03:34.790 --> 02:03:35.940
for others, as well.

02:03:35.940 --> 02:03:38.010
So we will talk about that.

02:03:38.010 --> 02:03:40.370
But I think it's also
important to talk

02:03:40.370 --> 02:03:43.460
about this notion of
choice and choices,

02:03:43.460 --> 02:03:46.730
and whether or not having
a lot of freedom to choose

02:03:46.730 --> 02:03:50.900
or limited freedom in choosing
what we do and what we get,

02:03:50.900 --> 02:03:53.160
and what we are able
to pursue in life,

02:03:53.160 --> 02:03:56.420
how that relates to
both natural happiness

02:03:56.420 --> 02:03:58.130
and synthetic happiness.

02:03:58.130 --> 02:04:00.710
Dan Gilbert and
others have explored

02:04:00.710 --> 02:04:03.140
this issue of freedom
of choice and how

02:04:03.140 --> 02:04:04.980
it relates to happiness.

02:04:04.980 --> 02:04:07.520
And there I must
say, the findings

02:04:07.520 --> 02:04:10.700
are incredibly counterintuitive.

02:04:10.700 --> 02:04:15.210
But very, very well supported
by all of their data.

02:04:15.210 --> 02:04:18.720
I'm going to summarize a large
amount of those studies at once

02:04:18.720 --> 02:04:20.640
by saying the following.

02:04:20.640 --> 02:04:22.557
Dan's laboratory and
other laboratories

02:04:22.557 --> 02:04:24.390
have done experiments
where they give people

02:04:24.390 --> 02:04:26.080
a series of options.

02:04:26.080 --> 02:04:28.200
And one of the more
classic examples, they

02:04:28.200 --> 02:04:30.540
give people the opportunity
to rate a number

02:04:30.540 --> 02:04:32.490
of different
paintings or pictures

02:04:32.490 --> 02:04:36.427
in ascending or descending
order of preference.

02:04:36.427 --> 02:04:38.010
In other words,
they're deciding which

02:04:38.010 --> 02:04:40.680
ones they like most which
ones they like least.

02:04:40.680 --> 02:04:43.440
Then what's interesting
is the experimenter

02:04:43.440 --> 02:04:45.360
will vary the
extent to which they

02:04:45.360 --> 02:04:47.080
have to stick to that choice.

02:04:47.080 --> 02:04:48.810
So this could be
sticking to the choice

02:04:48.810 --> 02:04:51.150
by receiving that
painting to take home.

02:04:51.150 --> 02:04:53.790
Or in another
experiment, it was having

02:04:53.790 --> 02:04:55.380
to make a choice
between giving up

02:04:55.380 --> 02:04:58.800
one photograph that they,
the research subject took,

02:04:58.800 --> 02:05:00.480
or another photograph
that they took.

02:05:00.480 --> 02:05:02.310
One of the photographs
was going to go off

02:05:02.310 --> 02:05:05.250
to a publication, another one
they could keep for themselves.

02:05:05.250 --> 02:05:07.590
And the conditions
in that experiment

02:05:07.590 --> 02:05:10.930
were either that you
had to make the decision

02:05:10.930 --> 02:05:13.980
and it was final, that is,
you could keep one and rate

02:05:13.980 --> 02:05:16.470
your decision, or
you could keep one

02:05:16.470 --> 02:05:18.870
and then you had the
opportunity to swap out

02:05:18.870 --> 02:05:21.340
that picture for the other
one at some later time.

02:05:21.340 --> 02:05:23.460
In other words, these
experiments really

02:05:23.460 --> 02:05:25.920
weren't about rating pictures,
they were really about

02:05:25.920 --> 02:05:29.760
whether or not constraining
your choice, meaning

02:05:29.760 --> 02:05:31.500
forcing somebody
to make a choice

02:05:31.500 --> 02:05:34.830
and stick to that choice, led
to greater levels or lesser

02:05:34.830 --> 02:05:38.040
levels of happiness and
satisfaction with that choice.

02:05:38.040 --> 02:05:41.010
And what they find
consistently is

02:05:41.010 --> 02:05:45.840
that when people have an
ongoing set of choices

02:05:45.840 --> 02:05:48.418
it leads to reduced
levels of happiness.

02:05:48.418 --> 02:05:50.460
Now that might come as
surprising to many of you,

02:05:50.460 --> 02:05:52.410
but I want to be clear
about what this means.

02:05:52.410 --> 02:05:56.460
This is not to say that having a
lot of choices of what you like

02:05:56.460 --> 02:05:59.790
most leads to lesser happiness.

02:05:59.790 --> 02:06:02.850
And that having fewer
choices about things

02:06:02.850 --> 02:06:06.270
you do, or objects you
acquire, et cetera,

02:06:06.270 --> 02:06:08.910
leads to greater happiness.

02:06:08.910 --> 02:06:11.820
What this set of
experiments really points to

02:06:11.820 --> 02:06:15.510
is that when we make a
choice, if we are forced

02:06:15.510 --> 02:06:18.060
to stick to that
choice we tend to be

02:06:18.060 --> 02:06:21.060
far happier with that
choice than if we maintain

02:06:21.060 --> 02:06:23.710
the option to change our mind.

02:06:23.710 --> 02:06:26.430
The results of these experiments
are extremely informative,

02:06:26.430 --> 02:06:30.130
I believe, in terms of
understanding our real life

02:06:30.130 --> 02:06:30.630
happiness.

02:06:30.630 --> 02:06:33.240
That is, happiness
outside the laboratory.

02:06:33.240 --> 02:06:35.550
But I think they are
often misunderstood

02:06:35.550 --> 02:06:37.590
as meaning that if we
have a lot of choices

02:06:37.590 --> 02:06:40.260
we tend to be less happy than
if we have fewer choices.

02:06:40.260 --> 02:06:41.340
That is not the case.

02:06:41.340 --> 02:06:44.310
Having freedom of
choice is terrific.

02:06:44.310 --> 02:06:47.940
And actually, correlates with
elevated levels of happiness.

02:06:47.940 --> 02:06:50.910
But once we make
our choice, it's

02:06:50.910 --> 02:06:53.910
clearly the case that
killing all other choices

02:06:53.910 --> 02:06:55.740
or having all other
options killed

02:06:55.740 --> 02:06:59.010
for us increases our
satisfaction with the choice

02:06:59.010 --> 02:06:59.970
that we've made.

02:06:59.970 --> 02:07:03.960
Whereas, leaving doors open,
leaving options open greatly

02:07:03.960 --> 02:07:07.110
diminishes our sense
of satisfaction.

02:07:07.110 --> 02:07:10.570
This has been exported to any
number of different domains.

02:07:10.570 --> 02:07:14.430
So this has been exported to
the domain of making choices

02:07:14.430 --> 02:07:19.800
about what college to go to or
what partner to select in life.

02:07:19.800 --> 02:07:21.990
In every one of
those instances, we

02:07:21.990 --> 02:07:23.760
see that our happiness
with our choice

02:07:23.760 --> 02:07:25.680
is very much related
to that choice

02:07:25.680 --> 02:07:30.558
being either the only one or
one of very few other options.

02:07:30.558 --> 02:07:32.850
There are a number of different
ways to interpret this.

02:07:32.850 --> 02:07:34.920
Through the lens
of neuroscience we

02:07:34.920 --> 02:07:38.400
might say that the
prefrontal cortex,

02:07:38.400 --> 02:07:41.850
the area of the brain that's
involved in decision making

02:07:41.850 --> 02:07:44.700
and evaluating
different options,

02:07:44.700 --> 02:07:48.420
is an area of the brain
that's vital, frankly,

02:07:48.420 --> 02:07:51.240
to our evolution as human
beings and to our daily life

02:07:51.240 --> 02:07:52.915
and to our whole life.

02:07:52.915 --> 02:07:54.540
It is, of course,
the thing that allows

02:07:54.540 --> 02:07:57.780
us to evaluate different rule
sets, to change rule sets,

02:07:57.780 --> 02:08:00.780
to switch contexts and to
create meaning, et cetera.

02:08:00.780 --> 02:08:02.730
To interpret what's
good what's bad.

02:08:02.730 --> 02:08:04.990
But it's also a
fairly costly process,

02:08:04.990 --> 02:08:06.900
meaning it's very
metabolically demanding.

02:08:06.900 --> 02:08:09.300
And there's an entire
literature related

02:08:09.300 --> 02:08:10.830
to what's called ego depletion.

02:08:10.830 --> 02:08:12.990
This is certainly a topic
for a future podcast.

02:08:12.990 --> 02:08:15.510
But ego depletion essentially
says that if I have you

02:08:15.510 --> 02:08:21.220
attend very intensely to a
given task, for instance,

02:08:21.220 --> 02:08:23.580
asking you to count
backwards from 1,000 to 0

02:08:23.580 --> 02:08:25.470
in increments of 13.

02:08:25.470 --> 02:08:28.230
And then have you
switch about halfway

02:08:28.230 --> 02:08:30.510
through, that's hard
for a lot of people.

02:08:30.510 --> 02:08:32.580
If I have you do that,
then your ability

02:08:32.580 --> 02:08:37.680
to suppress impulsive behavior
and to do a hard cognitive

02:08:37.680 --> 02:08:40.410
or physical task immediately
after that is actually

02:08:40.410 --> 02:08:40.920
suppressed.

02:08:40.920 --> 02:08:42.090
This so-called ego depletion.

02:08:42.090 --> 02:08:43.882
It relates to a number
of different things,

02:08:43.882 --> 02:08:46.770
but it certainly
relates to engagement

02:08:46.770 --> 02:08:48.240
of the prefrontal
cortex, which is

02:08:48.240 --> 02:08:49.830
very metabolically demanding.

02:08:49.830 --> 02:08:53.910
So evaluating choices and
doing computation of numbers

02:08:53.910 --> 02:08:56.160
or attending to
things with your mind

02:08:56.160 --> 02:08:58.200
and forcing yourself
to focus intensely

02:08:58.200 --> 02:09:00.360
is metabolically demanding.

02:09:00.360 --> 02:09:02.280
And that's a limited
resource that

02:09:02.280 --> 02:09:05.010
can be reset by things like
sleep and non sleep deep rest

02:09:05.010 --> 02:09:06.900
or idle time or letting
your mind wander.

02:09:06.900 --> 02:09:08.730
In that case a
positive mind wandering

02:09:08.730 --> 02:09:11.920
to allow your brain to
reset its ability to focus.

02:09:11.920 --> 02:09:15.250
But the other thing
that it does is

02:09:15.250 --> 02:09:18.520
it impacts the reward
circuitry of the brain,

02:09:18.520 --> 02:09:20.980
the so-called dopamine reward
circuitry and other reward

02:09:20.980 --> 02:09:23.262
circuitry of the brain.

02:09:23.262 --> 02:09:24.970
And here I'm painting
with a broad brush.

02:09:24.970 --> 02:09:29.060
But it essentially divides
them such that for instance,

02:09:29.060 --> 02:09:32.260
if a given choice of
a let's say a partner

02:09:32.260 --> 02:09:34.900
or maybe buying ourselves
an article of clothing,

02:09:34.900 --> 02:09:37.535
not that I want to compare
selection of a life partner

02:09:37.535 --> 02:09:39.160
to selection of an
article of clothing.

02:09:39.160 --> 02:09:40.810
But just to give
multiple examples.

02:09:40.810 --> 02:09:44.980
Might give us, and here
it's arbitrary units, x

02:09:44.980 --> 02:09:47.950
units of dopamine increase.

02:09:47.950 --> 02:09:50.470
Well, if we buy that
article of clothing or we

02:09:50.470 --> 02:09:52.300
select that life
partner, and then

02:09:52.300 --> 02:09:55.240
we emerge from the
store or the wedding

02:09:55.240 --> 02:09:58.970
and we are focused on what
we purchased for ourselves,

02:09:58.970 --> 02:10:03.610
our choice, or our life partner
choice, and only that, well

02:10:03.610 --> 02:10:07.370
then there's a certain amount of
neurochemical reward associated

02:10:07.370 --> 02:10:07.870
with that.

02:10:07.870 --> 02:10:09.460
And happiness and well-being.

02:10:09.460 --> 02:10:13.970
But it's also very
clear that if we

02:10:13.970 --> 02:10:18.300
leave those choices, the store
or our wedding for instance,

02:10:18.300 --> 02:10:20.960
or a life with somebody for
a moment, even just mentally,

02:10:20.960 --> 02:10:23.150
and start thinking
about the other options

02:10:23.150 --> 02:10:25.670
that we might
entertain as possible.

02:10:25.670 --> 02:10:28.790
If those are still open to
us in reality or in our mind,

02:10:28.790 --> 02:10:32.630
well then our reward circuitry
becomes fractured in a way.

02:10:32.630 --> 02:10:35.420
Not physically fractured,
but less attention

02:10:35.420 --> 02:10:38.420
is devoted to the reward
circuitry associated

02:10:38.420 --> 02:10:40.100
with our choice.

02:10:40.100 --> 02:10:43.820
And as a consequence, instead
of it being x units of dopamine,

02:10:43.820 --> 02:10:48.320
it's x divided by however
many other choices

02:10:48.320 --> 02:10:51.500
we might have available to
us in our mind or in reality.

02:10:51.500 --> 02:10:53.840
So instead of, and again,
these are arbitrary units,

02:10:53.840 --> 02:10:55.905
but instead of a certain
amount of reward,

02:10:55.905 --> 02:10:57.530
it's a certain amount
of reward divided

02:10:57.530 --> 02:10:59.480
by the number of
other options that we

02:10:59.480 --> 02:11:03.990
might be considering as
alternatives to what we chose.

02:11:03.990 --> 02:11:06.950
And I think this is a
very important aspect

02:11:06.950 --> 02:11:11.510
of understanding how limiting
our choices after we've

02:11:11.510 --> 02:11:14.690
made them is a
vital part of what

02:11:14.690 --> 02:11:16.320
we call synthetic happiness.

02:11:16.320 --> 02:11:18.080
In fact, we could
even go so far as

02:11:18.080 --> 02:11:20.720
to say that focusing on
the choices we've made

02:11:20.720 --> 02:11:24.470
and really investing in
those choices as good ones,

02:11:24.470 --> 02:11:26.847
or great ones, and
really trying to limit

02:11:26.847 --> 02:11:28.430
our thinking to the
choices that we've

02:11:28.430 --> 02:11:31.490
made once we've
made them is perhaps

02:11:31.490 --> 02:11:34.340
also important to our
natural happiness.

02:11:34.340 --> 02:11:38.810
Because it's so inextricably
entwined with what

02:11:38.810 --> 02:11:41.070
we think of as a good life.

02:11:41.070 --> 02:11:44.150
And what I mean by that
is if we are constantly

02:11:44.150 --> 02:11:46.350
in a mode of evaluative
decision making,

02:11:46.350 --> 02:11:48.080
even after we've
made a decision,

02:11:48.080 --> 02:11:52.070
we are not neurochemically
nor psychologically able

02:11:52.070 --> 02:11:54.762
to extract the feelings
of happiness associated

02:11:54.762 --> 02:11:55.970
with the choice that we made.

02:11:55.970 --> 02:11:57.680
So we've talked about a
number of different dimensions

02:11:57.680 --> 02:12:00.230
of happiness, both in synthetic
and natural happiness.

02:12:00.230 --> 02:12:02.900
And some of the more
counterintuitive aspects

02:12:02.900 --> 02:12:04.490
of happiness.

02:12:04.490 --> 02:12:06.470
For instance, that
people tend to adjust

02:12:06.470 --> 02:12:11.620
their levels of happiness not
regardless but often in spite

02:12:11.620 --> 02:12:13.700
of their life circumstances.

02:12:13.700 --> 02:12:17.140
But as we emphasized earlier
in the episode, that is not

02:12:17.140 --> 02:12:20.020
to say, at least the
research does not directly

02:12:20.020 --> 02:12:23.710
support the idea that
a major trauma or loss

02:12:23.710 --> 02:12:25.340
won't impact our happiness.

02:12:25.340 --> 02:12:26.950
In fact, it tends to.

02:12:26.950 --> 02:12:29.320
And that's why it's
important that people access

02:12:29.320 --> 02:12:31.990
resources and work devoted
to overcoming trauma, which

02:12:31.990 --> 02:12:34.430
certainly exists out there.

02:12:34.430 --> 02:12:37.540
And of course, there are
the longitudinal studies

02:12:37.540 --> 02:12:41.650
in short term studies showing
that income level and material

02:12:41.650 --> 02:12:46.030
things don't necessarily scale
with happiness, and vise versa.

02:12:46.030 --> 02:12:49.240
And yet, we also acknowledged
early in the episode

02:12:49.240 --> 02:12:51.760
that while indeed money
can't buy happiness,

02:12:51.760 --> 02:12:53.110
it can buffer stress.

02:12:53.110 --> 02:12:56.470
And while work doesn't
necessarily bring happiness,

02:12:56.470 --> 02:13:00.190
per se, work can bring
a tremendous feeling

02:13:00.190 --> 02:13:03.400
of meaning and resources
which can then put you

02:13:03.400 --> 02:13:07.030
into context in which things
like prosocial contact

02:13:07.030 --> 02:13:12.160
and enhanced bonds and
caretaking of others and of you

02:13:12.160 --> 02:13:13.210
can be enhanced.

02:13:13.210 --> 02:13:16.390
So it would be unfair
and in fact, inaccurate,

02:13:16.390 --> 02:13:19.450
to simply view happiness
through the lens of money

02:13:19.450 --> 02:13:21.980
doesn't matter, it's all
about social connection.

02:13:21.980 --> 02:13:23.890
And so on and so forth.

02:13:23.890 --> 02:13:26.600
Absolutely, social
connection is important,

02:13:26.600 --> 02:13:29.093
which is why we spent some
minutes talking about some

02:13:29.093 --> 02:13:30.760
of the ways to enhance
social connection

02:13:30.760 --> 02:13:33.010
both with other human
beings and other animals,

02:13:33.010 --> 02:13:35.680
and them with us.

02:13:35.680 --> 02:13:39.280
I think there's a opportunity
here to take the research

02:13:39.280 --> 02:13:43.150
on happiness, the research
on the neuroscience of what

02:13:43.150 --> 02:13:47.440
happiness and gratitude and
prosocial connection tells us,

02:13:47.440 --> 02:13:50.870
and to combine it into a
bit of a model or a toolkit,

02:13:50.870 --> 02:13:51.470
if you will.

02:13:51.470 --> 02:13:53.860
And I think indeed, this
will be a toolkit in one

02:13:53.860 --> 02:13:56.650
of our future toolkit episodes,
likely merged with the toolkit

02:13:56.650 --> 02:13:59.450
on gratitude, which
we haven't done yet.

02:13:59.450 --> 02:14:04.390
And perhaps even we will do an
entire episode on social bonds

02:14:04.390 --> 02:14:06.790
and how to enhance or
build social bonds.

02:14:06.790 --> 02:14:10.413
Or at least what the
science tells us about that.

02:14:10.413 --> 02:14:11.830
If we take a step
back and we look

02:14:11.830 --> 02:14:15.250
at the concept of happiness,
we can make a couple

02:14:15.250 --> 02:14:17.050
of absolute statements.

02:14:17.050 --> 02:14:19.490
That is, statements that I
think very few people, if any,

02:14:19.490 --> 02:14:20.110
would contest.

02:14:20.110 --> 02:14:22.690
First of all, there's no
single molecule or chemical

02:14:22.690 --> 02:14:25.330
associated with happiness,
but that the chemical milieu

02:14:25.330 --> 02:14:29.650
of the brain and body
is important for setting

02:14:29.650 --> 02:14:33.470
the stage or the
opportunity for happiness.

02:14:33.470 --> 02:14:37.160
Hence, why there are treatments
aimed at alleviating depression

02:14:37.160 --> 02:14:40.280
or mania that target certain
neurochemical systems

02:14:40.280 --> 02:14:41.990
and hormone systems.

02:14:41.990 --> 02:14:45.380
Happiness, at least the
way I'm framing it today,

02:14:45.380 --> 02:14:46.830
has essentially two components.

02:14:46.830 --> 02:14:49.190
One is meaning.

02:14:49.190 --> 02:14:54.470
That is, what sort of meaning
do certain types of interactions

02:14:54.470 --> 02:14:58.310
or behaviors, could be work,
could be social interactions,

02:14:58.310 --> 02:15:01.280
et cetera, carry for us?

02:15:01.280 --> 02:15:04.035
And nested in that is this
concept of connection.

02:15:04.035 --> 02:15:06.410
And we talked a bit about
tools for enhancing connection.

02:15:06.410 --> 02:15:08.930
Things like eye contact, but
not constant eye contact.

02:15:08.930 --> 02:15:12.500
Things like being very
present to a conversation

02:15:12.500 --> 02:15:15.050
or an activity that
you're engaging in.

02:15:15.050 --> 02:15:16.850
Remember, we talked
about the paper,

02:15:16.850 --> 02:15:19.218
"A Distracted Mind
is an Unhappy Mind"?

02:15:19.218 --> 02:15:20.510
The paper published in Science.

02:15:20.510 --> 02:15:22.843
And we talked about the study,
also published in Science

02:15:22.843 --> 02:15:28.380
in which giving money, but
also knowing how that money has

02:15:28.380 --> 02:15:30.570
positively impacted
others, leads

02:15:30.570 --> 02:15:33.090
to this feeling of pro-social
connection and happiness

02:15:33.090 --> 02:15:35.430
in the giver and
in the receiver.

02:15:35.430 --> 02:15:37.680
And I should mention
again that it's not

02:15:37.680 --> 02:15:40.560
just the giving of money but
also the giving of effort

02:15:40.560 --> 02:15:44.170
and time and attention that
can have similar effects.

02:15:44.170 --> 02:15:45.840
So we have meaning
and connection,

02:15:45.840 --> 02:15:48.310
and a number of different
ways to access those.

02:15:48.310 --> 02:15:50.640
And then we have
this access that I'm

02:15:50.640 --> 02:15:52.990
referring to as
performance and resources.

02:15:52.990 --> 02:15:56.130
And I'm talking about
performance and resources

02:15:56.130 --> 02:15:59.708
as it relates to
natural happiness, not

02:15:59.708 --> 02:16:01.500
synthetic happiness,
but natural happiness.

02:16:01.500 --> 02:16:05.340
Because we would be
wrong, I believe,

02:16:05.340 --> 02:16:08.250
if we were to say that
income doesn't matter.

02:16:08.250 --> 02:16:11.190
I think it's fair to say,
based on the research,

02:16:11.190 --> 02:16:13.290
that income matters.

02:16:13.290 --> 02:16:16.620
And income that can cover
costs of living plus that

02:16:16.620 --> 02:16:18.702
includes some buffer.

02:16:18.702 --> 02:16:19.910
And what do I mean by buffer?

02:16:19.910 --> 02:16:23.870
I mean buffer to the anxiety
that circumstances might change

02:16:23.870 --> 02:16:24.950
is important.

02:16:24.950 --> 02:16:28.520
Now that's going to vary
from person to person.

02:16:28.520 --> 02:16:31.190
Meaning some people
will be perfectly happy

02:16:31.190 --> 02:16:37.610
making $1 more than their
absolute cost of living

02:16:37.610 --> 02:16:38.900
every month.

02:16:38.900 --> 02:16:41.809
Other people will require
a more substantial buffer

02:16:41.809 --> 02:16:45.889
in order to protect them against
the negative psychological

02:16:45.889 --> 02:16:48.448
effects of worrying about
for instance, inflation.

02:16:48.448 --> 02:16:50.240
Or worrying that they
might lose their job.

02:16:50.240 --> 02:16:52.459
And this is why I think
most people recommend

02:16:52.459 --> 02:16:56.209
having, if possible, some
buffer in their bank account

02:16:56.209 --> 02:16:59.920
that could cover two or
three or maybe even six

02:16:59.920 --> 02:17:02.650
or maybe even 12 months
of living expenses

02:17:02.650 --> 02:17:04.389
were they to lose
their job or something

02:17:04.389 --> 02:17:06.320
catastrophic happened to them.

02:17:06.320 --> 02:17:08.950
So if we're going to
talk about happiness,

02:17:08.950 --> 02:17:12.309
I think it's only fair, only
accurate, and frankly, only

02:17:12.309 --> 02:17:17.440
respectful to talk about
living requirements and cost

02:17:17.440 --> 02:17:18.459
of living requirements.

02:17:18.459 --> 02:17:20.350
That includes this
sort of buffer.

02:17:20.350 --> 02:17:22.180
And that buffer to
anxiety is going

02:17:22.180 --> 02:17:25.030
to vary depending on how
anxious somebody gets

02:17:25.030 --> 02:17:27.309
about the possibility
of catastrophic things

02:17:27.309 --> 02:17:29.559
happening to them, like
losing their job or their rent

02:17:29.559 --> 02:17:30.570
going up or doubling.

02:17:30.570 --> 02:17:32.320
And here, I'm talking
about hypotheticals.

02:17:32.320 --> 02:17:34.900
But I think we all know
people and perhaps ourselves

02:17:34.900 --> 02:17:37.459
have experienced those
kinds of circumstances.

02:17:37.459 --> 02:17:39.760
So when we talk about
happiness we absolutely

02:17:39.760 --> 02:17:41.230
need to think about resources.

02:17:41.230 --> 02:17:43.209
And we also need to
think about performance.

02:17:43.209 --> 02:17:46.780
I think we would be completely
inaccurate if we simply

02:17:46.780 --> 02:17:51.219
said, oh, any work
leading to any outcomes.

02:17:51.219 --> 02:17:53.379
Any effort, regardless
of whether or not

02:17:53.379 --> 02:17:56.020
it gets you an A in
school or an F in school

02:17:56.020 --> 02:17:58.420
isn't going to impact
your happiness.

02:17:58.420 --> 02:18:00.709
I don't think anyone
would agree with that.

02:18:00.709 --> 02:18:02.950
And yet if you look at
the major takeaways,

02:18:02.950 --> 02:18:06.459
at least as they are
communicated typically

02:18:06.459 --> 02:18:10.809
in the public sphere around
the longitudinal and short term

02:18:10.809 --> 02:18:14.020
studies of happiness,
the takeaway generally

02:18:14.020 --> 02:18:17.530
is more focused on
social connection

02:18:17.530 --> 02:18:19.299
and how money is not important.

02:18:19.299 --> 02:18:22.420
I don't think anyone that's
saying that actually means

02:18:22.420 --> 02:18:26.860
that income that can cover
your expenses plus some buffer

02:18:26.860 --> 02:18:27.580
isn't important.

02:18:27.580 --> 02:18:29.270
But it's often not stated.

02:18:29.270 --> 02:18:32.020
So if we were to come up with a
general model of happiness that

02:18:32.020 --> 02:18:34.570
includes various tools
for how to increase

02:18:34.570 --> 02:18:36.790
our levels of happiness,
I think it's only

02:18:36.790 --> 02:18:40.389
fair to include both natural
and synthetic forms of happiness

02:18:40.389 --> 02:18:43.900
and to pursue both natural
and synthetic happiness.

02:18:43.900 --> 02:18:46.059
Just to remind you,
natural happiness

02:18:46.059 --> 02:18:48.370
is the kind of happiness
that we associate

02:18:48.370 --> 02:18:50.980
with obtaining something,
either by effort

02:18:50.980 --> 02:18:52.330
or because it was given to us.

02:18:52.330 --> 02:18:54.850
Although, I definitely want
to highlight the fact that

02:18:54.850 --> 02:18:59.889
receiving things that don't
require much reward in order

02:18:59.889 --> 02:19:02.440
to receive them over
time can be detrimental

02:19:02.440 --> 02:19:03.639
to our dopamine system.

02:19:03.639 --> 02:19:04.959
That's an important aside.

02:19:04.959 --> 02:19:08.290
The other form of happiness
is the form of happiness

02:19:08.290 --> 02:19:10.030
that we call
synthetic happiness.

02:19:10.030 --> 02:19:13.570
Which is, for instance,
focusing on social connection.

02:19:13.570 --> 02:19:16.270
And we talked about ways
to do that as a means

02:19:16.270 --> 02:19:17.770
to enhance your happiness.

02:19:17.770 --> 02:19:20.998
Again, the language, the
name synthetic happiness

02:19:20.998 --> 02:19:22.540
implies something
kind of artificial.

02:19:22.540 --> 02:19:25.942
But frankly, genuine social
connection is genuine.

02:19:25.942 --> 02:19:27.400
There's nothing
artificial about it

02:19:27.400 --> 02:19:29.740
or synthetic about it, is
that you can synthesize it

02:19:29.740 --> 02:19:31.990
through action, through
deliberate action.

02:19:31.990 --> 02:19:36.400
Likewise, being focused
or encouraging yourself.

02:19:36.400 --> 02:19:39.129
Working on being focused on
whatever activities you happen

02:19:39.129 --> 02:19:41.260
to be engaged in,
positive or negative,

02:19:41.260 --> 02:19:43.629
is known to increase
your levels of happiness.

02:19:43.629 --> 02:19:46.240
Again, this is a form
of synthetic happiness.

02:19:46.240 --> 02:19:49.480
You're not obtaining
anything new or additional

02:19:49.480 --> 02:19:50.710
as a consequence of this.

02:19:50.710 --> 02:19:52.930
It's entirely internal.

02:19:52.930 --> 02:19:54.400
There's no external reward.

02:19:54.400 --> 02:19:56.260
There isn't more
money that arrives

02:19:56.260 --> 02:19:57.820
with this or a better grade.

02:19:57.820 --> 02:19:59.410
Although, I would
make the argument

02:19:59.410 --> 02:20:01.360
that if you are
present to the work

02:20:01.360 --> 02:20:05.140
you're doing in any context,
physical or mental work,

02:20:05.140 --> 02:20:06.970
it's very likely
that you are going

02:20:06.970 --> 02:20:08.710
to perform better at that work.

02:20:08.710 --> 02:20:11.620
So we have natural happiness
and synthetic happiness.

02:20:11.620 --> 02:20:15.100
And both of them require
our attention and effort.

02:20:15.100 --> 02:20:18.490
And in fact, if we
were to draw a link

02:20:18.490 --> 02:20:20.740
between natural and
synthetic happiness,

02:20:20.740 --> 02:20:22.810
it really is this
concept of presence,

02:20:22.810 --> 02:20:24.910
of really being focused
on what we're doing,

02:20:24.910 --> 02:20:28.600
that's most likely to lead
to the outcomes that we want.

02:20:28.600 --> 02:20:31.840
Both externally, in terms of
receiving monetary rewards

02:20:31.840 --> 02:20:33.662
or grades, or
praise, or whatever

02:20:33.662 --> 02:20:35.620
it is that you happen to
be pursuing out there,

02:20:35.620 --> 02:20:37.180
resources of some kind.

02:20:37.180 --> 02:20:41.020
And presence, and
striving to be present

02:20:41.020 --> 02:20:44.680
when in the pursuit of so-called
synthetic happiness in the form

02:20:44.680 --> 02:20:48.492
of social connection or in
the form of really focusing

02:20:48.492 --> 02:20:50.200
on the choice that
you've made and making

02:20:50.200 --> 02:20:52.300
the best of that choice,
especially since you

02:20:52.300 --> 02:20:56.530
made that choice in a way that
you deemed best at the time.

02:20:56.530 --> 02:21:00.400
Well, that also is known to
increase your overall levels

02:21:00.400 --> 02:21:01.280
of happiness.

02:21:01.280 --> 02:21:04.300
So if an ability to focus
and attend to things deeply

02:21:04.300 --> 02:21:08.290
is really what's most important,
and really acts as the greatest

02:21:08.290 --> 02:21:10.750
lever for both natural
and synthetic happiness,

02:21:10.750 --> 02:21:14.860
well then, tools like a 5 minute
daily meditation or a 13 minute

02:21:14.860 --> 02:21:18.070
day meditation, as
well as tools that

02:21:18.070 --> 02:21:20.980
allow us to get excellent
sleep every night.

02:21:20.980 --> 02:21:24.247
Which, of course, sets the basis
for attention during the day.

02:21:24.247 --> 02:21:26.080
If you've ever had a
poor night's sleep then

02:21:26.080 --> 02:21:28.480
you are very familiar
with how hard it

02:21:28.480 --> 02:21:31.090
is to focus the following day.

02:21:31.090 --> 02:21:33.110
At least for long
periods of time.

02:21:33.110 --> 02:21:35.500
But building our
capacity to focus

02:21:35.500 --> 02:21:38.140
through a focusing exercise,
which again, is often

02:21:38.140 --> 02:21:40.390
called meditation, but
is really simply just

02:21:40.390 --> 02:21:43.150
a focusing and
perceptual exercise.

02:21:43.150 --> 02:21:45.370
That's going to create
an outsized effect

02:21:45.370 --> 02:21:47.920
on all the aspects,
all the behaviors

02:21:47.920 --> 02:21:50.590
that we know feed into
creating natural and synthetic

02:21:50.590 --> 02:21:51.430
happiness.

02:21:51.430 --> 02:21:53.800
And so it's really fair
to say that our ability

02:21:53.800 --> 02:21:57.780
to attend and focus really
equates to happiness.

